        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1196\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal addresses a real compliance hook in Article 12(3)(b), but it proposes building a bespoke cryptographic content-addressable storage system when the regulatory requirement is essentially to record which reference-database version was used per inference call—something our existing data-versioning and experiment-tracking infrastructure can extend with modest configuration. The framing around 'absolute reproducibility' and 'intellectual merit' signals ambition well beyond what the Act demands, and committing scarce engineering resources to a greenfield storage architecture (rather than bolting hash-pinned version identifiers onto systems we already operate) is a poor allocation against other compliance and research priorities in our portfolio.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 42\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is \"P1196: Verifiable Dataset Lineage Protocol\" - a content-addressable storage system using cryptographic hashes to link model outputs to data snapshots for Article 12(3)(b) compliance.\n\nLet me assess this from my specific vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- The proposal describes building a \"content-addressable storage system using cryptographic hashes\" - this is essentially building or adopting infrastructure like IPFS, Git-like content addressing, or similar systems. This is a significant engineering undertaking.\n- Article 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This is a compliance requirement for Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically (biometric identification). The proposal's scope seems reasonable in that it targets exactly this requirement.\n- However, the proposal frames this as creating a new system from scratch (\"We will implement a content-addressable storage system\"). Existing R&D infrastructure likely already has version control, data management systems, and audit trails. Building a custom cryptographic hash-based system may be overkill when existing tools (versioned databases, hash-chained logs, existing DMS) could satisfy the requirement more efficiently.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The language \"ensures absolute reproducibility and intellectual merit by eliminating ambiguity in data attribution\" suggests this is framed partly as a scientific/academic aspiration rather than purely a compliance necessity. Article 12(3)(b) requires recording which reference database was used - it doesn't mandate cryptographic content-addressable storage specifically. A simple version identifier + timestamp could satisfy the letter of the law.\n- The \"absolute reproducibility\" and \"intellectual merit\" framing suggests scope creep beyond what the regulation strictly demands. From a funding perspective, building a full cryptographic provenance system when a simpler audit trail would suffice is a misallocation of scarce resources.\n- However, if we're going to build something for compliance anyway, investing in a more robust system that also serves broader reproducibility goals could be defensible IF the incremental cost over a simple solution is manageable.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Most R&D labs already have data versioning (DVC, LakeFS, similar), experiment tracking (MLflow, W&B), and some form of audit logging. The proposal seems to assume we need to build new infrastructure rather than extend what exists.\n- A content-addressable storage system is a specific architectural choice. If our existing infrastructure already supports versioned data with checksums (which most modern data platforms do), this adds significant complexity for marginal compliance benefit.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal addresses a real requirement (Article 12(3)(b)) but over-engineers the solution. From a resource-allocation perspective, building a custom cryptographic content-addressable storage system when existing versioning infrastructure could be extended is inefficient. The \"absolute reproducibility\" and \"intellectual merit\" framing signals scientific ambition that may not justify the engineering cost for what is fundamentally an audit-trail requirement. However, it's not a bad idea per se - if implemented as an extension of existing systems rather than a greenfield build, it could be valuable.\n\nThe key concern from my vantage: this reads like a proposal to BUILD new infrastructure (content-addressable storage with cryptographic hashes) rather than leverage what we already have. The incremental compliance requirement is modest (record which reference DB version was used), and the proposed solution is architecturally heavy. I'd want to see it scoped as an extension of existing data management systems, not a standalone new system.\n\nI'll lean toward reject because:\n1. It proposes building new infrastructure rather than extending existing\n2. The compliance requirement (record which reference DB was used) doesn't strictly require cryptographic content-addressable storage\n3. The framing emphasizes \"intellectual merit\" and \"absolute reproducibility\" which suggests ambition beyond what the regulation demands, potentially consuming resources that could be better allocated elsewhere\n4. From a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, there are simpler ways to satisfy 12(3)(b)\n\nBut it's not terrible - maybe a 40-50 promise score. It addresses a real need but over-engineers the solution and doesn't leverage existing infrastructure well enough from my perspective.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise of around 42. It's addressing the right regulatory hook but proposing disproportionate infrastructure investment relative to what the requirement actually demands, and it doesn't demonstrate effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n",
