der the EU AI Act, post-market monitoring (Article 72) requires providers to actively monitor their systems. Having a tool that flags anomalies proactively strengthens our position in demonstrating due diligence.\n- Tracking human verification events for Annex III specifically addresses Article 14(5) requirements — this is a very targeted compliance gap-filler.\n- The risk matrix approach is good because it provides consistency and defensibility in regulatory audits.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This directly reduces regulatory breach risk by catching issues before they escalate\n- It demonstrates to regulators that the institution takes post-market monitoring seriously beyond mere log storage\n- The Annex III human verification tracking is specifically called out in the regulation and having it automated shows thoroughness\n- Market reputation: proactive compliance posture is a competitive differentiator\n\nPotential concerns:\n- \"Pre-defined risk matrix\" — who defines it? If it's too narrow, it might miss novel risks. But this is an implementation detail, not a reason to reject.\n- It's described as a \"dashboard\" — is that sufficient for regulatory audit trails? A dashboard implies visualization, but the underlying data must be tamper-evident and retained per Article 12. However, the proposal says it \"parses unified logs\" which suggests the raw log retention is separate; this tool is an analytical layer on top.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a solid compliance-supporting tool that directly addresses multiple sub-requirements of Article 12 and strengthens our post-market monitoring posture. It's not the foundational logging infrastructure itself, but it's the \"use\" layer that makes logs actually serve their regulatory purpose. From a survival/compliance perspective, this is valuable. I'd approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely useful for compliance operations. It addresses specific r