hes, identification of verification persons. An MLflow-based pipeline for training metadata doesn't inherently capture these runtime operational logs.\n- The proposal says it \"replaces manual reporting\" — but compliance documentation often requires human review and sign-off. Pure automation without human oversight could create gaps where edge cases or interpretive judgments need to be documented.\n- \"Live data\" is good for up-to-dateness, but there's a question about versioning and audit trails of the documentation itself — if the docs auto-update, how do you maintain an auditable history? Regulators may want to see what the documentation looked like at specific points in time.\n- The proposal doesn't address data retention requirements or who has access to these records.\n- Market reputation: showing regulators a robust automated system could be a positive signal of maturity and commitment to compliance, but only if it actually covers all required elements.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid partial solution that addresses the \"technical documentation\" aspect (Recital 71's reference to technical documentation being kept up to date) and supports the spirit of automatic recording. However, as framed, it conflates training/development metadata with operational runtime logging. It's useful but incomplete for full Article 12 compliance. That said, from a compliance perspective, having an automated pipeline that captures training data lineage is genuinely valuable — it reduces the risk of documentation gaps and supports the \"up to date\" requirement. The fact that it doesn't cover runtime logging is a gap, but it's not disqualifying; it's a complementary piece.\n\nFrom my vantage point: I would see this as a useful building block for compliance infrastructure. It addresses part of Article 12 and Recital 71 requirements well (technical documentation up-to-dateness, capture of algorithms/data/training/testing/validation info). The main risk is that leadership might mistake this for full Article 12 compliance when it's really only the development-side documentation piece. But as a standalone proposal to commit resources to, it does reduce real compliance risk around documentation currency and completeness during the development phase.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-good promise score. It's genuinely useful for compliance but is incomplete (doesn't cover runtime logging per Art 12(1)-(3) specifics). From my vantage point, I'd want to ensure it's scoped correctly as one component of a broader record-keeping program, not the whole thing. But on its own merits, it reduces documentation drift risk and supports the \"up to date\" requirement.\n\nI'll give it around 72 — solid useful piece that addresses real compliance pain points (documentation currency, consistency), but doesn't cover the full scope of Article 12 and has some gaps in addressing audit trail/versioning of the docs themselves.\n",
