rograms from disruption.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The proposal specifically tests whether our recording mechanisms \"actually function as intended.\" This is about verifying existing infrastructure works. It doesn't require building new systems — it requires exercising and validating what's already there. From an infrastructure efficiency standpoint, this is good: you want to know your logging infrastructure is operational before a crisis reveals it isn't.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Who runs this? \"The compliance team\" — if that means pulling scientists or engineers off research projects periodically, that's a real opportunity cost. But it says \"compliance team,\" suggesting dedicated staff.\n- Frequency and scope need to be bounded to avoid becoming an open-ended resource drain.\n- The proposal is reasonably scoped: \"periodic\" and \"random point in a system's lifecycle\" — not exhaustive testing of every log entry.\n\n**Assessment:** From my resource-efficiency vantage point, this is a low-cost, high-value verification activity. It uses existing personnel (compliance team) to validate existing infrastructure (logging mechanisms). The periodic nature bounds the resource commitment. The risk it mitigates (regulatory failure, system shutdown) would be enormously more expensive to deal with reactively. It's essentially a QA check on compliance infrastructure — similar to how you'd test fire suppression systems periodically. It doesn't require new funding or infrastructure investment.\n\nHowever, I should note that as Lab Director focused on scientific ambition, I want this to be genuinely bounded and not balloon into an open-ended compliance project that consumes R&D resources indefinitely. The proposal as written is reasonably scoped though — \"periodic\" and targeting specific log retrieval.\n\nI'd lean toward approve with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's efficient use of existing resources, protects against catastrophic d