erns:**\n1. \"Every new project\" - in an R&D lab, projects are fluid and experimental. A rigid gate could slow down scientific exploration. The \"before receiving internal funding\" requirement might be too early for exploratory work where the end-use isn't yet clear.\n2. The compliance review board adds overhead. Who staffs it? This is a resource cost itself. If we have limited people, dedicating them to classification reviews competes with actual R&D work.\n3. \"Formal risk certification\" language might be too bureaucratic for an R&D environment where things evolve rapidly. A project that starts as low-risk might morph into high-risk as the intended purpose becomes clearer.\n4. However - the Act's requirements (Article 12) only apply to high-risk systems. So getting this classification right IS critical. If we misclassify and don't build logging in, we're non-compliant when the system goes live.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThis is genuinely useful from a resource-allocation perspective. The key insight - you can't optimize what you haven't classified - is sound. Building Article 12 compliance into every project would be enormously wasteful; building it only where required is efficient. The gating at funding stage means we catch scope issues before expensive implementation begins.\n\nThe main risk is that the process becomes too slow/rigid for an R&D environment, but that's a design detail, not a fundamental flaw in the concept. The core idea - classify early, allocate compliance resources accordingly - is exactly what a resource-constrained lab needs.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It directly serves my domain concern (resource-allocation efficiency). I'll approve it with a moderately high promise score - maybe 72-78. It's clearly valuable but the \"every new project\" and \"before internal funding\" elements need careful design to not strangle R&D velocity. The concept is right; execution will determine how much overhead it adds.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promi