gging solutions. This preserves scientific ambition while meeting regulatory requirements within budget.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- A shared library leverages the fact that multiple teams are working in the same lab. It's an institutional asset that compounds value with each new project.\n- The decorator/interceptor pattern means it can be layered onto existing codebases without requiring rewrites, preserving existing investment in research code.\n- It creates a single point of maintenance for compliance logic—if regulations update or interpretations change, you fix one library rather than N scattered implementations.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- There's an upfront cost to building the middleware library. But compared to N teams each building their own solution, this is strictly more efficient.\n- \"Lightweight\" and \"language-agnostic\" might be aspirational; real-world implementation could require some framework-specific adapters. But the architecture described (decorators/interceptors) is well-understood and feasible.\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded—it's about baseline compliance logging, not a full observability platform.\n\n**Verdict from my vantage point:**\nThis is exactly the kind of proposal I'd want to approve. It addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement in the most resource-efficient way possible—centralizing a one-time engineering investment that then scales across all future projects at near-zero marginal cost. It doesn't compete with research ambitions; it enables them by removing compliance friction. It uses existing infrastructure (the teams' codebases) without requiring disruption. The ROI is clear: one library build vs. N redundant implementations, and it de-risks the lab's entire high-risk AI pipeline.\n\nI'd rate this very highly. Maybe 85-90. It's not a perfect 100 because there's some upfront engineering investment required before any payoff, and \"language-agnostic\" in practice might require more wo