        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2662\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core mechanism—event-driven document regeneration triggered by version-control hooks on model artifact and dataset commits—is a well-understood DevOps pattern (analogous to auto-generated API docs or changelogs) and is technically viable; a working prototype for a subset of change types is achievable in 3–4 weeks. However, the 'real time' framing overstates what Article 12 actually requires (logs that are up-to-date over the system's lifetime), and the proposal underspecifies where most of the engineering effort will land: encoding the mapping from granular technical deltas (a hyperparameter shift, a dataset swap) to the specific regulatory text sections that must change, which requires iterative legal-compliance co-design rather than a pure engineering build. As a prototyping target I'd narrow scope to an event-triggered pipeline with templated section updates and human-in-the-loop review before full automation, but the underlying architecture is sound and deployable.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2662 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\" - an automated system that generates and updates technical documentation in real time using version-control hooks triggered by model parameter or dataset changes, mapping logs to EU AI Act requirements.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability Assessment:**\n\n1. **Core concept**: Automated documentation generation via version-control hooks that trigger on model/dataset changes. This is technically feasible - we already have CI/CD pipelines, git hooks, and build systems. Extending these to generate compliance documents is a well-understood engineering pattern (like generating API docs from OpenAPI specs, or generating changelogs).\n\n2. **Real-time generation**: \"Real time\" documentation updates are achievable through event-driven architectures (webhooks on commit/push events triggering doc regeneration pipelines). This is standard DevOps practice.\n\n3. **Mapping to EU AI Act requirements**: This is the harder part. The mapping between technical changes (a new training epoch, a hyperparameter tweak) and what that means for the compliance dossier requires domain expertise encoded into templates/rules. This isn't purely mechanical - you need to know which model changes require updating which sections of the documentation. However, this can be prototyped incrementally.\n\n4. **\"Living records\" replacing static documents**: Technically sound. Git-based versioning with semantic tagging per compliance requirement is a well-established pattern.\n\n5. **Version-control hooks triggering updates on parameter/dataset modification**: This requires instrumenting training pipelines to emit events when parameters change or datasets are swapped. Feasible but adds complexity to existing ML infrastructure. For datasets, you need content hashing and provenance tracking. For model parameters, you need to detect meaningful changes vs. noise (e.g., a retrain that converges to essentially the same weights).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n\n1. **Prototyping speed**: A prototype is quite achievable in 2-4 weeks. You can:\n   - Set up a git repo for compliance docs\n   - Write hooks on model artifact commits (MLflow, Weights & Biases integration)\n   - Create templated document sections that get auto-populated from metadata\n   - Map specific change types to document sections\n\n2. **Quality considerations**: \n   - The \"real time\" claim is slightly overblown - it's really \"event-triggered\" not truly real-time, but functionally equivalent for compliance purposes\n   - The main risk is scope creep: trying to handle ALL possible model changes with perfect documentation updates vs. handling the 80% case well\n   - The mapping from technical change → regulatory language requires legal/compliance input, which could slow prototyping\n\n3. **Risks**: \n   - Over-engineering: building a fully automated system when a semi-automated one (auto-generate draft, human review) would be faster to deploy and equally compliant\n   - The \"human error mitigation\" argument is valid but the solution might be more complex than needed for initial compliance\n   - Integration with existing ML infrastructure could take longer than expected if our current pipelines aren't well-instrumented\n\n4. **What's missing technically**: \n   - No mention of how the system handles partial changes (e.g., one layer retrained)\n   - No discussion of audit trail for the documentation generation process itself (who/what triggered the update)\n   - No mention of how to handle multi-model systems where a change in one component affects the whole\n\n**Overall assessment**: This is technically viable and prototyped-able. The core mechanism (event-driven doc generation from version control) is well-understood. The main technical challenges are in the mapping layer (change → regulatory text) and in instrumenting existing ML pipelines to emit meaningful events. A prototype demonstrating the pipeline end-to-end for a subset of changes is very achievable. However, \"real time\" is marketing language for what should be an event-driven batch process, and the full scope (\"replace static documents with living records\") might overstate what's needed for initial compliance - you need up-to-date docs, not necessarily ones that regenerate on every parameter tweak.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This clears the bar for technical viability. It's a reasonable engineering project with clear prototype milestones. I'd approve it but note the \"real time\" framing is slightly misleading and the scope should be narrowed to what Article 12 actually requires (automatic logging of events + up-to-date technical documentation) rather than an aspirational full automation system.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's technically sound, prototyped-able within reasonable timelines, addresses a real requirement. Docked points for: \"real time\" being overkill/misleading, the mapping layer being underspecified (which is where most of the actual engineering effort would be), and the risk of building more automation than needed when semi-automated might suffice for initial compliance. The core idea is good but the framing suggests a larger build than what's strictly necessary for a viable prototype.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
