ential bottlenecks\n- Technical viability is moderate-to-high for the extraction parts, but lower for the \"always in sync\" guarantee and full regulatory format compliance\n- The proposal underestimates the difficulty of mapping code artifacts to the specific narrative/qualitative requirements of Recital 71 (limitations, risk management documentation)\n- For prototyping speed: if it's a blocking step in every build, this HURTS prototyping. If async, it helps but adds operational complexity.\n\nI think this is a reasonable proposal with genuine technical merit for the extraction parts, but the implementation approach (hooks in training pipeline) poses risks to prototyping velocity, and the scope overestimates what can be fully automated versus what still requires human judgment (limitations, risk context). I'd lean toward approve with moderate promise - it's technically viable in its core concept, addresses a real pain point, but needs significant scoping work before implementation. The \"always in sync\" guarantee is a stretch goal that could sink the project if treated as a hard requirement.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My bar: does this clear my threshold for committing scarce implementation resources? \n\nPros:\n- Reduces a real manual burden (doc writing)\n- Keeps docs version-synced (addresses Recital 71's \"kept up to date\" requirement)\n- Core extraction is technically feasible with existing tooling patterns\n\nCons:\n- Hook-based integration into training pipeline couples ML infra with compliance - bad for prototyping speed\n- \"Always in sync\" is a very high bar that will be hard to maintain\n- Regulatory format is a moving target; hard-coding it in hooks = brittle\n- Doesn't address the qualitative/narrative parts of technical documentation (limitations, risk context)\n- The scope as stated is large and underspecified for a \"pipeline\" - could balloon\n\nI'll approve it but with moderate promise. It's a valid direction that addresses real needs, technically feasible in its core, but has architectural risks to prototyping speed and an over-scoped \"always in sync\" guarantee that could lead to resource drain. I'd say around 55-62 on the promise scale. Let me settle on 58.\n",
