f large reference databases at every inference call could introduce latency. You'd want to hash once per version change, not per inference. But the proposal says \"which specific version\" — so you'd hash the dataset when it's loaded/updated and then reference that hash for each inference. That's reasonable.\n- The \"automated versioning\" part is well-trodden ground (Git-like semantics for data, or DVC-style). Not novel but reliable.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- Directly maps to Article 12(3)(b): \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" This is a specific, testable requirement.\n- Also supports 12(1) (automatic recording of events over lifetime) and 12(2)(a) (identifying situations that may present risk).\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal says \"every inference call or training run\" — for high-throughput systems, logging a hash reference per inference is fine (it's just a pointer), but the initial hashing of large databases needs to be handled carefully. This is a solvable engineering problem though, not a showstopper.\n- It doesn't address storage/retrieval of the actual logs over \"lifetime\" — but that's arguably out of scope for this specific proposal and would be handled by the broader logging infrastructure.\n- The proposal is well-scoped and addresses one clear sub-requirement without trying to boil the ocean.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a technically sound, well-scoped, implementable solution to a specific regulatory requirement. From a development/prototyping standpoint, it's straightforward to build, uses mature patterns, and has clear acceptance criteria (can we produce a log entry that says \"inference #X used dataset version Y with hash Z\"?). I'd approve this without hesitation from my vantage point. The prototype-to-production path is short and the technical risk is low.\n\nPromise score: This is very strong from a dev/prototyping perspective. It's bounded, technically feasible, 