        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2277\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core technical approach is self-contradictory: Article 12(3)(a)–(d) explicitly requires logs that capture specific input data, reference-database matches, and natural-person identifiers for traceability and post-market monitoring, yet differential privacy (which adds noise to obscure individual records) and secure multi-party computation (which prevents any single party from seeing raw values) would defeat the very auditability the regulation demands. From a prototyping standpoint, SMC-based logging introduces order-of-magnitude latency overheads and implementation complexity that make it unsuitable for real-time event capture over a system's lifetime, while DP applied to structured log tuples (timestamps, DB references, I/O pairs) is an open research problem with no off-the-shelf path to a working prototype on any reasonable timeline. The actual engineering need—a tamper-evident, access-controlled, encrypted log store with retention policies and role-based regulator access—is well-understood and buildable in weeks; wrapping it in a cryptographic research layer solves a GDPR conflict that may be overstated (compliance-logging under Art 12 typically has its own legal basis) while adding enormous integration risk.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 8\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is about building a \"Secure and Privacy-Preserving Log Management System\" that would use differential privacy or secure multi-party computation to anonymize sensitive input logs while maintaining auditability, plus building a prototype vault for records.\n\nLet me assess this from a development engineering perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n1. Differential privacy for logs is technically problematic. DP adds noise to data to provide formal privacy guarantees, but for logging purposes (Article 12), the entire point is traceability and auditability. If you add differential privacy noise to input data logs, you fundamentally undermine the ability to identify \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" (Art 12(3)(c)) or \"identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk.\" You can't trace back an event if the log is noisy/anonymized.\n\n2. Secure multi-party computation for logging is extraordinarily complex and computationally expensive. SMC protocols have significant performance overhead (often orders of magnitude slower than plain-text operations). For a logging system that needs to capture events in real-time over the lifetime of a system, SMC would introduce unacceptable latency and complexity. This is not something you can prototype quickly - it's a deep cryptographic research problem with implementation challenges.\n\n3. The fundamental tension: Article 12 requires logs that enable traceability (identifying specific inputs, matching against reference databases, identifying natural persons involved in verification). Differential privacy or SMC would obscure exactly the data needed for these purposes. You can't have it both ways - you need the actual data for audit/traceability AND you want to anonymize it. This is a contradiction in terms for the specific requirements listed in Article 12(3)(a)-(d).\n\n4. \"Balancing GDPR constraints\" - this conflates two different regulatory frameworks. The AI Act's logging requirement and GDPR operate on different legal bases and purposes. A log kept under a legitimate interest or contractual necessity (for compliance with another regulation) may not violate GDPR in the first place. The proposal assumes a conflict that may be overstated, leading to solving the wrong problem.\n\n**Prototyping Speed/Quality Concerns:**\n1. This is framed as a \"dedicated research project\" - which signals it's exploratory and not a near-term deliverable. From a development engineering standpoint, this doesn't give me confidence in speed of delivery or quality of output within reasonable timelines.\n\n2. Building a \"prototype vault for these records\" is relatively straightforward (it's essentially a secure database with access controls), but the differential privacy/SMC layer on top is where all the complexity and risk lives. The prototype would likely be a toy that doesn't scale or handle real-world edge cases.\n\n3. SMC implementations are notoriously difficult to get right, debug, and integrate into existing system architectures. The prototyping effort would be enormous relative to the value delivered for compliance purposes.\n\n4. Differential privacy is more tractable than SMC, but applying it to structured log data (timestamps, database references, input-output pairs) while preserving queryability for regulators is a genuinely open research problem. You can't just \"apply DP\" and expect audit trails to work.\n\n**What's actually needed vs. what this proposes:**\n- What Article 12 actually needs: a logging infrastructure that captures events with appropriate retention, access controls, integrity verification (tamper-evidence), and secure storage. This is a well-understood engineering problem (structured logging, encryption at rest/in transit, access control lists, audit trails).\n- What this proposes: solving a research-grade cryptography/privacy problem as if it's the primary technical challenge when it may be largely unnecessary or even counterproductive to the compliance goal.\n\n**Verdict:** From my vantage point, this proposal has low technical viability for its stated purpose because the core techniques (DP, SMC) work against the very requirements they're trying to satisfy (traceability, auditability). The prototyping timeline would be long and uncertain given the research nature of the cryptographic approaches. A much simpler engineering solution (encrypted structured logs with role-based access, retention policies, integrity hashes) would meet Article 12 without the privacy paradox this proposal creates. I'd reject it.\n\nPromise score: This is not something I'd back from a dev engineering standpoint. The technical approach contradicts the regulatory requirement, and the prototyping path is long and uncertain. Maybe an 8-12 range. It's not zero because the awareness of the privacy angle isn't entirely wrong (there ARE data minimization considerations), but the proposed solution is technically incoherent for the stated goal.\n",
