        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1286\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal embeds auxiliary AI models into what should be a deterministic, verifiable logging pipeline, introducing new failure modes (annotation errors, hallucinated narratives) that undermine the traceability and auditability Article 12 demands; for Annex III 1(a) systems the required log fields are discrete structured facts (timestamps, reference DB ID, matched input, verifier identity), so a 'new architecture' built around real-time semantic annotation is technically over-scoped, adds significant latency/complexity to high-risk inference paths, and has no clear path to a working prototype within a reasonable cycle compared to a straightforward schema-driven event recorder.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 18\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1286 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Semantic Logging for High-Risk Model Interpretability\" — using auxiliary models to annotate complex internal state changes in real-time, transforming raw telemetry into human-understandable semantic narratives.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability, speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n1. \"Auxiliary models to annotate complex internal state changes in real-time\" — This is a massive technical undertaking. You're proposing to build secondary AI systems whose job is to interpret the primary system's internals and produce human-readable narratives. The accuracy, latency, and reliability of these auxiliary annotation models would be extremely difficult to guarantee. In a high-risk system context, if your logging/annotation layer itself is an AI model, you now have a cascading failure mode: the log could be wrong or misleading, which undermines the entire traceability purpose of Article 12.\n\n2. \"Real-time\" annotation of \"complex internal state changes\" — This is a significant engineering challenge. The computational overhead of running auxiliary models in the logging pipeline adds latency and complexity to an already performance-sensitive system. For high-risk systems (e.g., biometric verification under Annex III point 1(a)), you need low-latency operation. Adding real-time semantic interpretation on top could be a bottleneck.\n\n3. \"New logging architecture\" — This implies not just adding a feature but redesigning the logging stack. That's a significant engineering investment with unclear scope. The regulation (Article 12) requires automatic recording of events with specific data points (start/end times, reference databases, input data, identification of natural persons). A \"new architecture\" is overkill for what is fundamentally a structured data capture requirement.\n\n4. The proposal reframes regulatory compliance as \"scientific discovery\" and \"AI transparency.\" While intellectually appealing, this misaligns the engineering goal. Article 12 is about traceability, verifiability, and monitoring — it needs reliable, tamper-evident, queryable records. A semantic narrative is actually harder to verify than raw structured data. A regulator or auditor wants to see \"at time T, input X was compared against database D and produced match Y\" — not a narrative summary that could be ambiguous.\n\n5. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), the logging requirements are very specific: start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of verifying persons. These are discrete, structured facts. \"Semantic narratives\" don't map well to these atomic data points. You'd need both the raw structured logs AND the semantic layer, doubling complexity without clear regulatory benefit.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a research-heavy proposal (\"Research into...\"). As a development engineer focused on prototyping speed, I see this as slow to deliver value. Building auxiliary annotation models, validating their accuracy, integrating them into a real-time pipeline, and ensuring they don't degrade system performance — this is months of R&D before you have a working prototype.\n- The \"quality\" bar is unclear: what makes a semantic annotation \"correct\"? Without ground truth for internal state interpretation, quality assurance becomes extremely difficult.\n- You could get a basic compliant logging system (structured logs capturing the required fields) in weeks. This proposal would take significantly longer and introduces uncertainty about whether the auxiliary models will work reliably enough for production high-risk systems.\n\n**What works:**\n- The general idea of making logs more interpretable has value for post-market monitoring (Article 72) and human oversight (Article 14).\n- There's a legitimate need for dashboards or visualization layers on top of raw logs.\n\n**But the specific proposal as stated:**\n- Is too ambitious relative to the regulatory requirement\n- Introduces new AI components into what should be a deterministic logging pipeline (adding its own failure modes)\n- Misaligns with the structured, verifiable nature of compliance records\n- Has unclear path to prototype within reasonable timelines\n- The \"scientific contribution\" framing suggests this is more of an R&D exploration than a deliverable engineering solution\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is not technically viable as a primary approach to Article 12 compliance. It adds unnecessary complexity, introduces new failure modes (the auxiliary models themselves), and doesn't map well to the specific structured data requirements in paragraph 3. A simpler, deterministic logging architecture that captures the required fields with proper schema would be far more prototypable, reliable, and compliant. The semantic layer could perhaps be a later add-on for monitoring dashboards, but it shouldn't be the core architecture.\n\nDecision: Reject.\n",
