kes existing logs accessible and interpretable in the context of specific regulatory requirements. This is exactly what a compliance officer needs to demonstrate that Art. 12 obligations are being met.\n- It maps directly to the three sub-categories in Art. 12(2) and the four minimum items in Art. 12(3).\n- Read-only design is appropriate — we don't want any risk of log tampering or modification through this interface.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Being able to demonstrate robust compliance observability to partners, clients, and regulators strengthens market trust.\n- It signals organizational maturity in AI governance.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- It's a frontend/presentation tool that depends on the underlying unified telemetry logs actually existing and being correctly structured. If the backend logging infrastructure isn't built first, this dashboard has nothing to display. However, as a compliance screening exercise, I should judge it on its own merits — it's a valid complement to whatever logging infrastructure is in place.\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded — read-only, focused specifically on Art. 12 requirements, not a bloated enterprise tool.\n- \"Risk triggers and verification steps\" are the right focus areas for compliance demonstration.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, directly relevant proposal that addresses a genuine operational gap in demonstrating Article 12 compliance. It's read-only (no risk of data integrity issues), focused on the exact regulatory requirements, and reduces audit risk. From my vantage point, this is exactly the kind of tool I would want to have ready when a notified body or market surveillance authority requests evidence of logging compliance.\n\nIt doesn't create new logging capability per se — it's an observability layer. But for a compliance officer, the ability to quickly demonstrate that logs are being generated correctly and contain the required fields is critical for institutional survival during 