y systems (LDAP, SSO, etc.)\n   - Audit logging frameworks\n   - Timestamping mechanisms (NTP-synchronized clocks)\n   - Data management for input tracking\n   \n   If the lab builds a bespoke solution when it could integrate with existing infrastructure, that's poor resource allocation.\n\n2. **Funding constraints**: This is compliance work, not research. It doesn't advance scientific ambition. However, it's also not purely administrative overhead - if the lab deploys Annex III systems, this is mandatory and the cost of non-compliance (fines under EU AI Act can be up to €35M or 7% of global turnover) dwarfs the implementation cost. The question is whether the scope is right-sized.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The proposal says \"The lab will build a secure authentication interface\" - this suggests building from scratch rather than integrating with what exists. A logging module that records identities, timestamps, and links to input data could be largely achieved through:\n   - Extending existing logging/monitoring infrastructure\n   - Adding a digital signature step to an existing workflow management system\n   - Using existing identity providers\n\n   If the proposal is scoped as \"build everything new,\" it's inefficient. But if it's really just \"add a verification audit layer on top of what we already have,\" it's reasonable and proportionate.\n\n4. **Scope assessment**: The proposal covers exactly Article 12(3)(a)-(d): start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons verifying results. This is appropriately scoped to the legal minimum. It doesn't over-scope into building a full post-market monitoring platform (Article 72) or a general risk-management system.\n\n5. **The \"digital signature\" component**: This adds complexity. Article 12(3)(d) says \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\" It doesn't explicitly mandate digital signatures - 