ns of logs over lifetime.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Art 12(1) — automatic logging capability\n- \"Unified\" and \"common API across all projects\" is excellent for institutional compliance because it prevents teams from having ad-hoc, inconsistent implementations that might miss requirements\n- \"Prevents costly retrofitting\" is a strong argument — retrofitting logging into systems after design is notoriously difficult and error-prone\n- Real-time capture of system states aligns with the traceability purpose in Recital 71\n- Standardization across research teams reduces the risk of one team's prototype not meeting logging requirements while another does\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It says \"capture system states and event triggers\" but doesn't explicitly map to the specific categories in Art 12(2)(a)-(c) or the minimum requirements in Art 12(3). However, as a framework/library, it could be designed with those as specifications.\n- The proposal focuses on high-risk AI prototypes specifically, which is appropriate scope.\n- It doesn't mention retention periods, log integrity/security, or how logs would be made available to regulators (though these might be separate workstreams).\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — the proposal says \"during execution\" which could be interpreted as only during active use, not necessarily covering the full lifecycle. But this is a minor semantic point and likely intended to mean the same thing.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong compliance-aligned proposal. It directly implements the core technical requirement of Article 12(1) in a way that institutionalizes compliance across all teams rather than leaving it to individual projects. The \"unified\" approach reduces compliance risk significantly — instead of auditing N different logging implementations, I'd be reviewing one standardized library. This is exactly the kind