bility requirements in Article 12(2).\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal conflates \"documentation\" with \"logging.\" Article 12(1) is specifically about automatic recording of *events* (logs) — start/end times, input data, reference databases. A documentation pipeline doesn't capture operational event logs. It's a documentation management tool, not an event logging system. However, the proposal does claim to address Recital 71 broadly, and documentation IS part of what Recital 71 requires.\n- The scope as stated is focused on technical documentation (algorithms, training data, risk assessments) rather than operational logs (Article 12(3)(a)-(d) for Annex III point 1(a) systems). But the proposal doesn't claim to be the complete solution — it's one component.\n- \"Mandatory documentation template integrated into Git workflow\" — this is a strong process control. From a compliance standpoint, embedding requirements into the development workflow (shift-left compliance) is best practice.\n- The \"automatic compilation into regulatory dossiers\" is valuable but also risky if done incorrectly. A regulator expects specific formats and content. However, as an initial pipeline concept, it's directionally correct.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n\nThis proposal directly addresses a core obligation under Recital 71 and supports Article 12's traceability requirements. The fact that it embeds compliance into the development workflow (Git integration) means it scales with the organization rather than relying on manual compliance efforts that break down under pressure. This is exactly what a regulator would want to see: systematic, automated documentation management.\n\nThe \"mandatory\" aspect before deployment is a strong gate — it prevents non-compliant systems from entering production without proper documentation. This protects institutional survival by reducing the risk of deploying a system that doesn't