licating work and maintaining coherence in how systems are managed.\n\n**Arguments for caution/rejection:**\n1. There's a risk of over-engineering: building a \"standardized software library\" with an \"internal API\" could become a massive engineering project that diverts resources from actual R&D. If the lab is small or has few high-risk systems, this might be disproportionate.\n2. The proposal doesn't specify scope — how many high-risk systems does the lab actually develop? If it's 1-2, a unified framework might be overkill compared to a simpler solution.\n3. \"Consistent timestamps, input tracking, and database references\" — these are relatively straightforward requirements from Article 12(3). A lightweight shared module might suffice rather than a full \"framework.\"\n4. The risk of the project becoming a bottomless pit: \"standardized software library\" could balloon in scope as different teams want different features.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is actually a very strong proposal. Here's why:\n- It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by centralizing a compliance requirement that would otherwise be duplicated across projects.\n- It protects scientific ambition by not requiring individual research teams to spend their limited time on boilerplate compliance logging — they just integrate an API call.\n- It makes effective use of existing R&D infrastructure by creating a shared platform layer rather than ad-hoc solutions per project.\n- The EU AI Act mandates this (Article 12), so it's not optional — the question is whether to do it once or many times. Doing it once is clearly more efficient.\n- \"Prevents fragmented implementation\" speaks directly to infrastructure management concerns I care about.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep in building the framework itself, but the concept is sound and the resource logic is clear: one investment, N systems served. The alternative (each team building their own logging) is strictly worse