tudied field. The specific application to AI system failure modes adds context, but the core methodology (pattern detection in logs, correlation with performance degradation) isn't obviously at the frontier unless more specific technical challenges are identified.\n- \"Log spikes\" correlating with \"model performance degradation\" sounds somewhat generic. In practice, for many high-risk AI systems under Annex III, the logging requirements in Article 12(3) are quite narrow (timestamps, reference database references, input data matches, verifier IDs). These aren't really rich enough to do deep forensic analysis of \"emergent risks and unpredictable behaviors.\" The logs mandated by the Act are designed for traceability and compliance verification, not for capturing the internal dynamics that would reveal emergent behavior.\n- There's a tension: the proposal frames this as \"scientific insight\" into \"emergent risks,\" but the actual data available under Article 12 is fairly superficial (usage periods, database references, input matches, verifier IDs). To actually study emergent behaviors, you'd need much deeper telemetry than what the regulation mandates. So the scientific promise may be overstated relative to the data actually available.\n- The phrase \"turns compliance data into scientific insight\" is appealing but potentially misleading — it's not clear that Article 12 logs contain enough signal for meaningful failure-mode forensics of complex AI systems.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe intellectual framing is attractive — using regulatory data as a research resource, developing detection algorithms for system failures. However, the scientific novelty claim feels somewhat thin when examined closely. The core methodology (pattern matching in logs, correlation analysis) isn't obviously at the frontier unless there are very specific technical challenges unique to AI systems that aren't captured here. And the data actually mandated by Article 1