        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1095 is about an \"Automated Technical Documentation Synthesis Engine\" — an internal AI tool that generates living technical documentation by analyzing code commits and experiment logs in real-time, fine-tuning a specialized model on research notes and codebase to auto-synthesize summaries required by Recital 71.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point (technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping):\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n1. The proposal conflates two different regulatory requirements: Article 12's logging requirement (automatic recording of events over the lifetime of a high-risk AI system) and Recital 71's technical documentation requirement. Article 12 is about the *system under regulation* having logging capabilities built in, not about an internal tool generating documentation for auditors. The proposal seems to misunderstand what \"record-keeping\" means here — it's not just producing pretty summaries of code commits; it's about the AI system itself technically allowing automatic recording of specific events (start/end times, reference databases used, input data matched, verifier identification).\n\n2. Fine-tuning a model on research notes and codebase to \"synthesize summaries\" is a fundamentally different problem from what Article 12 requires. Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires very specific structured data: exact timestamps, reference database identifiers, specific input data that matched, and identification of natural persons who verified results. This isn't a \"summarization\" problem — it's a structured event logging problem with precise fields.\n\n3. Using an AI model to generate documentation introduces reliability concerns from a prototyping standpoint. For audit purposes, you need deterministic, verifiable records. An LLM generating summaries could hallucinate, omit critical details, or reformat information in ways that lose precision. This is technically risky for compliance.\n\n4. The \"real-time\" analysis of code commits and experiment logs sounds ambitious but the actual regulatory requirement is about runtime event logging in deployed systems, not about analyzing development artifacts post-hoc. Code commits don't capture production events like \"the system was used from 14:23 to 14:47 against database X and matched input Y with verification by person Z.\"\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Perspective:**\n- Fine-tuning a specialized model is a significant engineering investment (data curation, training infrastructure, evaluation) before you even have a working prototype. This slows down prototyping.\n- The simpler, more technically viable approach would be structured event logging at the application layer — emitting JSON events with required fields to a persistent store. That's a weekend project, not a model-fine-tuning project.\n- The proposal over-engineers the solution. The \"AI\" aspect adds complexity without adding value for what is fundamentally a deterministic data-capture problem.\n\n**What it gets right:**\n- The instinct to automate documentation generation has some merit — manual documentation is painful and error-prone.\n- Keeping documentation \"up to date throughout lifetime\" (Recital 71) does benefit from automation.\n- But the specific mechanism proposed (fine-tuned LLM summarizing code commits) is technically misaligned with what Article 12 actually requires.\n\n**My assessment:** The proposal identifies a real pain point (documentation overhead) but proposes a technically mismatched solution. Fine-tuning an AI model to summarize code commits does not produce the structured, deterministic event logs that Article 12(3) mandates. It also introduces hallucination risk into compliance-critical records. From a prototyping speed perspective, this is a months-long ML project where weeks of structured logging engineering would suffice for the core requirement. The \"real-time\" aspect and \"living documentation\" framing sounds appealing but obscures the fact that the regulatory text requires specific data fields captured at runtime, not synthesized narratives.\n\nI'm going to reject this. It's technically misaligned with the actual regulatory requirement, over-engineered for what is a structured logging problem, and introduces reliability risks into compliance-critical records. The prototyping path it suggests (fine-tune a model) is slow and uncertain compared to the straightforward engineering solution of implementing structured event emitters in the system pipeline.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 20-25. There's a kernel of valid thinking about automating documentation, but the specific technical approach is wrong for this regulatory requirement and would be a poor use of prototyping resources.\n",
