veillance.\n- Reduces manual labor costs — this matters for institutional survival because it makes compliance sustainable rather than a one-time spike.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It describes \"a digital management system\" without specifying whether it meets the specific logging requirements in Art. 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.). However, this may not be relevant if the lab's systems don't fall under that category.\n- It doesn't explicitly mention \"automatic recording of events\" as a technical capability of the AI system itself (Art. 12(1)) — it seems more focused on documentation management rather than the logging infrastructure embedded in the system. But it does say \"automated versioning\" which is adjacent.\n- The scope says \"technical documentation and logs\" so it covers both aspects.\n\nOverall: This is a solid, well-targeted compliance proposal. It addresses the core regulatory requirement for lifetime traceability through automated systems rather than manual processes. From a compliance standpoint, this reduces enforcement risk significantly — if we can't demonstrate up-to-date technical documentation linked to releases, we face non-conformity findings, potential withdrawal orders, and reputational damage with regulators and clients. The automation angle also makes it sustainable for institutional survival (cost management).\n\nI would approve this. It's directly on-point for the regulatory requirements in my domain. The promise score: it's a strong proposal that addresses a core compliance obligation, but it's somewhat generic in its description — I'd want to see more specificity about what \"digital management system\" means technically and how it ensures completeness of logs per Art. 12(2)(a)-(c). But as a directional commitment to build this capability, it clears the bar. I'll give it around 78-82. Let me settle on 80 — strong alignment with my mandate, but the proposal