        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal P2095 is about an \"Automated Provenance Framework for Model Forensics\" — using Article 12 logs to create causal maps of decision-making processes by correlating event logs with latent space activations during inference. The goal is to backtrack from risk-events to specific training data or input triggers, converting compliance record-keeping into a scientific diagnostic tool.\n\nLet me assess this from my resource-allocation and infrastructure perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- It leverages mandatory Article 12 logging (which we must implement anyway) as a foundation, so there's some synergy with compliance work. The logs are being generated regardless; this proposal uses them for additional value.\n- Converting a compliance cost into a research asset is philosophically attractive from an efficiency standpoint — \"two birds, one stone.\"\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The technical ambition is extremely high: correlating event logs (which are temporal operational records) with \"latent space activations during inference\" requires instrumenting models at the activation level during every inference pass. This is NOT what Article 12 logging provides. Article 12(1) says systems shall technically allow automatic recording of events (logs). The specific requirements in paragraphs 2 and 3 are about operational events (start/end times, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of verifying persons). It does not require or even naturally produce latent space activation traces.\n- To get latent space activations, you need to instrument the model architecture itself with hooks at every layer, capture tensor states during inference, store them, and then build a correlation engine between those massive activation tensors and the operational logs. This is an entirely separate infrastructure investment from what Article 12 mandates.\n- \"Causal maps of decision-making processes\" in deep learning models is an extremely active and largely unsolved research problem. Interpreting which specific training data point caused which activation pattern at which layer to produce a final output is not a solved engineering problem — it's frontier interpretability research (related to attribution methods, causal tracing, etc.). Building this as an \"internal tool\" assumes a level of technical maturity that doesn't exist yet.\n- The resource commitment would be enormous: you need model instrumentation infrastructure, massive storage for activation tensors, GPU-compute for correlation analysis, and a team of interpretability researchers (not just engineers) to develop and maintain the causal mapping algorithms. This is essentially funding an entire interpretability research program disguised as a compliance tool.\n- The \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" criterion: this proposal does NOT primarily use existing infrastructure. It requires building new infrastructure for activation capture, new storage systems (activation tensors are orders of magnitude larger than operational logs), and new computational pipelines.\n- The balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints is badly skewed here. The scientific ambition (solving model interpretability via causal tracing) vastly exceeds what the compliance requirement actually demands or enables. Article 12 logging gives you operational traces, not neural activity traces.\n\n**From my vantage point:**\nThis is a proposal that uses the language of compliance (\"leverages Article 12 logs\") to smuggle in a massive interpretability research program that requires entirely new infrastructure, frontier-level R&D investment, and has no clear timeline to produce usable outputs. The \"converting mandatory record-keeping into a powerful diagnostic tool\" framing is seductive but misleading — the mandatory record-keeping (operational event logs) does not contain or enable the latent space correlation described. You'd be building a new system that happens to also read the compliance logs as one input among many.\n\nThe resource-allocation efficiency question: Is this the best use of scarce implementation resources? The compliance obligation is clear and bounded — implement logging per Article 12(1)-(3). This proposal adds an unbounded interpretability research program on top, with uncertain scientific payoff and massive infrastructure requirements. It's not \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — it's a request to build new, expensive infrastructure for a scientifically uncertain outcome.\n\nI would reject this from my vantage point. The compliance component is trivial (just implement the logs). The research component is frontier interpretability work dressed in compliance clothing, requiring resources disproportionate to any clear deliverable.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 15-20. There's a kernel of interesting thinking (using operational data for diagnostic purposes), but as framed, it's a massive over-commitment with unclear scientific payoff and infrastructure that doesn't exist yet. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this is not an efficient use of our constrained implementation budget.\n\nLet me settle on 18. It has intellectual interest but the resource ask relative to compliance value is badly proportioned, and the scientific ambition far exceeds what the infrastructure supports or the funding constraints allow for a \"compliance response.\"\n",
