esn't require preserving every equation. A standardized lexicon could be useful but risks becoming an academic exercise rather than a practical tool.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: A version-controlled wiki linked to model repositories is actually quite aligned with how we already work (Git-based repos, documentation). This leverages existing infrastructure well. The \"living templates\" concept means the documentation updates as models evolve — this is actually smart for compliance because Article 12 requires docs be kept current.\n\nStrengths from my vantage:\n- Leverages existing infrastructure (version control, model repos)\n- Addresses a genuine requirement (Art 12/Recital 71) that we must fulfill anyway\n- \"Living\" nature means it stays current without periodic re-documentation sprints\n- Standardized lexicon reduces repeated work across projects\n\nWeaknesses from my vantage:\n- \"Interdisciplinary collaborative effort\" is vague and potentially expensive — how many researcher-hours are we committing?\n- The framing around \"preventing loss of intellectual nuance\" could lead to over-engineering; regulators want clarity, not mathematical rigor in documentation\n- No clear scope or deliverable timeline mentioned\n- Could become a perpetual \"initiative\" that consumes resources without producing the specific compliance artifacts needed\n- The \"living templates\" concept is good but the proposal doesn't specify how it integrates with actual Article 12 logging requirements (the automatic event recording, the Annex III point 1(a) specifics)\n\nOverall assessment: This addresses a real need and leverages existing infrastructure reasonably well. However, from my resource-allocation perspective, it's somewhat vague in scope and has a risk of becoming an open-ended collaborative project that consumes researcher time without producing concrete compliance deliverables. The core idea (version-controlled documentation linked to model repos, st