lly requires \"automatic\" recording. A middleware approach ensures this happens without relying on individual researchers remembering or manually implementing it. This is a strong alignment with the letter of the law.\n3. **Immutable storage**: Writing to \"secure, immutable storage\" addresses the traceability and non-tampering aspects implied by Recital 71 (\"traceability of those systems, verify compliance\"). If logs can be altered, they're useless for regulatory verification.\n4. **Minimizes researcher friction**: This is important because if the logging system is too burdensome, researchers will work around it or implement it poorly, creating compliance gaps. Reducing friction increases adoption and therefore actual compliance coverage.\n5. **API-driven / central library**: Makes it easier to ensure uniformity and easier to update when regulations evolve.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. The proposal is somewhat generic - \"event timestamps and input data\" may not fully cover Article 12(2)(a)-(c) requirements or the specific Annex III 1(a) minimums in Article 12(3). It doesn't explicitly mention logging for risk identification, substantial modification detection, or post-market monitoring events.\n2. \"Universal wrapper\" is ambitious - different model architectures may make a truly universal implementation difficult. But this is more of an engineering concern than a compliance one; the intent is right.\n3. The proposal doesn't mention retention periods or lifetime duration explicitly, though \"over the lifetime of the system\" is in Article 12(1).\n4. No mention of how logs will be made available to notified bodies or regulators for verification purposes (Article 71 requirements).\n\nHowever, from a compliance officer's perspective, this is a strong foundational proposal. It addresses the core mechanical requirement of Article 12(1) - automatic logging - and does so in a way that institutionalizes compliance rather than leaving it to individual project teams. The immutable storage addresses integrity concerns. The standardization reduces the risk of non-conforming implementations across projects.\n\nThe main gap is specificity around the Article 12(2) event categories and Article 12(3) minimums, but those can be addressed in implementation specs. The architectural direction is correct and compliance-aligned.\n\nI would approve this. It's a strong institutional commitment to meeting the baseline requirement of Article 12(1) in a way that supports ongoing compliance across all projects. From a market reputation standpoint, having a unified, auditable logging infrastructure demonstrates organizational maturity to regulators and customers.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my concerns. It addresses automatic recording (Art 12(1)), standardization for institutional consistency, immutability for integrity, and reduces the risk of non-compliance through researcher error. The main limitation is that it's a general framework - it needs to be configured per project type to meet Art 12(2) and 12(3) specifics. But as an architectural commitment, it's strong. I'd put this at around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because it doesn't explicitly address the specific event categories or Annex III minimums, but the direction is clearly right and it addresses the foundational requirement well.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
