oncept addresses both security of logs and potential GDPR concerns about who can access identity verification records.\n\nConcerns from a compliance perspective:\n1. The proposal says \"encrypted ledger\" - but Article 12 requires that logs enable traceability and post-market monitoring (Art 72). If the ledger is too heavily encrypted, it could impede regulatory authorities' ability to inspect, or the deployer's obligation under Art 26(5) to monitor operations. We need to ensure encryption doesn't compromise accessibility for compliance purposes (e.g., market surveillance authorities).\n2. The proposal focuses narrowly on Annex III point 1(a). If we develop other high-risk systems (Annex I products, or other Annex III categories), this wouldn't cover them. But the proposal is specifically targeted, so that's acceptable as a scope limitation.\n3. \"Transforms a strict regulatory burden into a proprietary technical advantage\" - from my compliance perspective, this framing is fine but slightly misaligned. Compliance is about meeting obligations, not creating competitive advantages per se. However, having robust systems does help with audit readiness and demonstrates commitment to regulators.\n4. The proposal mentions \"human verification identities\" which maps to Article 14(5) - the identification of natural persons involved in verification of results. This is correct and important for compliance.\n5. One concern: the proposal doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or how logs will be made available to competent authorities (which may be required under other articles). But as an R&D project, this might be addressed later.\n\n**Institutional survival lens:** If we develop Annex III 1(a) systems and don't have compliant logging, we face:\n- Prohibition of placing the system on the market\n- Fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover (for prohibited practices) or €15M/3% for other violations\n- Reputational damage that could be existential for an R&D institution\n\