ojects,\" \"across various model architectures,\" generating full technical documentation automatically) represents a very large resource commitment that may not be justified relative to simpler approaches (adapting existing observability tools, building minimal compliance logging modules rather than a full middleware layer). From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, I'd want this scoped down significantly before committing scarce engineering resources. The proposal as stated risks becoming a multi-year infrastructure project that diverts engineers from core R&D work — the very balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints that's my domain of concern.\n\nThat said, it's not a terrible idea. The modular framing is sensible. The \"minimizes administrative overhead\" angle is good for researcher productivity. But as written, it's over-scoped and under-justified in terms of why you can't adapt existing tools. I'd lean toward rejecting as written — not because the goal is wrong, but because the resource commitment implied by \"develop a standardized middleware API across various model architectures\" to serve \"all internal projects\" is too large without evidence that lighter approaches won't suffice. This is exactly the kind of proposal where an R&D lab could sink 6-12 months of senior engineering into infrastructure while the science stalls, and then find they've built something more complex than needed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The promise score should reflect how promising it is from MY vantage point specifically. The core idea has merit (standardization, reducing per-project overhead). But the execution as proposed is a major resource drain risk. I'd put this at maybe 45-55 range. It's not terrible, but it's over-scoped and doesn't demonstrate efficient use of existing infrastructure or respect for the funding constraints that would make me commit resources to it.\n\nI'll go with reject, promise around 48.\n",
