g against failure modes would be critical for defending against enforcement action or liability claims.\n- \"Substantial modification\" is a key concept in the Act — if logs fail to capture what constitutes a substantial modification, we could be operating an unregistered/unassessed system without knowing it. This is an existential compliance risk.\n- Market reputation: demonstrating rigorous logging validation in our technical documentation (Annex IV) signals to customers and regulators that we take compliance seriously.\n\nArguments AGAINST / Concerns:\n- Resource intensity: building a comprehensive adversarial stress-test suite for logging could be expensive and time-consuming. But as Compliance Officer, I need to weigh this against the risk of non-compliance.\n- The proposal says \"converts compliance into a measurable engineering metric\" — this is somewhat reductive from my perspective. Compliance isn't just about metrics; it's about legal adequacy, proportionality, and documented decision-making. However, measurability helps in demonstrating compliance to regulators.\n- There's a risk of over-engineering: the Act requires \"a level of traceability... that is appropriate to the intended purpose.\" Not all systems need exhaustive adversarial testing of every possible failure mode. But as a high-risk AI developer, having this capability available and applied where proportionate is sensible.\n- The proposal focuses on Article 79(1) analysis specifically. Article 72 (post-market monitoring) and Article 26(5) monitoring are also referenced in Art 12(2)(b) and (c). A comprehensive suite should cover all three, not just 79(1). But this is a scoping detail that can be addressed in implementation.\n\nOverall assessment: This proposal directly addresses the core obligation of Article 12 — that logs must actually function for their regulatory purpose, not merely exist. From a compliance standpoint, being able to demonstrate through testing that our logging captures risk 