ntal work) have a meaningful impact on broader scientific impact? There's an argument that premature application of full production-grade logging requirements to exploratory research could slow down iteration and experimentation. But the proposal as stated is about governance and resource allocation, not about advancing science.\n\nFrom my strict vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal:\n- Does not advance knowledge in any domain\n- Does not contribute to intellectual merit of the lab's research output\n- Has at most a very tangential relationship to broader scientific impact (protecting agility is operational, not scientific)\n- Is essentially an administrative/compliance governance mechanism\n\nMy assessment: This falls well below my bar. It's an operations/governance proposal that doesn't touch on knowledge advancement, intellectual merit of research, or meaningful scientific impact. I would reject it from my vantage point.\n\nPromise score: Very low. Maybe 5-10. There's a tiny sliver where \"protecting scientific agility\" could be seen as enabling more frontier exploration by not over-burdening early work, but the proposal itself doesn't do anything to advance science — it just classifies projects. I'll give it around 7-8.\n\nLet me settle on my assessment:\n- Rationale: This is an internal governance and resource-allocation mechanism with no bearing on knowledge advancement, intellectual merit of research output, or broader scientific impact. At most it tangentially protects experimental agility by preventing premature application of production-grade logging to early-stage work, but the proposal itself contributes nothing to the scientific frontier.\n- Decision: reject\n- Promise: 7\n",
