frastructure build - it's instrumenting existing data flows.\n\n**Cons / Risks:**\n- There's a question of whether \"automated extraction\" can truly satisfy the \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement. Recital 71 mentions algorithms, training, testing, validation processes, risk-management system documentation. Auto-extraction from logs covers hyperparameters and metrics well, but narrative explanations of algorithmic choices, risk management rationale, etc., may still need human input. The proposal somewhat overstates what automation can cover.\n- However, even partial automation (covering the data-heavy portions) saves significant time while humans focus on the interpretive/narrative parts.\n- Build cost: There is a one-time investment in building and maintaining this tool. But given that it's integrated into an existing CI/CD pipeline and leverages existing training logs, the marginal cost should be moderate rather than massive.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\nThis is a high-leverage investment. The ongoing compliance burden for high-risk AI systems under Article 12 (and related articles) is continuous. Building this once pays dividends across all future high-risk systems and throughout their entire lifetimes. It's the kind of infrastructure investment that transforms a recurring cost into a one-time amortized cost.\n\nThe fact that it integrates with existing CI/CD infrastructure means we're not building from scratch - we're adding instrumentation to what already runs. This is efficient use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is scoped reasonably: it's about capturing metadata and generating structured reports, not about replacing all human judgment in documentation. The \"live, code-driven manifest\" language suggests a practical engineering approach rather than an over-ambitious \"AI writes everything\" fantasy.\n\nI think this clears my bar comfortably. It's a resource-efficient use of existing infrastructure that reduces recurring compliance costs and frees researcher capacity for scientific work. The scope is well-defined and the ROI over system lifetimes is clear.\n\nPromise score: This is very strong from my vantage point. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a perfect solution (some documentation will still need human narrative), and there's build risk, but the efficiency gains are substantial and it leverages existing infrastructure well. I'll go with 84.\n",
