g capabilities\n- The documentation of algorithms, training data, validation processes is genuinely required for compliance\n- Without this, we'd face regulatory issues that could block market entry\n\nBut the proposal as written is somewhat over-engineered for prototyping:\n- \"Mandatory review cycle at each development milestone\" - during rapid prototyping, you might have 5+ milestones a week. Requiring full documentation and audit before proceeding to next phase would dramatically slow iteration.\n- The gate mechanism (cannot proceed without auditor verification) is a single point of failure in the development pipeline.\n- It focuses on \"preventing loss of institutional knowledge\" which is a valid concern but somewhat secondary to the actual regulatory requirement of traceability and logging.\n\nOn the positive side:\n- Having a standardized template reduces time spent figuring out what to document each time\n- A secure repository is technically simple to implement\n- Documenting training data and validation processes is genuinely needed and doing it incrementally at milestones is better than trying to reconstruct documentation after the fact\n\nThe key tension for me: the proposal conflates \"keeping documentation up to date\" (which Recital 71 requires) with a hard blocking gate that must clear before ANY development progress. A more prototyping-friendly approach would be to have documentation as a parallel track that catches up, or to make the gate less frequent (e.g., per major release rather than every milestone), or to make the template lightweight enough that it doesn't add significant overhead.\n\nMy assessment: The core idea is sound and technically viable - you do need structured documentation for compliance. But the implementation as described (hard gate at EVERY milestone with dedicated auditor) will create a significant bottleneck for prototyping speed. It's not unworkable, but it's heavy-handed. I'd lean toward approving with reservations because