ern is scope - it may be positioned as solving Art. 12(3)(b) when it really solves the training-side provenance and creates a foundation for inference-side logging, but as a prototyping project, it's low-risk and fast to deliver.\n\nI would approve this. It's technically viable, quick to prototype, uses well-known tools, and addresses a genuine compliance requirement. The promise score should reflect that while there's the scope gap (training vs inference), the core engineering is solid and fast-moving. I'd put it around 72-78. It's not a 90+ because of the scope limitation relative to what Art. 12(3) actually requires at runtime, but it's clearly a green-light project from an engineering viability standpoint.\n",
