        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2374\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Technically this is well-understood engineering (auth integration + structured audit logging + data linkage), and the narrow scope to Annex III 1(a) systems with four explicit sub-requirements gives us clear acceptance criteria for a fast prototype. My main reservation is that mandating full digital-signature capture per verifier action may over-specify beyond what Article 12(3)(d) literally demands (\\\"identification of the natural persons\\\"), adding PKI/infrastructure overhead that slows first-iteration delivery; however, as a defense-in-depth posture for liability it's defensible and still prototypeable within a standard sprint cycle.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2374 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Human-in-the-Loop Verification Audit System\" - a specialized logging module for Annex III systems that records identities of natural persons verifying AI results, tracks match between input data and reference databases, builds a secure authentication interface requiring digital signatures when a human verifier approves/rejects output, timestamps interactions, links to specific input data. Claims to satisfy Article 12(3).\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- A logging module that records identities of verifiers - this is technically straightforward. Authentication + audit logging is well-understood engineering.\n- Secure authentication interface with digital signatures - standard security infrastructure (PKI, SAML/OIDC, etc.). Well-trodden path.\n- Timestamping and linking to input data - database design question, very feasible.\n- The proposal specifically targets Annex III 1(a) systems (biometrics/identification), which is a narrower scope - good for prototyping speed since you're not building a general-purpose logging framework.\n\n**Speed of Prototyping:**\n- This is scoped well enough to prototype quickly. It's essentially: auth integration + structured log storage + digital signature capture. A team could have a working prototype in weeks, not months.\n- The scope is limited to the specific Article 12(3) requirements (4 sub-points), which gives clear acceptance criteria.\n- However, there's a question of how much this integrates with existing systems. If we already have authentication infrastructure, this becomes even faster. If we need to build digital signature capture from scratch, it adds complexity but still manageable.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- The proposal is quite prescriptive about the solution (\"secure authentication interface that requires a digital signature\"). This might be over-engineering for some use cases - do all Annex III 1(a) systems truly need full PKI-level digital signatures? A simpler authenticated session with audit trail might suffice for many deployments. But from a compliance standpoint, being more robust is safer.\n- The proposal doesn't address how logs are stored long-term (lifetime of system), what happens to logs when the system is decommissioned, or how log integrity is preserved against tampering. These are important technical considerations but don't invalidate the core idea.\n- The \"match between input data and reference databases\" tracking is interesting - this means we need to capture not just that a check was performed but what the database state was at that time. This has storage implications for large reference databases.\n\n**Potential issues from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal somewhat conflates the logging requirement (Article 12) with the human oversight requirement (Article 14(5)). The Article 12(3)(d) requirement is to record \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification\" - it doesn't necessarily mandate a digital signature mechanism. A simple authenticated session ID could satisfy this. The proposal over-specifies the implementation approach.\n- That said, having a more robust verification trail (digital signatures) provides stronger evidence and reduces liability risk, which aligns with the lab's interest in compliance.\n- The scope is appropriately narrow for a prototype - it targets one specific regulatory requirement with clear technical boundaries.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my perspective as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed:\n- Technically viable: Yes, clearly. This is standard engineering work (auth + audit logging + data linkage).\n- Speed to prototype: Good. Narrow scope, well-understood components, clear acceptance criteria from the regulation.\n- Quality: The core idea is sound, though the digital signature requirement might be slightly over-engineered for what Article 12(3)(d) literally requires (just \"identification\"). However, as a defense-in-depth measure and for liability protection, it's reasonable.\n- One concern: it's somewhat narrow - only covers Article 12(3) and not the broader logging requirements in 12(1)-(2). But that's fine because it's scoped to Annex III systems specifically.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this. It's technically feasible, well-scoped for rapid prototyping, addresses a clear regulatory requirement, and the components are all standard engineering. The main reservation is whether the digital signature layer is necessary (vs. simpler authenticated session logging), but that's an implementation detail that doesn't block the overall approach.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, technically sound, well-scoped for prototyping. Not a 100 because there are some integration considerations and the over-specification of digital signatures vs. what's strictly required creates some scope risk in terms of delivery speed. But it's clearly buildable and addresses a real compliance gap.\n\nLet me settle on 75.\n",
