at simulate failures in your own compliance-critical systems. This needs careful governance to avoid accidentally triggering actual risk situations or creating documentation that could be misinterpreted by regulators.\n  - The proposal frames this also as advancing \"research on AI reliability, auditing, and the science of observability\" — this is a research framing. As Compliance Officer, my concern is whether this is primarily a compliance verification tool (good) or a research project with compliance as a side benefit (less directly useful for survival).\n  - The scope seems broad: \"a library of adversarial edge cases\" and \"quantitatively measure the latency and accuracy.\" This could be resource-intensive. But it's focused on Article 12 specifically, which is good scoping.\n\n**From institutional survival:** Having a demonstrable, quantified verification that your logging works under stress is critical for regulatory confidence. If a regulator comes to audit or if an incident occurs, being able to show \"we stress-tested our logging against X scenarios and it performed within Y parameters\" is powerful evidence of compliance culture.\n\n**From regulatory compliance:** This directly supports the traceability requirement in Article 12(2) and the specific logging requirements in 12(3). It provides a methodology for verifying that the technical capability exists and functions as required. This is something a notified body or market surveillance authority might reasonably expect to see evidence of.\n\n**From market reputation:** Demonstrating rigorous internal verification of compliance mechanisms positions the institution well with clients, partners, and regulators. If published (carefully), it could become an industry reference point.\n\n**Concerns/risks:**\n- The \"adversarial edge cases\" language could be concerning if not carefully managed — creating documented failure scenarios for high-risk AI systems is sensitive material.\n- Resource commitment: building a full benchmark suite with quantitative measurement is non-trivial engineering work.\n- It's a verification tool, not the actual compliance mechanism. If the logging implementation itself isn't solid, this won't save you.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from a compliance perspective. It creates a repeatable, quantifiable method to verify Article 12 compliance. The main risk is scope creep and resource intensity, but as framed (focused on Article 12 log verification), it's well-scoped. I'd approve it with moderate-to-high promise.\n\nI'll rate this around 72-78. It's very relevant to my domain, directly supports demonstrable compliance, but it's a secondary layer (verification of implementation) rather than the primary implementation itself, and the \"adversarial edge cases\" component needs careful governance. I'll go with 74.\n",
