trative burden\" framing suggests efficiency motivation rather than compliance motivation. From a compliance standpoint, I need to ensure the pipeline produces documentation that meets Article 13 and Annex IV requirements, not just \"compliant\" in a loose sense.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention retention periods, access controls, or audit trails for the logs themselves — which are critical for regulatory inspections.\n- It doesn't address Article 12(3)'s specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (start/end times of use, reference databases, input data matches, identification of verifying persons).\n- \"At every model checkpoint\" is good but the regulation speaks to the *lifetime* of the system in deployment, not just at development checkpoints. There's a gap between training-time documentation and operational logging.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis is a solid proposal that addresses a real compliance need (keeping technical documentation current throughout the system lifecycle). It reduces risk of non-compliance due to documentation drift or gaps. However, it doesn't fully cover the operational logging requirements of Article 12(1) and (3), which are about runtime event recording, not just training/validation documentation.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- It supports compliance with Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation\n- It reduces the risk of documentation gaps that would be flagged in a conformity assessment or post-market monitoring audit\n- It supports market reputation by demonstrating systematic compliance infrastructure\n- However, it's incomplete as a standalone solution to Article 12 — it addresses the \"technical documentation\" side but not the \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" side, especially for deployed systems\n\nI'd lean toward approving this because:\n1. It directly supports a core regulatory requirement (up-to-date technical documentation)\n2. It's practical and integrates with 