k-management system documentation, capabilities and limitations analysis) that doesn't naturally live in MLflow/W&B.\n- The proposal mentions hyperparameters, dataset hashes, and validation results — but Recital 71 also requires documentation of the \"risk-management system\" which is unlikely to be captured in an experiment tracker.\n- It says it \"removes the manual documentation burden that typically slows down rapid prototyping cycles.\" This framing suggests the motivation is R&D velocity, not compliance assurance. The compliance risk is that if we fully automate without human review, we may produce documentation that looks comprehensive but has gaps or inaccuracies. Regulators expect documented justification and expert sign-off.\n- Article 12 specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" — this proposal is about generating technical documentation reports, not about the logging capability itself. It's adjacent to Article 12 but doesn't directly implement the logging requirement.\n- The proposal says it transforms \"existing R&D logs into formal records\" — but there's a gap between what R&D teams log (often ad hoc) and what regulators expect in structured technical documentation.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a genuinely useful compliance enabler, but it's incomplete as a standalone solution for Article 12/Recital 71 compliance. It addresses part of the technical documentation requirement well (the data/training/validation aspects), but misses other required elements (risk-management system documentation, capabilities and limitations). The \"auto-generate\" approach carries a risk of producing documents that are technically generated but substantively incomplete or lacking the expert judgment that regulators expect. However, as one component of a broader compliance architecture, it has clear value in reducing documentation gaps and improving consistency.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point: I'd approve this because it directly reduces a m