xisting tools into a unified layer rather than building from scratch. That's more feasible.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 specifically:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime. This addresses training-time logs, which is part of the broader record-keeping requirement mentioned in Recital 71 (\"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used\").\n- However, Article 12's specific requirements (paragraphs 2 and 3) focus on operational/inference-time logging: identifying risk situations, post-market monitoring, recording periods of use, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of verification persons. This proposal is primarily about TRAINING provenance, not operational logging.\n- So this addresses the Recital 71 broader documentation requirements but doesn't directly satisfy the specific Article 12(2)(a)(b)(c) or 12(3) requirements about runtime event logging.\n\n**From my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality):**\n- The technical approach is sound and buildable. It's not a moonshot.\n- It leverages existing tooling patterns, which makes it more viable.\n- For prototyping: if it's at the infrastructure layer, it shouldn't slow individual researchers down much after initial setup. This is good.\n- However, it's primarily addressing training provenance/documentation rather than the specific operational logging that Article 12(2) and 12(3) demand. It's a useful component but incomplete for full compliance.\n- The scope seems reasonable - not over-engineered, not under-scoped for what it addresses.\n- \"Eliminating tedious manual documentation burdens\" - from a prototyping speed perspective, this is valuable. If researchers spend time manually logging, that's time not spent experimenting.\n\nOverall assessment: Technically viable, well-scoped for what it does (training provenance), leverages existing patterns, and should actually improve prototyping velocity by removing ma