        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2554\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A unified SDK that bakes automatic event-recording into every prototype directly operationalises Article 12(1)'s 'shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events' mandate and standardises the data capture needed for traceability under Art. 12(2)(a)–(c); from an institutional-risk standpoint, a single audited logging layer dramatically reduces the probability that any high-risk system in our portfolio ships without conformant logs or with inconsistent schemas that would frustrate post-market monitoring (Art. 72) and operator obligations (Art. 26(5)). The main gaps I note are that the proposal frames capture around 'runtime' rather than explicitly addressing lifetime retention, and it does not yet specify how Annex III point-1(a) fields (reference-database identifier, match input data, verifier identity under Art. 14(5)) are encoded in the wrappers—these must be locked before implementation—but as a foundational compliance-infrastructure commitment the direction is clearly correct.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2554 from my perspective as Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is a \"Unified Compliance Logging SDK\" — a standardized software development kit providing pre-built functions for automatic event recording across all AI prototypes, with decorators and wrappers that capture timestamps, input data, and user IDs automatically during runtime. The stated goal is minimizing boilerplate code while preserving prototyping speed and guaranteeing data consistency.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Strengths (from a compliance perspective):**\n- Article 12(1) requires that high-risk AI systems \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified SDK directly addresses this technical requirement.\n- By making logging \"automatic\" and \"pre-built,\" it reduces the risk that individual researchers forget to implement logging, which would be a compliance failure.\n- Data consistency is critical for Article 12(2)'s traceability requirements — inconsistent logging formats across systems would make post-market monitoring (Art. 72) and operation monitoring (Art. 26(5)) difficult or impossible.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.), the SDK's capture of \"reference database\" and \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" could be built into the wrappers, addressing Article 12(3)(b) and (c).\n- Capturing user IDs addresses Article 12(3)(d) — identification of natural persons involved in verification.\n- Standardization across all prototypes means we don't have to audit each system's logging implementation individually; if the SDK is compliant, all systems built on it inherit that compliance. This is a powerful institutional risk-reduction strategy.\n\n**Weaknesses / Concerns (from a compliance perspective):**\n- The proposal says \"across all AI prototypes\" — not all AI systems are high-risk. Applying logging to non-high-risk systems might be over-engineering, but from a compliance standpoint, it's safer to log everything and then filter. However, the cost of building this for ALL prototypes when only some will be high-risk could be a resource drain.\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data, and user IDs\" — but Article 12(3) requires more specific things like \"the reference database against which input data has been checked.\" Does the SDK address these? It says \"pre-built functions for automatic event recording\" generally. The specificity matters.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is critical. A prototype-level SDK might not persist logs through deployment, maintenance, and decommissioning phases. The proposal focuses on \"runtime\" capture but doesn't explicitly address long-term retention.\n- The SDK needs to be maintained as regulations evolve, systems are modified (substantial modifications under Art. 79), etc. Who maintains it? What's the governance model?\n- \"Preserving prototyping speed\" — from a compliance perspective, I need to ensure that the SDK doesn't become a black box where researchers assume logging is happening but can't verify what exactly is being captured or whether it meets the specific Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) requirements for their particular system's intended purpose.\n\n**Compliance-specific assessment:**\n- This is fundamentally aligned with Article 12's requirement for \"automatic recording.\" The \"technically allow\" language means the system must have this capability built-in, not bolted on after the fact. A SDK approach ensures it's baked in from design.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having a unified compliance logging infrastructure signals to regulators and customers that we take traceability seriously as an institutional matter, not an ad-hoc per-project effort.\n- From an institutional survival standpoint, if we get caught with inconsistent or missing logs across our portfolio, the penalty exposure is significant (up to 7% of global turnover under Art. 99). A unified SDK reduces this tail risk.\n- However, I need to be careful: the SDK alone doesn't constitute compliance. We still need technical documentation per Recital 71 and Article 11/Annex IV. The SDK is a necessary but not sufficient component. But as a building block, it's highly valuable.\n\n**Resource commitment concern:**\nThe proposal commits us to developing an SDK — that's engineering time, ongoing maintenance, versioning, documentation of the SDK itself for auditors. It's a significant but bounded investment. Compared to having every research team build their own logging (inconsistently), this is far more efficient and far less risky from a compliance standpoint.\n\n**Decision:**\nThis clears my bar. It directly addresses a core technical requirement of Article 12, reduces institutional risk through standardization, supports market reputation by demonstrating systematic compliance infrastructure, and protects against the kind of \"we forgot to log X in project Y\" finding that could trigger enforcement action. The main caveat is ensuring it covers all of Article 12(3) specifics for Annex III systems and addresses lifetime retention, but as a foundational component, it's strongly aligned with my mandate.\n\nI'll approve this. Promise score: I'd say around 82-85. It's very well-aligned but has some gaps (lifetime retention isn't explicitly addressed, the specificity of what's captured for Annex III systems needs validation, governance/maintenance model is unclear). These are manageable but mean it's not a perfect 100 from my vantage point.\n",
