        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1242\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal frames Article 12 logging as a data source for exploratory academic research on model drift and silent failures, with its stated output being 'original scientific contributions' rather than implementation of any regulatory obligation. From my compliance vantage point, the binding duty under Art. 12 is to engineer automatic event-recording into high-risk systems at a level of traceability appropriate to intended purpose (Art. 12(2)) — this research project neither builds that capability nor directly verifies it meets the standard; it assumes logs already exist and are 'high-resolution,' which may be premature if our logging architecture is still in design. While the work could tangentially inform what events must be captured for post-market monitoring under Art. 72, its primary framing as a scientific publishing play means scarce implementation resources would be diverted from the actual compliance build-out without a clear regulatory return.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 35\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1242 suggests using Article 12 logging requirements as a basis for a \"research project\" on traceability-driven debugging — specifically investigating how mandated logs can detect emergent risks, model drift, and silent failures. It frames compliance logging as an opportunity for \"original scientific contributions regarding AI safety and reliability.\"\n\nLet me think about this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**What the regulation actually requires:**\n- Article 12(1): High-risk AI systems SHALL technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime. This is a mandatory technical requirement on the system itself.\n- Article 12(2): Logging capabilities must enable recording of events relevant to: (a) identifying risk situations or substantial modifications; (b) facilitating post-market monitoring under Art. 72; (c) monitoring operation under Art. 26(5).\n- Article 12(3): Specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics-related).\n\n**Assessment from compliance vantage:**\n\nThe proposal is essentially a research project that would analyze the logs we're already required to produce and develop \"new diagnostic methods.\" Let me think about whether this serves my function:\n\nPositives:\n- It acknowledges Article 12 logging as mandatory infrastructure. If we build robust logging anyway (which we must), having a research team look at how to exploit those logs for anomaly detection could strengthen our post-market monitoring capability under Art. 72, which is directly referenced in Art. 12(2)(b).\n- Better understanding of what the logs capture and don't capture could inform whether our logging implementation truly meets the \"appropriate traceability\" standard in Art. 12(2). If we discover gaps during this research, that's valuable for compliance.\n- Market reputation: Publishing research on AI safety/reliability derived from regulatory compliance infrastructure positions the lab as a thought leader, which helps institutional reputation.\n\nNegatives/Concerns:\n- The core obligation under Article 12 is to BUILD the logging capability into the system. This proposal doesn't directly address that engineering task. It's a derivative research project that assumes the logs exist and are high-quality. If we haven't built the logging infrastructure yet, this research is premature — there may not be adequate data to analyze.\n- The framing as \"original scientific contributions\" suggests this is more of an academic publishing play than a compliance implementation play. From my vantage point, I care about whether the system MEETS Article 12 requirements, not whether we can publish papers about them.\n- However — and this is important — if the research actually feeds back into improving our logging architecture (identifying what events need to be captured for meaningful risk detection), that IS compliance-relevant. The \"diagnostic methods\" could reveal gaps in what we're currently logging versus what's needed for Art. 12(2)(a) \"identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk.\"\n- There's a resource allocation concern: this is a research project, not an implementation project. My scarce resources are better spent ensuring Article 12 compliance is actually implemented rather than doing exploratory research on how to use logs we haven't fully built yet.\n\n**Key question for my bar:** Does this proposal directly advance regulatory compliance, institutional survival, or market reputation? \n\n- Regulatory compliance: Indirectly. It could inform better logging design, but it's not an implementation project. It's more of a \"nice to have\" research overlay on top of what must be done anyway.\n- Institutional survival: Minimal direct impact. The lab doesn't survive by publishing papers on log analysis; it survives by being compliant and having its systems approved for market.\n- Market reputation: Moderate positive. If published well, it shows the lab is at the frontier of AI safety practice.\n\n**My decision framework:** I approve proposals that clear my bar for advancing compliance, survival, or reputation. This proposal has a tangential connection to compliance (it uses compliance-mandated data) but doesn't directly implement any compliance obligation. It's more of an academic/reputation play. The risk is that it consumes resources that should go toward actually building the Article 12 logging infrastructure.\n\nHowever — I should be fair. If the lab MUST build the logs anyway, having a research team simultaneously work on how to make those logs maximally useful for risk detection could accelerate the compliance implementation by informing what events need to be captured. The \"new diagnostic methods\" language is concerning because it sounds exploratory rather than prescriptive.\n\nI think this sits in a middle zone. It's not harmful, it has some reputation upside, and it could provide useful feedback into logging design. But it's not something I would champion as a compliance officer — it doesn't directly implement an obligation, and the framing suggests it's more about scientific contribution than regulatory readiness. The \"converts a compliance requirement into original scientific contributions\" language makes me think this is primarily motivated by academic output rather than compliance robustness.\n\nI'll lean toward reject or low approve. Actually, let me reconsider: my bar is whether it clears from MY vantage point. Does it advance institutional survival? Marginally (reputation). Does it advance regulatory compliance? Only indirectly and speculatively — it might inform better logging, but that's not its stated purpose; its stated purpose is \"original scientific contributions.\" Does it protect market reputation? Modestly positive if published well.\n\nI think I'll reject this because:\n1. It doesn't directly implement any Article 12 obligation.\n2. Its stated goal is scientific contribution, not compliance implementation.\n3. The resources committed here are research resources that could be directed toward actually ensuring our logging meets Art. 12(2)'s \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" standard.\n4. The phrase \"high-resolution logs\" and \"new diagnostic methods\" suggests this assumes a level of logging sophistication we may not yet have, making it premature from a compliance standpoint.\n\nBut I'll give it a moderate promise score because it's not harmful and has some tangential value.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time. The instruction says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar.\" What IS my bar? My function is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation. Does this advance any of those three sufficiently to warrant committing scarce implementation resources?\n\n- It doesn't directly implement compliance (it's research, not engineering).\n- It doesn't protect institutional survival in any meaningful way.\n- It has modest positive effect on market reputation IF the research is published successfully, but that's speculative and depends on academic outcomes.\n\nI think the bar for me to approve is: does this materially advance our compliance posture or protect the institution from regulatory risk? This proposal doesn't clearly do that. It's a nice-to-have research overlay. I'll reject with a moderate promise score reflecting its tangential relevance.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise around 35-40.\n",
