geable.\n\n**Alignment with Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). This directly addresses that.\n- Article 12(2)(a)-(c) requires logging relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. A unified middleware can be designed to capture these event types.\n- Article 12(3) for biometric systems has more specific requirements (period of use, reference database, input data matching, identification of natural persons). The proposal as stated is more general-purpose but could accommodate these as configuration options.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n- \"Plug-and-play\" is a strong claim. In practice, integrating with diverse research stacks (PyTorch, TensorFlow, custom C++ inference engines, batch vs. streaming pipelines) will require project-specific work. The middleware handles the standard 80%, but the last 20% per project still needs effort.\n- \"Removes the burden of manual logging\" — good framing, and technically achievable for the standard cases. But edge cases in novel research won't be fully automated on day one.\n- The proposal is scoped to a \"small cross-functional team\" which is reasonable for an initial library, but ongoing maintenance across evolving projects will need sustained investment.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound and well-motivated engineering proposal. It addresses the core technical requirement of Article 12(1) in the most scalable way. From a prototyping speed perspective, having logging infrastructure ready BEFORE you start building new high-risk systems (or retrofitting early prototypes) saves significant rework later. The architecture is conventional enough to be low-risk from an engineering standpoint. The main risk is over-promising on \"plug-and-play\" universality, but the core value proposition — a shared, well-tested logging SDK that standardizes compliance capture — is very high-value and technically achievable.\n\nI'd say this clears m