ntensive for production deployment. A scoped investigation into whether adaptive sampling meets the minimum requirements of Article 12(3) (which specifies what MUST be recorded at a minimum) could save resources downstream. The proposal does mention \"strict EU traceability requirements\" as a constraint, which shows some regulatory awareness.\n\n6. **But the framing is wrong for my bar:** As Lab Director focused on resource efficiency, I need to ask: does this proposal have a clear, bounded deliverable that directly unblocks our compliance implementation? Or is it an open-ended \"scientific inquiry\"? The text says \"A scientific inquiry into maintaining high-fidelity event logs without incurring significant computational overhead.\" This sounds like it could go on indefinitely. There's no stated endpoint, no specific system to be instrumented, no deadline tied to the AI Act timeline.\n\n7. **The \"lossy compression\" angle is concerning:** Article 12 requires recording of *specific* events (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons). You can't apply lossy compression to legally required records without risking non-compliance. The proposal's framing of treating this as an optimization problem where you might sacrifice fidelity is in tension with the regulatory floor. This could waste resources exploring approaches that are ultimately non-viable from a compliance standpoint.\n\n**My assessment:** From my resource-allocation perspective, this proposal:\n- Has a vague scope (\"scientific inquiry\" without clear deliverables)\n- Risks misallocating research resources to what is largely an engineering implementation problem\n- The \"lossy compression\" framing conflicts with the minimum recording requirements in Article 12(3)\n- Doesn't clearly leverage our specific R&D infrastructure or domain expertise\n- Could be a useful sub-component within a broader compliance project but doesn't stand alone as justifiable use o