        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1175\",\n      \"rationale\": \"From a prototyping and engineering-viability standpoint, this proposal is problematic: differential privacy fundamentally conflicts with Article 12(3)(c)-(d) requirements to log the *exact* input data that matched and the *specific* natural person involved in verification — you cannot add calibrated noise to 'which biometric sample triggered a match' or 'who verified it' without voiding the traceability obligation. SMPC in a logging pipeline introduces enormous infrastructure complexity (synchronous multi-party coordination, latency, failure modes) for a problem that is largely solvable with standard engineering: encryption at rest, role-based access controls, pseudonymization where permissible, and data-retention policies. The proposal also presents DP and SMPC as interchangeable options without demonstrating which actually satisfies the letter of Annex III 1(a) logging mandates, suggesting the technical path is not yet worked out. As a committed implementation project this carries high schedule risk (multi-year protocol design + integration), high resource cost relative to simpler alternatives, and a real chance of producing an over-engineered artifact that regulators find unnecessary once Commission guidelines clarify access-tier expectations.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 15\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1175 is about \"Privacy-Preserving Event Logging Protocols\" — developing cryptographic methods (differential privacy, secure multi-party computation) within the logging pipeline for high-risk AI systems to balance traceability with data minimization under GDPR.\n\nLet me assess this from a development/prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Differential privacy and SMPC are well-established research areas, but integrating them into a real-time logging pipeline that must meet Article 12's specific requirements (recording exact input data matches, reference database references, start/end times, identification of natural persons) is extremely challenging.\n- The core tension: Article 12(3)(c) requires \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" and 12(3)(d) requires \"identification of the natural persons involved in the verification.\" Differential privacy adds noise, which fundamentally conflicts with recording exact inputs and identifying specific individuals. SMPC could theoretically help but introduces enormous latency and infrastructure complexity.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification), you need to log the actual input data that matched. Applying differential privacy to this means you're no longer logging the actual input — which defeats the purpose of traceability under Article 12(2)(a). This is a fundamental technical contradiction.\n- SMPC in a logging pipeline: You'd need all parties online simultaneously, significant computational overhead, and it doesn't really solve the problem because the regulator (or the system operator) still needs to be one party to query the log. It adds massive infrastructure complexity for marginal benefit.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a research-heavy project. Developing novel cryptographic protocols that satisfy both AI Act traceability requirements AND GDPR data minimization simultaneously is not a straightforward engineering task. The state of the art in privacy-preserving computation is still catching up to practical deployment at scale.\n- Prototyping SMPC systems is notoriously slow and complex — setting up secure enclaves, coordinating multiple parties, dealing with communication overhead. Differential privacy implementation for structured log data (which includes timestamps, database references, person identifiers) is non-trivial.\n- The proposal conflates two very different technical approaches (DP and SMPC) as if they're interchangeable options, suggesting the team hasn't deeply explored which would actually work given Article 12's specific requirements.\n- This could easily become a multi-year research project with uncertain outcome, rather than something that can be prototyped quickly and iteratively.\n\n**Key Concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. The proposal seems to solve a problem that may not exist in the way described. Article 12 requires logging, but it doesn't say the logs must be accessible to \"the public\" or all regulators simultaneously. Access controls, encryption at rest, and role-based access are standard engineering solutions that achieve data minimization without exotic cryptography.\n2. The specific requirements of Art 12(3) for biometric systems (exact input data, person identification) are fundamentally incompatible with differential privacy — you can't add noise to \"which specific person was identified\" and still satisfy the traceability requirement.\n3. SMPC adds enormous engineering complexity (infrastructure, latency, failure modes) for a problem that might be solvable with standard access control + encryption + audit trails.\n4. As a development engineer, I'd worry about prototyping speed: this is not something you can prototype in weeks or months. You need to design the protocol, implement it, test edge cases, validate compliance — easily 1-2+ years for a working system.\n5. The risk is that by the time this is built, the regulatory interpretation may have shifted (e.g., Commission guidelines clarifying what level of access regulators need), making the entire approach moot or over-engineered.\n\nHowever, there IS some merit to thinking about privacy in logging — you don't want raw biometric data sitting in unencrypted logs. But the solution proposed (DP/SMPC) seems over-engineered and technically misaligned with the actual requirements. A simpler engineering solution (encryption at rest, access controls, data retention policies, pseudonymization where legally permitted) would achieve most of the privacy benefit without the cryptographic complexity.\n\nMy decision: This is technically interesting as a research question but not viable as an implementation project given the constraints. The specific technical approaches proposed conflict with the letter of Article 12(3), and the prototyping timeline would be very long with uncertain success. I'd reject this from a \"commit scarce implementation resources\" standpoint — it's more of a research exploration than an engineering solution, and there are simpler engineering paths to achieve the privacy goal.\n\nPromise score: Maybe around 15-20. There's some validity in thinking about privacy in logging pipelines, but the specific technical approach is problematic, the prototyping would be slow, and it likely over-engineers a solvable problem.\n",
