ies only to Annex III point 1(a). This is a minor scope issue — implementing it more broadly than required isn't harmful from compliance perspective (it's over-compliance), but it could be a resource concern.\n- The \"lightweight authentication layer\" for user IDs — this is necessary because you can't just have someone type their name; you need verifiable identity linkage for the record to hold up in a regulatory audit or legal challenge. This shows the proposal understands that the record must actually prove WHO verified, not just what was logged.\n- \"Without disrupting the manual review workflow\" — from my perspective as Compliance Officer, this is good because if the logging burden causes reviewers to skip verification steps (to save time), then you have a compliance gap in practice even if the technical system exists. A frictionless integration reduces the risk of non-compliance in execution.\n\n**Institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- If a regulator comes asking for Article 12(3)(d) records and we don't have them, that's a direct violation with potential fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations). Having this in place is table stakes.\n- From market reputation: demonstrating robust human oversight documentation is also a selling point when B2B clients ask about AI governance.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a narrowly scoped, well-targeted compliance implementation that addresses a specific letter of the law (Art. 12(3)(d)). It's not over-engineered, it integrates into existing workflows, and the technical choices (time-stamped, read-only, authenticated) are appropriate for evidentiary value. From my compliance vantage point, this is exactly the kind of thing we need — a discrete, implementable piece that closes one specific regulatory gap. It doesn't claim to solve all of Article 12, but it solves its assigned slice correctly.\n\nThe only minor reservation: I'd want to confirm it's scoped specifically to Annex III 1(a) systems rather than blanket-applied (resource efficiency), and I'd want the \"centralized database\" to have proper access controls and retention policies aligned with data protection law (GDPR intersection). But these are implementation details, not reasons to reject the proposal.\n\nDecision: Approve. This directly serves my mandate of regulatory compliance and is a necessary component for any Annex III 1(a) high-risk system we deploy.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's clearly needed and well-conceived for its specific purpose, but it's one small piece of a larger logging architecture (Art. 12(3)(a)-(c) still need separate solutions), and the \"centralized database\" approach has some operational risks I'd want to manage. It's not a transformative compliance initiative on its own, but it's a necessary, well-scoped brick in the wall.\n",
