dditional bureaucratic layer. Researchers would need to have their projects vetted by this board before knowing what logging requirements apply. This could slow down research iteration, create friction in the R&D pipeline. The concern is whether this administrative overhead might stifle exploratory research or create chilling effects on researchers who aren't sure if their work will be classified as \"high-risk\" and thus require extensive documentation upfront.\n\n4. **Is it even my job to evaluate this?** My domain is knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact. This proposal is fundamentally a regulatory compliance mechanism. It doesn't generate new knowledge, doesn't have intellectual merit in the research sense, and its \"impact\" on science is at best indirect (resource allocation) or at worst negative (bureaucratic friction on research).\n\n5. **Counter-argument**: One could argue that by clearly delineating what requires logging vs. what doesn't, researchers gain certainty and can focus their energy appropriately. Knowing you DON'T need to build elaborate logging infrastructure for your exploratory work is itself a form of enabling research freedom. But this is a stretch - the absence of a requirement is already established by law; what's being proposed is an internal mechanism to determine applicability.\n\n6. **Intellectual merit assessment**: The proposal has essentially zero intellectual merit from a research perspective. It's an administrative process design. There's no algorithmic innovation, no new methodology for scientific discovery, no advance in understanding AI systems.\n\n7. **Broader scientific impact**: The most generous reading is that it prevents resource misallocation. But the actual benefit is marginal - the legal text already defines what's high-risk. An internal classification board is a practical necessity but doesn't create scientific value.\n\nMy assessment: From my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal has very little to offer. It's an administrative/compliance mechanism. It might have minor indirect positive effects on research by preventing resource waste, but it introduces potential bureaucratic friction. It doesn't advance knowledge, has no inherent intellectual merit in the research sense, and its scientific impact is negligible or at best marginally positive through resource preservation.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: rejecting a proposal that prevents \"wasteful application of expensive record-keeping infrastructure to low-risk research\" could be seen as ignoring a real concern about research resources being consumed by compliance overhead. But the solution proposed (a review board doing classification) is not something that would come from or advance the knowledge frontier.\n\nDecision: I'll lean toward reject, with a moderate promise score reflecting that while it's not my domain and doesn't advance science, it at least acknowledges a real resource-allocation concern that indirectly affects research capacity. But it fundamentally isn't a scientific contribution.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with maybe a 15-20 promise score. It has some indirect relevance to preserving research resources but is fundamentally an administrative mechanism with no intellectual merit or knowledge-frontier advancement.\n",
