ld become bureaucratic overhead if not kept lightweight. If project leads spend more time classifying than doing research, that's counterproductive.\n- Classification isn't always clean-cut — some systems might be borderline or evolve from non-high-risk to high-risk as their intended purpose shifts. The tool needs to account for re-classification over the project lifecycle.\n- \"Digital tagging process\" implies new software infrastructure. If we already have project management tools (Jira, Confluence, etc.), can we extend those rather than building a separate system? Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure matters to me.\n- The proposal says \"project leads use to flag their systems\" — this puts the classification burden on researchers who may not be regulatory experts. Risk of misclassification in either direction.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is squarely within my domain and directly serves resource-allocation efficiency. It's a gating mechanism that prevents the lab from deploying expensive logging infrastructure unnecessarily. The cost-benefit ratio is favorable: a relatively cheap classification tool saves potentially millions in unnecessary engineering work. However, I need to be careful about implementation scope — it should be lightweight, integrated into existing project management workflows, and include periodic re-assessment as projects evolve.\n\nI would approve this. It's high-value, low-cost (relative to the logging systems it gates), and directly addresses my core concern of not wasting scarce resources. The promise score should be high but not perfect — I have some concerns about implementation bloat and classification accuracy, but the fundamental value proposition is strong from my vantage point.\n\nI'd give this around 82-85. It's very aligned with my mandate, but there are execution risks that keep it from being a perfect 100.\n",
