at it can be configured per-project to meet specific traceability requirements. If it's too rigid, projects might not capture what's needed; if teams bypass or misconfigure it, we have gaps.\n  - There's no mention of data retention periods, access controls, audit trails for the logs themselves, or how the logging module integrates with the broader technical documentation (Annex IV) and post-market monitoring system (Art. 72).\n  - The proposal focuses on engineering efficiency (\"prevents individual research teams from wasting limited resources\") which is a valid operational concern but the framing doesn't explicitly address compliance verification — i.e., how do we *verify* that the module is correctly capturing what Article 12 requires for each specific system?\n\nFrom institutional survival and market reputation:\n- Having a single, auditable logging infrastructure is actually a strength in regulatory audits. A regulator reviewing our systems would find it easier to verify compliance if there's one consistent mechanism rather than N different ad-hoc implementations. This reduces the risk of a \"missing log\" finding that could trigger enforcement.\n- It also protects market reputation: if we can demonstrate systematic, enterprise-wide logging infrastructure, it signals organizational maturity to both regulators and commercial partners.\n\nFrom the \"committing scarce implementation resources\" angle:\n- Building this once vs. building it N times is clearly more resource-efficient. The compliance risk of having inconsistent or missing logging across projects is real and could lead to enforcement action under Articles 99-101 (fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, though for non-compliance with Article 12 it would fall under general penalties).\n- However, the risk is if the module is built incompletely and we discover gaps during an audit. The proposal doesn't mention a compliance validation layer or how it will be tested against the