w hardware - good for funding constraints.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Building on the shared compute cluster is smart - no new hardware procurement needed.\n- A standardized API means all projects plug into one system, reducing infrastructure sprawl.\n- The risk: if research teams need non-standard logging (e.g., for novel experimental setups not yet anticipated), a rigid central library could become constraining and force workarounds or delays. The \"modular\" qualifier helps but doesn't eliminate this concern.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Small team of DevOps engineers\" - what happens when they're done? Is there maintenance overhead forever? Who owns updates when regulations change or new system types emerge?\n- Standardization can sometimes stifle the flexibility needed in R&D where experiments evolve rapidly. But for compliance logging, which is a fixed requirement, standardization makes more sense than for research code itself.\n- The proposal frames it as preventing \"wasteful\" per-team builds, which from my efficiency lens is exactly right. The alternative (each team building their own) would be strictly less efficient use of our engineering resources.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is clearly the right architecture decision. Compliance logging is a uniform requirement across all high-risk systems; there's little reason for each team to solve it independently. Centralizing saves engineering hours, reduces maintenance surface area (one codebase vs. N), and leverages existing compute infrastructure. The main risk is that the \"small team\" becomes an ongoing fixed cost that might be harder to justify as projects come and go, but the per-team alternative would be worse on almost every metric I care about.\n\nThe promise score: This aligns very well with my domain concerns. It's efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, reduces waste, and addresses a mandatory requirement in