tomatic recording of events during use).\n\nArticle 12(1) says: \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is about runtime event logging.\n\nArticle 12(2)(a)-(c) are about specific operational events: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation.\n\nThe proposal's \"metadata layer using graph databases to map lineage from raw data through the entire lifecycle to final inference events\" touches on the boundary — it mentions \"final inference events\" which could connect to operational logging, but the primary focus is clearly on training provenance and documentation.\n\n**Assessment:**\n\nPros:\n- Addresses Recital 71's requirement for technical documentation (data, training, testing, validation processes)\n- Creates a chain of evidence that supports traceability\n- \"Kept up to date\" through automatic linking\n- Supports compliance with the broader record-keeping framework\n- A graph database approach is reasonable for lineage tracking\n- Could support demonstrating that systems are \"kept up to date appropriately throughout the lifetime\"\n\nCons:\n- The primary focus is on *development-time* provenance, not *operational logging* as Article 12 specifically requires. Article 12(1) mandates automatic recording of events over the system's operational lifetime — this is about what happens at inference time, in production, during use.\n- \"Total scientific reproducibility\" is a nice goal but goes beyond what the Act strictly requires and could be resource-intensive without directly mapping to compliance obligations\n- The proposal doesn't clearly address Article 12(3) minimum requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (start/end times of use, reference database, input data matches, identification of verifying persons)\n- Doesn't explicitly address the risk-identification logging in Art. 12(2)(a) or post-market monitoring facilitation in Art