ompliance obligation in any concrete way\n5. It commits scarce implementation resources to an exploratory research project when we need actual logging infrastructure built and operational\n6. There's no clear path from \"researching how to narrativize logs\" to \"demonstrating Article 12 compliance to a regulator\"\n\nHowever, let me think about whether there's any legitimate compliance value:\n- Better understanding of our logs could inform what we log (point 2 of Article 12)\n- Causal inference on logs could help identify risk situations (Article 79(1))\n- Post-market monitoring (Article 72) could benefit from better log analysis\n\nBut these are speculative. The proposal is explicitly a \"research project investigating how to\" do things, not an implementation plan. It's framed around publication and scientific contribution rather than compliance readiness. A regulator would not accept \"we published a paper on narrative logging\" as evidence of Article 12 compliance. The requirement is that the system *technically allows* for automatic recording - it's an engineering/implementation obligation, not a research question.\n\nFrom institutional survival perspective: committing resources to a publication-oriented research project when we need working logging infrastructure in production systems is misaligned with the regulatory deadline and obligation.\n\nFrom market reputation: publishing at top-tier conferences has some reputation value, but if it delays actual compliance implementation, the reputational risk of non-compliance far outweighs conference prestige. Also, \"cognitive traceability\" is an overblown claim that a regulator might see as the institution not understanding what's actually being asked.\n\nFrom regulatory compliance: This does nothing to implement Article 12. It's tangential at best. The proposal doesn't specify how it maps to specific logging requirements in points 2(a), 2(b), 2(c), or point 3(a-d).\n\nMy decision: REJECT. This is a research exp