        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2592\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises Article 12(1)'s mandate for automatic event recording over the system lifetime by embedding logging at the infrastructure layer rather than leaving it to per-project discretion, which is precisely where compliance risk compounds if individual researchers treat it as optional. From an institutional-survival standpoint, a single maintained middleware gives us one coherent audit trail across every high-risk deployment, eliminates the 'I forgot to configure logs on project X' failure mode, and presents regulators with a defensible, consistent architecture rather than a patchwork of ad-hoc scripts that vary in fidelity and retention.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2592 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P2592: Unified Logging Infrastructure Layer**\n\nThis proposes building a standardized middleware framework that automatically captures and stores system events across all high-risk AI projects. Key features:\n- Standardized middleware for automatic event recording\n- Baseline level of event recording without manual setup by researchers\n- Shared API abstracting logging requirements\n- Integration into existing GPU compute clusters and data lakes\n- Maintained as a core service by DevOps team\n- Minimizes redundant engineering on individual scientists\n- Ensures total regulatory consistency across the lab\n\n**From my Compliance Officer perspective:**\n\nStrengths:\n1. Article 12(1) mandates that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified infrastructure layer directly addresses this by making logging a default, not an opt-in per project. This is exactly what the regulation demands — automatic recording over the lifetime.\n\n2. Article 12(2) requires traceability \"appropriate to the intended purpose of the system\" for specific purposes (risk identification, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring). A centralized framework ensures consistent logging across all systems, which supports this traceability requirement uniformly.\n\n3. Recital 71 emphasizes that records must be kept \"throughout the lifetime of the AI system\" and that high-risk systems should \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" A unified infrastructure that is maintained as a core service ensures longevity — it doesn't die when one researcher leaves or a project winds down.\n\n4. From an institutional survival perspective: if we get audited by a notified body or market surveillance authority, having a single, consistent logging architecture across all projects dramatically reduces compliance risk. Inconsistency between projects is a red flag for regulators.\n\n5. Market reputation: demonstrating a systematic, institution-wide approach to record-keeping signals maturity and seriousness to partners, clients, and regulators. It shows we've embedded compliance into our engineering fabric rather than bolting it on per-project.\n\n6. The \"without requiring manual setup by the researcher\" aspect is critical — researchers are not compliance officers. If logging depends on individual researchers remembering or choosing to implement it, we're gambling on human behavior. Automation at the infrastructure level removes that risk.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n1. \"Baseline level of event recording\" — Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.) requires specific minimum fields. A baseline might not capture all those specifics. However, the proposal says it's a framework with a shared API that abstracts requirements — presumably configurable per project type.\n\n2. The scope is large — building and maintaining a middleware layer across all GPU clusters and data lakes is a significant engineering investment. But from compliance perspective, this is exactly where we want to invest: in systemic infrastructure rather than piecemeal fixes.\n\n3. \"Total regulatory consistency\" — this is actually a feature, not a bug, from my vantage point. Regulators want consistency in how records are kept and maintained.\n\n4. One concern: the proposal frames it as DevOps maintaining it as a \"core service.\" This is good for longevity (lifetime requirement) but I'd want to ensure there's a clear ownership model for log retention periods, access controls, integrity verification, etc. But these are implementation details that don't change my overall assessment.\n\n5. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention the specific fields in Article 12(3) for biometric systems, but as an infrastructure layer with configurable APIs, it should be able to accommodate those. It's a framework, not a fixed schema.\n\nOverall assessment: This is strongly aligned with my compliance mandate. It addresses the core requirement of Article 12 (automatic recording over lifetime), provides institutional consistency (critical for audit readiness), removes dependency on individual researcher behavior (reducing risk of gaps), and creates a defensible posture against regulators. The investment is significant but it's exactly the kind of systemic compliance infrastructure that protects institutional survival.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high — from my vantage point, this is one of the most directly compliance-relevant proposals I could expect. It addresses the \"how\" of Article 12 at the architectural level. I'd give it around 85-90. It's not a perfect 100 because:\n- It needs to be verified that the \"baseline\" actually meets all specific requirements in Article 12(3) for different system categories\n- The lifetime requirement means this infrastructure itself needs governance (who updates it as regulations evolve?)\n- Integration with data lakes raises questions about log integrity, immutability, and retention\n\nBut these are refinements, not deal-breakers. I'll give it 87.\n",
