am can ship their high-risk AI system. If the middleware isn't ready, no team can proceed. This creates a critical path dependency.\n- \"Software engineers will integrate this middleware into every AI pipeline\" - this is a significant integration burden on already-scrambled prototyping teams. Every new model architecture, every experiment, every rapid iteration cycle now has to go through a compliance logging layer first.\n- However, once built, it DOES eliminate duplicated effort across projects. If we have 5 high-risk projects each building their own logging from scratch, a shared framework saves time in aggregate.\n- The risk: if the \"standardized API\" is too rigid or too complex to integrate, it will slow down prototyping significantly. If it's too thin, it won't capture what Article 12 actually requires (especially the specific items in paragraph 3).\n- For rapid prototyping cycles, having logging \"by default\" is good - you don't forget compliance requirements at the end. But if the middleware adds latency or complexity to every experiment iteration, it hurts speed.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound and necessary piece of infrastructure. The EU AI Act mandates this capability (Article 12(1): \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events\"). Without some form of standardized approach, we'd be reinventing logging in every project. The key risk from my vantage point is execution: if this becomes a massive upfront build that blocks all prototyping teams, it's counterproductive. But as a concept - shared middleware for compliance-relevant event capture - it's the right engineering approach.\n\nThe proposal is reasonable but somewhat high-level. It doesn't address:\n- How to handle the specific Article 12(3) requirements (which are domain-specific)\n- The integration cost per project\n- Latency/overhead concerns during prototyping\n- How \"internal system states\" maps across fundamentally different model architectures\n\n