acity. I need to consider whether this is better than each project handling it ad hoc (probably not at scale) or buying off-the-shelf (maybe, but the proposal says integrate into OUR base pipeline).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Explicitly integrates into \"the lab's base ML pipeline\" - this means we're not building parallel infrastructure. We're extending what already exists. Very efficient.\n- The fact that it's a library/API rather than a separate system means minimal architectural disruption to ongoing research projects.\n- Centralized approach means updates (e.g., when the EU AI Act is amended or guidance evolves) only need to happen in one place, not across N individual project codebases.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The systems team's capacity: if we commit them to building this middleware, what does that mean for other infrastructure work? But given that EVERY high-risk project needs this, the ROI is clear - it pays for itself by avoiding N separate implementations.\n- \"Standardized\" and \"plug-and-play\" are somewhat idealistic - real research projects often have unusual architectures. However, as a framework with reasonable defaults and some customization hooks, this should be manageable.\n- The upfront investment is significant but bounded (one team, one library), whereas the alternative (each project doing it independently) has unbounded total cost that scales with number of projects.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is exactly the kind of proposal I'd want to see from an infrastructure efficiency perspective. It's a single, bounded investment that eliminates a recurring compliance burden across the entire portfolio. It leverages existing pipeline architecture rather than creating new parallel systems. It protects researcher time for actual scientific work. The risk is manageable - it's a well-understood software pattern (middleware/interceptor) and the requirements are clearly specified in Article 12.\n