estions about what constitutes \"comprehensible information\" for traceability (as Recital 71 mentions), or how to automatically determine which documentation needs updating given arbitrary architectural changes, but the proposal as stated doesn't engage with those at a research depth. It just says \"automatically triggers documentation updates.\"\n\n**Broader scientific impact:** Would this advance the field of AI research? Minimally, if at all. A well-built internal documentation system is good practice and might be shareable as open-source tooling, but it doesn't change how we understand or develop AI systems. It doesn't produce new methods, benchmarks, theoretical insights, or empirical findings that would influence other researchers. Its impact is confined to regulatory compliance within the lab (and perhaps similar labs).\n\nHowever, I should also consider: does this have any indirect research value? One could argue that good documentation practices enable reproducibility, which underpins scientific rigor. And if the system captures rich metadata about training data, validation results, and model architectures over time, it could in principle become a dataset of \"what worked and what didn't\" — but the proposal doesn't frame or design for this. It's explicitly positioned as an auditor-facing compliance artifact (\"single source of truth for auditors\").\n\nAlso worth noting: the proposal is essentially a non-research activity. The Researcher function should be screening whether this advances knowledge, contributes intellectually to the field, or has broader scientific impact. A documentation automation system for regulatory compliance is important operationally (the Legal/Compliance or Engineering functions would care deeply), but from my vantage point, it scores low on all three criteria I'm responsible for.\n\nI'll reject it, but not at 0 — there's a tiny sliver of indirect value (reproducibility infrastructure, potential to capture longitudinal model perfo