on can be a risk for prototyping speed (single point of failure, coupling), but the framing here seems to be a library/middleware layer that projects integrate into, not necessarily a single monolithic service. That's more appropriate.\n- Quality: Standardized logging means consistent data formats, which makes post-market monitoring (Art 72) and traceability much easier. From a prototyping quality standpoint, if the logging is built in from day one via the framework, you're less likely to have gaps that get discovered late.\n- Concerns: The proposal is somewhat high-level. \"Middleware layer that automatically captures system inputs, outputs, and user interactions\" — for ML/AI systems specifically, what does \"input data\" mean? Training data? Inference-time features? Model parameters? The Annex III specific requirements (point 3) are very specific about biometric identification logging (reference databases, match data, verifier identity). A generic framework needs to be extensible enough to handle domain-specific logging without becoming a kitchen-sink mess.\n- Speed concern: Building this framework is itself a multi-month engineering effort. During that time, projects can't proceed with full compliance logging. But the alternative — each project building its own — is worse in aggregate.\n\nOverall assessment: This is technically viable, addresses a core regulatory requirement directly, and from a development perspective, having standardized infrastructure for mandatory compliance features is a net positive for prototyping speed (you don't have to figure out logging architecture every time you start a new high-risk project). The main risk is execution complexity and ensuring the framework doesn't become a bottleneck. But as a proposal to build it, it's sound engineering practice.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly from my vantage point — maybe 78-82. It's clearly technically feasible, directly addresses Article 12(1) and (2), and improves prototyping velocity for high