        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1962 is about \"Semantic Logging for Enhanced Model Interpretability\" - developing methods to map internal latent space transitions to human-readable log entries during system operation, using auxiliary models to describe reasoning paths of primary high-risk AI systems.\n\nLet me assess this from a technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n1. \"Mapping internal latent space transitions to human-readable log entries\" - This is an extremely open research problem. There's no established, reliable method for doing this in production systems. The interpretability of transformer-based or other deep learning models' latent spaces is an active and largely unsolved research area. Training auxiliary models (like probes or VAEs) to describe latent transitions is at best a partial solution and highly model-dependent.\n\n2. \"Training auxiliary models to describe the reasoning paths\" - This adds significant computational overhead, introduces another failure mode (the auxiliary model could be wrong), and creates a chicken-and-egg problem: you need to understand the system well enough to build an interpreter of it. For novel or evolving architectures, this becomes intractable.\n\n3. Real-time requirement: Article 12(1) says logs must be recorded \"over the lifetime of the system\" - implying they happen during operation. Adding semantic interpretation at inference time adds latency and complexity that's hard to make reliable in production.\n\n4. The proposal explicitly frames itself as a \"research project\" aiming to \"advance the knowledge frontier.\" This is not a prototyping-ready solution. It's exploratory R&D with no clear path to a working, testable prototype in a reasonable timeframe.\n\n5. The phrase \"exceeding the basic requirements of Recital 71\" suggests this isn't even targeting compliance - it's going beyond what's needed. From a resource allocation standpoint, this is even harder to justify when you need to commit scarce implementation resources.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- No clear prototype boundary: What would a \"minimum viable\" version look like? The problem space is too open.\n- High risk of getting stuck in research without producing deployable artifacts.\n- The auxiliary model approach introduces additional training, validation, and maintenance burden.\n- No clear success criteria that map to Article 12 requirements - the regulation asks for specific logged events (start/end times, reference databases, input data, person identification), not semantic explanations of why states were reached.\n\n**Does it even address Article 12?**\nArticle 12 is about automatic recording of specific events (logs). It's a fairly concrete technical requirement: log start/end times, log which database was queried, log input data matches, identify verification personnel. The \"semantic logging\" approach is over-engineering for what is essentially an instrumentation/logging infrastructure problem. You don't need to interpret latent space transitions to know when the system started and stopped running, or what inputs it processed.\n\nHowever, I should note that for point 2(a) - \"identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk\" - there might be *some* value in understanding why certain outputs were produced. But even then, you could achieve this with simpler techniques (confidence scores, input/output logging, feature importance if applicable) without needing to fully interpret latent space transitions.\n\n**My verdict:**\nFrom a development engineering perspective focused on technical viability and prototyping speed:\n- This is fundamentally a research problem, not an engineering solution I can prototype quickly.\n- The technical path is unclear and the success criteria are vague.\n- It over-engineers what Article 12 actually requires (concrete event logging).\n- Committing implementation resources to this would tie up engineers in open-ended research with no guaranteed deliverable.\n- There's no clear MVP or incremental delivery path.\n\nThis should be rejected. The promise score should reflect that while the idea has some intellectual appeal, it's not viable as a committed engineering effort from my vantage point. I'd give it maybe 15-20 - there's something interesting in the space, but it's nowhere near ready for resource commitment and doesn't map well to what Article 12 actually demands technically.\n",
