osal has genuine value for compliance — data lineage, version control, and structured metadata all support the traceability goals of Article 12 and Recital 71. However, it has a significant gap: the reliance on researcher-initiated tagging (API hooks) conflicts with the \"automatic recording\" mandate. Also, it doesn't directly address the specific minimum logging requirements in Article 12(3). It's more of a technical documentation enhancement than an Article 12 compliance solution per se.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- The structured metadata and data lineage aspects are valuable for compliance audits\n- Version control linking checkpoints to datasets is useful for post-market monitoring (Art 12(2)(b))\n- But the \"researcher tags milestones\" element is a red flag from a compliance standpoint — it introduces human dependency into what should be automatic processes\n- The proposal doesn't fully cover the Article 12(3) minimums for biometric systems\n- It's a useful complement but not sufficient on its own\n\nI'd say this is moderately promising. It addresses real needs (traceability, documentation structure) but has compliance gaps (automatic vs manual, missing specific Art 12(3) elements). I'd approve it as a component of the broader compliance architecture, but with the caveat that it's not sufficient alone. Actually, let me reconsider — my job is to screen whether this clears my bar from MY vantage point.\n\nThe proposal does contribute meaningfully to:\n- Traceability (Recital 71 core purpose)\n- Technical documentation currency (kept up to date throughout lifetime)\n- Post-market monitoring facilitation (Art 12(2)(b))\n\nBut it has issues:\n- The manual tagging element undermines the \"automatic\" requirement\n- It doesn't address the specific Art 12(3) minimums\n- It's framed in scientific terms rather than regulatory audit terms\n- No mention of log integrity, retention, or access controls\n\nI think I'd approve this as it does contribute to compliance goals,