        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2830 is about establishing a \"High-Risk System Classification Protocol\" - a formal internal review process with a quarterly review board (legal counsel + senior scientists) to determine which R&D projects fall under the high-risk category, serving as a gateway for resource allocation for logging resources.\n\nFrom my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**What it does well:**\n- It's a sensible governance step - not building logging infrastructure for every project is technically smart. You don't want to bloat every prototype with compliance overhead if it doesn't need to be there.\n- Resource allocation focus aligns with practical engineering concerns.\n\n**Problems from my vantage point:**\n\n1. **Quarterly cadence is too slow for R&D prototyping.** In an R&D lab, projects evolve rapidly. A project that starts as a low-risk exploratory prototype might pivot into a high-risk use case within weeks. A quarterly review board means I could be building for 3 months without knowing whether I need to architect logging in from day one. This creates rework - the worst enemy of prototyping speed and quality.\n\n2. **The \"gateway\" framing is problematic.** It positions this as a gate before resources are allocated. From a prototyping perspective, I need to know early (ideally at project inception) whether logging architecture needs to be built in. If classification only happens quarterly, I either:\n   - Build everything with full logging capability (expensive, slows prototyping), or\n   - Build without it and then retrofit later (rework, quality degradation).\n\n3. **Legal counsel + senior scientists is not a technical engineering review.** The board composition doesn't include the people who actually need to implement logging architectures. From my perspective, the decision about what \"technically allow for automatic recording\" means in practice should involve engineers who understand the system architecture. A legal/scientific board might make a classification decision that's technically unworkable or misses implementation nuances.\n\n4. **\"Recorded in central management system\"** - This is vague and doesn't address the actual technical challenge. The hard part isn't deciding \"is this high-risk?\" (that's somewhat binary once you understand Annex III). The hard part is: given a specific project, what logging architecture do we build? What events are \"relevant\"? How does that interact with our prototyping stack?\n\n5. **It doesn't actually solve the technical problem.** Article 12 requires systems to \"technically allow for automatic recording.\" A classification board tells you WHETHER you need it, but says nothing about HOW you implement it technically. From my perspective as a dev engineer, this proposal addresses governance/administration, not technical viability of logging solutions.\n\n6. **Risk of being wrong and expensive.** If the quarterly board misclassifies (either way), the cost is high. Misclassify upward: wasted engineering resources building unnecessary compliance infrastructure into prototypes, slowing iteration. Misclassify downward: you're non-compliant and face regulatory penalty, plus massive retrofit costs.\n\n**However**, there IS value here from my perspective:\n- Not over-engineering every prototype with full logging is technically sound\n- Having a clear signal about which projects need logging architecture helps me plan prototyping timelines\n- Avoiding \"compliance tax\" on every exploratory project protects speed of iteration\n\nBut the quarterly cadence and the composition of the board are significant issues for my domain. The proposal is more of an administrative/governance mechanism than a technical enabler. It doesn't give me what I need: early, clear, technically-informed guidance that lets me architect logging appropriately from day one without slowing down prototyping.\n\nI'd lean toward reject or low promise. It's not harmful per se, but it's misaligned with my domain concerns. The quarterly cadence is the killer - it's incompatible with R&D iteration speed. And it doesn't address the actual technical implementation challenge. I'd say maybe a 25-30 promise score. It has some indirect value (resource allocation) but the execution details (quarterly, wrong composition, no technical content) make it poorly suited to my vantage point.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Is this proposal something that would help or hinder my work? \n\nIf I have to wait a quarter for classification before I can build logging in, that's bad. But the proposal says \"ensures we only allocate expensive logging resources to projects that actually require them legally.\" From a prototyping speed perspective, NOT building logging into every project IS important - it keeps prototypes lean and fast. So there's genuine value in not over-engineering.\n\nBut the mechanism (quarterly board) is wrong for R&D speed. A better approach would be: classify at project inception with a lightweight technical assessment, and have a trigger-based re-evaluation if the project scope changes. Not quarterly.\n\nI'll go with reject, promise around 25. It addresses a real concern (don't build logging everywhere) but the mechanism is poorly designed for R&D prototyping speed and quality.\n",
