's a preventive infrastructure investment rather than a reactive one. Building it once at the framework level is cheaper than retrofitting compliance into each project individually.\n- The \"across all R&D projects\" scope means it scales — as you take on more high-risk AI work, the marginal cost of compliance stays near zero.\n\nEffective use of existing R&D infrastructure:\n- The middleware approach wraps around existing pipelines rather than replacing them. This is good.\n- It creates a single point of truth for traceability across projects, which reduces fragmentation and duplicated logging efforts.\n- Risk: if the wrapper becomes a bottleneck or single point of failure, it could actually slow down experiments. But that's an implementation risk, not a conceptual flaw.\n\nPotential concerns from my vantage point:\n- The scope \"across all R&D projects\" is ambitious. Do we have the engineering capacity to build and maintain this? It's a significant ongoing maintenance burden.\n- \"Every experiment is inherently compliant\" — this overstates what middleware can do. Compliance also requires policy decisions about what constitutes relevant events, retention periods, etc. The technical logging is necessary but not sufficient.\n- However, the core idea — automated, structured logging as infrastructure rather than per-project ad-hoc effort — is sound and resource-efficient.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my perspective. It's a force multiplier for compliance that reduces ongoing operational costs. The middleware approach respects existing infrastructure. The key risk is upfront engineering investment and ongoing maintenance, but the long-term efficiency gains are clear. I'd approve this with high confidence.\n\nDecision: Approve\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my domain concerns. It's efficient, scalable, uses existing infrastructure well, and protects researcher time. I'd say around 82-85. The main deduction is that \"every experi