he other hand, if the certification process is slow or unclear in its criteria, it creates uncertainty and delays that harm prototyping velocity.\n\n4. **Practical concerns**:\n   - Annex III classification can be ambiguous for novel AI systems — a review board might take time to make determinations on edge cases\n   - For an R&D lab, many projects are exploratory/prototypical at the start — their \"intended purpose\" may not be fully defined yet, making Annex III evaluation premature or difficult\n   - The proposal says \"during the initial design phase\" but for prototyping, you often need to build to understand what you're building\n\n5. **Positive aspects from my angle**: \n   - If we know early it's high-risk, we can architect logging capabilities (Article 12) into the system from the start rather than bolting them on later\n   - Prevents wasting engineering resources adding full compliance infrastructure to systems that don't need it\n   - The \"over-engineering low-risk systems\" concern is valid — if every prototype gets full Article 12 logging treatment, we waste cycles\n\n6. **Negative aspects from my angle**:\n   - Adds a serial dependency before prototyping can begin\n   - For an R&D institution where projects evolve rapidly, a static \"certification\" at design phase may become stale\n   - The process itself (board meetings, documentation for certification) consumes time that could be spent on actual development\n   - Doesn't address the technical \"how\" of implementation at all\n\nMy assessment: This is primarily a governance/process proposal. From my development engineering vantage point focused on speed and quality of prototyping, it introduces friction into the project initiation pipeline. However, it does have a legitimate upside for architectural planning (knowing early whether you need full logging infrastructure). The key risk is that the \"certification before funding\" gate slows down prototyping cycles.\n\nI think this is borderline. It's not technically non-viable — it's implementable as a process. But from my specific vantage point of speed/quality of prototyping, it adds overhead without directly contributing to technical output. The upside (architectural planning) is real but could be achieved with lighter-weight mechanisms (e.g., a simple classification tag in the project brief rather than a full \"certification board\").\n\nI'll lean toward reject or low-approve. Let me think more carefully...\n\nActually, re-reading: \"formal risk certification before receiving internal funding\" — this means NO prototyping happens until the board clears it. For an R&D lab where speed matters, this is a significant gate. But it's also true that if you start building and then discover mid-project that it's high-risk, retrofitting logging capabilities is more expensive than designing them in from the start.\n\nI think the right call from my vantage point is to reject or give very low promise. The proposal addresses a real need (early classification) but its mechanism (board + certification gate before funding) is heavy and will slow prototyping. A lighter-weight approach (e.g., a simple self-assessment checklist completed during project scoping, with a fast-track for clear cases) would serve the same purpose without the bureaucratic overhead. The proposal as written prioritizes compliance certainty over engineering velocity.\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise: I'll give it maybe 30-35. There's value in early classification from an architecture standpoint, but the mechanism proposed will impede prototyping speed. It's not a terrible idea (hence not 10), but as written it adds a serial gate that my function would find painful to work through repeatedly.\n",
