s pragmatic and sustainable\n- However, if it's marketed internally as \"baseline,\" that could be a reputational issue with auditors\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a solid foundational proposal. It addresses the core mechanical requirement of Art 12(1) in a scalable way. The immutable repository and standardized middleware approach are exactly what a compliance officer would want to see — it removes individual researcher discretion from logging, which is where compliance typically breaks down in R&D environments.\n\nHowever, calling it \"baseline\" and not explicitly addressing the specific sub-requirements of Art 12(2) and 12(3) (especially for Annex III systems) means it's necessary but not sufficient on its own. It's a good first layer that I would approve as a commitment to implement, understanding that additional configuration/specifics will be needed per system category.\n\nThe risk of NOT approving: without this unified infrastructure, each research team might build ad-hoc logging (or worse, no logging), creating enormous compliance exposure across the institution. The standardization value alone justifies approval from a survival standpoint.\n\nI'll approve it. Promise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's strong and addresses the core requirement well, but the \"baseline\" framing and gaps in specific Art 12(2)/(3) coverage mean it's not a complete compliance solution by itself. It's a necessary foundation that I would definitely back. Let me settle on 75.\n",
