nsion between universal applicability and specific regulatory requirements.\n\n**Speed of prototyping impact:** Mixed. If built well as a thin instrumentation layer with sensible defaults, it could actually speed up prototyping by making experiments reproducible out-of-the-box. But if it becomes a mandatory gate (\"you can't run an experiment without registering in the framework first\"), it will slow things down significantly. The \"modular API\" framing suggests the former, which is good.\n\n**Overall assessment:** The core technical idea is viable and well-aligned with existing patterns. However, the scope is ambitious (entire lab, all experiments), there's a gap between what's described and what Art. 12(3) specifically requires for Annex III systems, and the \"open-source\" framing adds overhead without clear regulatory benefit. From my vantage point focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, I see a solid core but an over-scoped outer layer. The proposal as written is more of a vision than a buildable increment.\n\nI'd lean toward rejecting this as written because:\n1. It's too broadly scoped for a concrete implementation commitment (\"across all experiments,\" \"universal telemetry system\") — it's not a prototype-able unit, it's an infrastructure program.\n2. It conflates ML experiment tracking (hyperparameters, dataset versions) with the specific forensic event logging that Article 12 actually mandates (start/end times of use, reference database identity, input data matching, natural person identification). These are different engineering problems.\n3. The \"open-source\" commitment is an unnecessary constraint from a delivery-speed perspective and adds governance overhead.\n\nBut the core idea (modular API hook into training loops for automatic event capture) IS technically viable and would improve prototyping quality if scoped tightly to one use case first.\n\nMy decision: I'll reject it because as written, it's too broad and misaligned 