        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P1791 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is a \"Human Verifier Attribution System\" — an OAuth-based sign-off portal that logs which natural person approved specific AI outputs, with cryptographic linking to event logs for search matches (Annex III(1)(a) systems).\n\nLet me assess from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- OAuth-based authentication is well-established technology. There's no novel engineering challenge here — it's a standard auth flow with logging. This is highly technically viable.\n- Cryptographic linking to event logs is straightforward (hashing, signed timestamps, or simple foreign key references in a structured log store). No exotic tech needed.\n- The scope is narrow and well-defined: it addresses Article 12(3)(d) specifically — \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\" This is a clean, bounded problem.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- OAuth integration with an internal identity provider (or even a simple one) can be prototyped quickly. A basic sign-off portal with timestamp and user ID is days to weeks of work for a prototype.\n- The cryptographic linkage to event logs is trivial if you design the log schema properly from the start — just include a `verified_by` field or a reference token.\n- However, there's a risk of over-engineering: \"cryptographically linked\" could balloon into building a full PKI infrastructure when a simple signed timestamp + user ID in a structured log would satisfy the regulatory requirement. The regulation says \"identification of natural persons\" — it doesn't mandate cryptographic signatures specifically.\n- The portal aspect (researchers must use it to validate outputs) introduces workflow integration complexity that could slow prototyping. Who are these