g - potentially years.\n- For prototyping speed: the lab needs working logging solutions NOW for compliance. This proposal doesn't deliver a prototype in any reasonable timeframe. It's fundamentally a research endeavor, not an engineering one.\n- However, if we think about it from a \"speed of prototyping\" angle: building ZKP-based log verification is extremely complex to prototype. Even a minimal working proof-of-concept for ZKPs over structured AI system logs would take significant engineering effort with limited near-term payoff.\n\n**Does it clear my bar?**\nMy domain is technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping. \n\nArguments for approval:\n- It addresses a real tension (GDPR vs. logging requirements) that will eventually need solving.\n- If the lab has systems where privacy is genuinely at stake, this could be important long-term.\n- Publishing at top-tier venues gives visibility and positions the lab.\n\nArguments for rejection:\n- This is fundamentally a research project, not an engineering/prototyping project. It doesn't produce a deployable solution in any near timeframe.\n- ZKPs for AI system logging are at an early research stage; the computational overhead makes them impractical for real-time high-throughput systems (especially biometric matching under Annex III 1(a)).\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, this is extremely slow to get anywhere useful. You're building cryptographic infrastructure before you even have the basic logging architecture in place.\n- The proposal says \"investigate\" and \"develop\" - it's exploratory research, not a path to a shippable prototype.\n- For the immediate compliance needs of Article 12, there are much more straightforward engineering solutions (structured logging with access controls, encryption at rest, data minimization in what gets logged) that don't require ZKPs.\n- The \"publish in top-tier AI venues\" framing tells me this is academically motivated rather than driven by a clear engineering deliverable.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is a scientifically interesting problem but it's not technically viable as a near-term prototyping path, and the speed to any usable output would be very slow. The computational overhead of ZKPs makes them impractical for the real-time logging requirements that Article 12 implies (especially for biometric systems). I'd need to see a concrete engineering deliverable with a timeline, not \"we will investigate.\"\n\nI'll reject it. My promise score should reflect that while I acknowledge the intellectual merit, from my specific vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality), this scores low. Maybe around 15-20? It's not zero because if the lab had a genuine privacy constraint where standard encrypted logging wasn't sufficient, ZKPs could eventually help. But as stated, it's a research project with no clear path to a deployable prototype in any reasonable timeframe, and the technical approach has significant overhead concerns for real-time systems.\n\nI'll go with 18 - there's some long-term potential but from my engineering/prototyping perspective this is very far from viable or fast.\n",
