Syncs model versioning and training logs directly into required technical documentation formats\" — this is somewhat vague. What exactly are the \"required technical documentation formats mandated by Article 12\"? Actually, Article 12 itself doesn't mandate specific formats; it mandates logging capabilities. The detailed technical documentation requirements are in Annex IV (Article 18). This conflation could be a problem.\n- The proposal is more about documentation generation than about the actual technical capability for automatic event recording (logs over lifetime), which is the core of Article 12(1)\n- \"Real-time compliance dossiers\" — while appealing, there's a risk that automated pipelines can produce documentation that looks complete but misses nuances or context needed for genuine compliance assessment\n- From a regulatory perspective, I need to ensure that what's generated actually meets the substance of the requirements, not just produces formatted documents\n\n**Institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- Having an automated system demonstrates to regulators and auditors that the institution has systemic, ongoing compliance infrastructure rather than ad-hoc responses — this is reputationally positive\n- If the pipeline fails or produces inaccurate documentation, it could be worse than having no system at all (gives false confidence)\n- The fact that it reduces burden on scientists is a secondary benefit; from compliance standpoint, what matters is whether the output meets regulatory requirements\n\n**Verdict:**\nThis proposal addresses a real and important gap. From a compliance perspective, having systematic, automated documentation generation that keeps pace with development is far superior to manual, periodic compilation. The alignment with Article 12's lifetime record-keeping obligation is strong. However, the proposal as stated is somewhat focused on \"documentation formats\" rather than the core technical logging capability (automatic recording of events). But it does mention \"training logs\" and \"model versioning,\" which are relevant inputs.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It directly supports Article 12 compliance by ensuring documentation is current throughout the lifecycle, reduces risk of gaps, provides audit trails, and demonstrates organizational commitment to ongoing compliance. The slight imprecision in referring to \"formats mandated by Article 12\" (when formats are more in Annex IV) doesn't disqualify it — it's a framing issue that can be corrected in implementation.\n\nI'll approve with a high promise score but not 100 because of the scope limitation (it focuses on documentation pipeline rather than the full logging capability including the specific data points in Art 12(3)).\n",
