ict regulatory burden into a proprietary technical advantage\").\n- The \"reputation for safety and reliability\" framing is about market positioning, not scientific contribution.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, broader scientific impact), this proposal is primarily an applied engineering/compliance project with modest research content. It applies known cryptographic techniques to a well-defined regulatory requirement. The intellectual depth is moderate at best — it's more of a systems integration challenge than a frontier research question. The framing as \"proprietary technical advantage\" and \"reputation\" suggests the output will be closed, limiting broader scientific impact.\n\nHowever, I should give credit where due: the intersection of privacy-preserving computation with AI audit trails is an emerging area, and there may be genuine novel contributions in how to structure verifiable yet privacy-preserving logs for biometric verification events. The specific problem of logging \"who verified\" without exposing identity could yield publishable results if approached from a research perspective.\n\nBut the proposal as written doesn't signal that it's aimed at generating generalizable knowledge. It's framed as building a system (\"build an encrypted ledger\"), not as investigating open questions. The promise score should reflect modest intellectual merit and limited scientific impact, though there's some genuine novelty in the specific application domain.\n\nI'd lean toward rejecting this from my vantage point — it's more of an engineering implementation project than a research initiative, despite being labeled \"R&D.\" The knowledge frontier contribution is thin, the intellectual merit is moderate (applying known techniques), and the broader scientific impact is deliberately limited by the proprietary framing. However, I won't go to 0 because there IS some genuine research content in designing privacy-pre