e\" — this is somewhat subjective, so automated tests would need clearly defined criteria mapped from the regulation to test assertions.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a strong fit for my domain. Catching compliance gaps early in CI/CD means developers get immediate feedback rather than discovering logging deficiencies during later QA or audit phases.\n- It reduces rework: if you know at commit time that your logging is missing the \"reference database\" field, you fix it immediately rather than weeks later.\n- The test suites are reusable across projects — once built, they apply to any high-risk AI system in the lab.\n- However, there's a risk of brittleness: if the logging format changes (which it likely will as systems evolve), tests break and create noise. Need good abstraction.\n- The \"simulated high-risk events\" part requires understanding what constitutes each specific use case's high-risk scenario. This is domain-specific and may not generalize perfectly, but per-system test configurations are manageable.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The proposal assumes logging capabilities can be tested in isolation via simulated events. In practice, some logging (especially around Article 14(5) human verification) requires integration with broader system workflows, not just the model endpoint. This makes the \"automated\" part slightly more complex than pure unit/integration testing of a model API.\n- There's a risk that over-automating compliance checks in CI creates a false sense of security — passing automated tests doesn't guarantee full regulatory compliance (the regulation requires logging to be \"appropriate to intended purpose,\" which is a judgment call). But this doesn't invalidate the approach; it just means these are necessary but not sufficient conditions.\n- Technical feasibility: HIGH. This is standard test engineering applied to a new domain. No novel research needed.\n\n**Verdict:** From my vantage point, this is a highly viable and pr