ar is: does it materially advance our compliance posture, protect us from regulatory action, or preserve market reputation in a way that justifies the resource commitment?\n\nI think this is a \"nice to have\" rather than a \"must have.\" It's intellectually valuable and could provide some defensive evidence, but it doesn't directly implement Article 12 requirements. We need working logging systems first; benchmarks second. Also, the ambiguity of \"appropriate to intended purpose\" means no single dataset will be definitive - regulators will judge case by case.\n\nHowever, there IS a real compliance value: if we can show our logging meets an objective standard, that strengthens our position in any enforcement action or market surveillance. And peer-review adds credibility. For a lab developing multiple high-risk AI systems, having a validated traceability metric could be operationally useful for internal QA of our logging implementations.\n\nI'll lean toward approve but with moderate promise score. It's not core compliance work (we don't need it to meet the letter of Article 12), but it provides valuable defensive evidence and positions us well. Actually, let me reconsider - is this really something that should be in MY portfolio? As Compliance Officer, I care about: do our systems comply? Can we demonstrate compliance? Are we protected from enforcement? This proposal helps with \"can we demonstrate\" in a more rigorous way.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate promise score - maybe 55-60. It's useful but not critical. It doesn't directly implement the regulation; it creates an evaluation framework. The direct compliance work (building logging into systems, ensuring start/end times are recorded for Annex III 1(a) systems, etc.) is more urgent and more clearly within my mandate.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 58.\n",
