d infrastructure on expensive logging for systems that are not legally classified as high-risk.\" While this is a legitimate efficiency concern, the framing slightly prioritizes cost avoidance over compliance assurance. From my vantage point, the PRIMARY purpose must be ensuring NO high-risk system falls through the cracks. However, I can read this charitably — it's saying \"we need to know which systems are in scope so we allocate resources correctly,\" which is sound.\n- A quarterly cadence might be debatable — some would argue more frequent (monthly) given rapid R&D iteration. But quarterly with a classification matrix is a reasonable starting framework.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — it's a governance/process proposal rather than a technical implementation. But that's appropriate at the planning stage.\n\n**Decision from Compliance perspective:**\nThis is clearly a foundational, necessary step for regulatory compliance. Without knowing which systems are high-risk, you cannot implement Article 12 correctly. It also protects against the existential risk of non-compliance (fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under the Act). The quarterly review cycle ensures ongoing compliance as the portfolio evolves. This directly supports institutional survival and regulatory compliance.\n\nI would approve this. It's a core compliance infrastructure need. The promise score should be high — this is almost table-stakes for any serious AI R&D institution operating in the EU market. I'd put it at 88-92. It's not a perfect proposal (it could be more specific about escalation paths, who owns the classification decisions, what happens when there's ambiguity), but as a governance framework, it's strongly aligned with my mandate.\n\nLet me settle on 90.\n",
