ecision.\n- Risk: if the framework is too rigid or takes too long to build, it could delay research timelines. But using OpenTelemetry mitigates this since it's mature and well-documented.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- OpenTelemetry is widely adopted in industry and has active community support. Building on top of it means the lab isn't maintaining a proprietary logging system forever — the upstream project continues to evolve.\n- The proposal specifically mentions \"regardless of the model architecture used,\" which suggests they're thinking about integration with diverse infrastructure (different GPU clusters, different training frameworks, inference servers). This is good for leveraging existing heterogeneous R&D infrastructure rather than forcing everything into one mold.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- Who maintains this framework long-term? If it's assigned to a small team, that could be a bottleneck or single point of failure. But that's an implementation detail, not a reason to reject the proposal.\n- Does \"all high-risk prototypes\" include early-stage research where you might not yet know if something will become high-risk? This could impose overhead too early. But Article 12 applies to high-risk systems specifically, and the framework can be designed with graduated adoption (opt-in for teams who are close to deployment).\n- The scope is reasonable: timestamps, input data, system events. These are core logging needs that align directly with Articles 12(1), 12(2)(a)-(c), and potentially 12(3) requirements. It's not over-scoped.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-targeted infrastructure investment that directly addresses the lab's compliance burden while leveraging existing open-source tooling (OpenTelemetry). It reduces per-project engineering overhead, aligns with efficient resource allocation by amortizing development cost across many projects, and uses established standards rather than proprietary solutions. The scope is appropriately bounded — it's a logging framework, not a full observability platform or data pipeline. From my vantage point of resource efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, this clears the bar comfortably.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly. It's not a moonshot, it's practical, well-scoped, leverages existing standards, and addresses a real compliance cost that would otherwise fragment across many teams. I'll give it around 82-85. It's very good but not a 100 because there are some execution risks (scope creep into \"all prototypes,\" maintenance burden) and the ROI depends on the lab actually having multiple high-risk projects in parallel, which is likely given we're an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the Act.\n\nI'll go with 83.\n",
