e and interpretive, not just metrics.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A prototype could be built quickly: a template engine (Jinja2, or even structured Markdown generation) connected to an MLflow/W&B API. You could have a working v1 in days to a couple weeks.\n- The quality concern: auto-generated documentation risks being superficial. If the template just fills in numbers without proper context, narrative, and interpretation, it won't satisfy \"clear and comprehensive form\" as Recital 71 demands. Regulators expect understanding, not just data dumps.\n- The \"eliminates massive manual administrative burden\" claim is somewhat overstated. Some content (limitations, risk assessment narratives, intended purpose descriptions) genuinely requires human authorship. But the proposal does reduce the tedious copy-paste and formatting work.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- This is a very buildable project with clear scope. I could prototype it quickly.\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (version control for data/hyperparameters), which reduces risk.\n- The technical architecture is simple: API → template engine → document output. No novel algorithms needed.\n- Risk: over-promising on automation degree. But as a development engineer, I can build the 70% automation part and flag the 30% that needs human input.\n- Speed of delivery: high. This is not a research problem; it's an engineering integration task with well-understood components.\n- Quality: The prototype will be solid technically. The quality question is whether auto-generated docs satisfy regulatory requirements, which is more of a legal/compliance question than a technical one. But from my perspective, the system can produce accurate, well-formatted documents that incorporate real data — that's the technical deliverable.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a technically viable, quickly prototypable solution. It addresses a real burden. The main risk is scope creep into trying to automate qualitat