ecture.\n\nConcerns/weaknesses:\n1. **Scope creep risk:** \"Unified\" across ALL R&D prototypes could become a bottleneck. If the framework team's roadmap doesn't match my specific prototype needs, I'm stuck waiting or fighting with abstractions. But this is more of an organizational concern than a technical one.\n2. **\"Plug-and-play\" in quotes:** In practice, even standardized logging libraries require per-project configuration (what events to log, what fields are relevant). For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, the requirements are quite specific (reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons verifying results). A truly \"plug-and-play\" solution might not cover all edge cases without some project-specific code. But a good framework provides the scaffolding and you fill in specifics - that's still much faster than building from zero.\n3. **Maturity:** It's being proposed as something to develop (\"We will develop\"), so there's an initial investment period before it pays off. The first few projects won't benefit until the library is built and stabilized. But for a lab with multiple high-risk prototypes in pipeline, ROI comes quickly.\n4. **Async event-bus adds operational complexity:** One more moving part (message broker) to maintain. In containerized environments this is manageable but not zero-cost. However, the trade-off of decoupling logging from critical inference path is well worth it for high-risk systems where latency matters and where you can't afford log-write failures to crash the system.\n\n**Overall assessment:** From a development engineering perspective focused on prototyping speed and quality, this is strongly aligned with my concerns. The main pain in iterative development of compliant AI systems is the repetitive compliance plumbing that slows down experimentation. This proposal directly attacks that. The technical architecture (async event bus + containerization) is sound, mature, and low-risk. The \"p