        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2574 from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on: resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**What the proposal is:** A research initiative to develop \"semantic logging methods\" that record internal reasoning of high-risk systems in human-comprehensible format. It aims to distill neural activations into traceable event logs mapped to regulatory risk indicators. The framing explicitly positions it as turning a compliance task into an XAI contribution.\n\n**From my vantage point (resource-allocation efficiency, scientific ambition vs funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure):**\n\nArguments for approval:\n- Article 12 requires logging, so there's a genuine regulatory driver. However, the actual requirement in Art. 12 is about recording events (start/end times, reference databases, input data, identification of verification persons) — it does NOT require recording \"internal reasoning\" or \"neural activations.\" The regulation asks for event-level logs, not interpretability of model internals.\n- The proposal conflates a compliance obligation with a research frontier problem. What the law requires (recording events, inputs, outputs, timestamps) is well-understood engineering. What this proposal wants to do (distill neural activations into traceable semantic logs) is an open research problem in XAI — not something Article 12 mandates or even clearly requires.\n- The framing \"transforms a mandatory compliance task into an intellectual contribution\" is intellectually appealing but potentially misleading about the actual scope of the obligation.\n\nArguments for rejection:\n- **Resource misallocation:** This is a high-risk, uncertain research bet. Distilling neural activations into human-comprehensible traces is an open problem — it may not be solvable in any reasonable timeframe. Committing scarce implementation resources to this when the regulatory requirement is far more modest (event logging) is poor resource allocation.\n- **Scope mismatch with regulation:** Article 12(1)-(3) requires automatic recording of events, specific data points for Annex III point 1(a) systems. It does not require interpretability of internal model states. The proposal over-engineers the solution to a problem the regulation doesn't actually pose at that level.\n- **Funding constraints:** A \"research initiative\" to develop new methods for distilling neural activations is expensive, open-ended, and has no clear completion criterion. This conflicts with the need to allocate finite resources efficiently. We could meet Article 12 compliance with standard engineering approaches (structured event logging, timestamps, data provenance tracking) at a fraction of the cost.\n- **Effective use of existing infrastructure:** If we already have R&D infrastructure for model training and deployment, adding a separate \"semantic logging\" research track means building new tooling, hiring or diverting researchers into XAI, and potentially creating parallel systems that don't integrate cleanly with our compliance pipeline.\n- **No clear deliverable tied to the regulation:** The proposal doesn't specify what concrete artifact would satisfy Article 12. It's framed as a research exploration without a defined endpoint.\n\nHowever, there IS a kernel of value: understanding why the system made certain decisions could aid in post-market monitoring (Art. 72) and identifying risk situations (Art. 79(1)). But that can be addressed with targeted, scoped engineering work rather than an open-ended \"research initiative.\"\n\nMy assessment: This proposal, as written, is a research bet on an unsolved problem dressed up as regulatory compliance. From my resource-allocation perspective, it commits scarce resources to high-uncertainty R&D when the actual regulatory requirement can be met with well-understood engineering. The scientific ambition is real but misaligned with the funding constraint reality. I would reject this in its current form — though a narrower, scoped version (e.g., \"develop structured logging that captures decision-relevant features at inference time for post-market monitoring\") might be worth exploring as part of our compliance engineering effort rather than as a standalone research initiative.\n\nDecision: Reject.\nPromise score: Maybe 30-40. There's intellectual value in the XAI angle, and the general direction (better logging that aids traceability) is sound. But the specific proposal to develop new methods for distilling neural activations is too open-ended and resource-intensive relative to what the regulation actually demands. I'll say 35.\n",
