aid we don't fund - \"approving a proposal means committing the lab's scarce implementation resources to pursue it, not funding to explore whether an idea might someday work.\"\n\n**Regulatory Alignment:**\n- The regulation requires specific data points to be recorded. You can compress the *storage format* (e.g., use columnar storage, delta encoding for timestamps, etc.) but you cannot apply lossy compression or sampling to the actual required data fields.\n- Article 12(2) says logging shall \"enable the recording of events\" - it doesn't say you can record a compressed/sampled version that loses information.\n- The \"tension between detailed record-keeping and system performance\" is somewhat overstated for most AI systems. Logging structured events (timestamps, IDs, references) is computationally trivial compared to the inference itself. The real challenge might be storage volume over long lifetimes, not computational overhead during operation.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- Technically, lossy compression on regulatory data is non-viable as a compliance solution. You'd need to demonstrate that the compressed form still meets all Article 12 requirements, which for lossy compression means you'd lose required information.\n- As a research project with publication goals, it's not a prototyping path. It doesn't produce an implementable artifact on any reasonable timeline.\n- The actual engineering problem (if one exists) is more mundane: efficient storage and retrieval of structured logs at scale, which is well-solved in existing databases and logging frameworks. This doesn't require novel algorithms or top-tier conference publications.\n- The framing as \"turning compliance into a publishable contribution\" suggests the motivation is academic output rather than solving an actual engineering bottleneck.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal misidentifies the technical problem, proposes a technically non-viable s