yment\" timing is good for prototyping speed: you catch issues when they're cheap to fix rather than after an auditor flags them.\n- However, there's a subtlety: to truly validate logging, you need to actually exercise the system (run inference, trigger events) and then inspect the logs. This means the sandbox needs to be able to run the prototype in a representative way. For AI systems, this could mean loading test datasets, triggering various code paths. That's not trivial but is standard testing infrastructure.\n- The \"dedicated testing environment\" framing suggests some overhead — spinning up another environment. But if it's integrated into the existing CI pipeline as a test stage rather than a completely separate environment, the overhead is minimal. If it truly means a separate sandbox, that adds operational complexity.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The proposal says \"dedicated testing environment\" which could mean extra infrastructure cost and maintenance. For prototyping speed, you want this integrated into your existing pipeline, not a separate thing to maintain.\n- The \"reconstruct event sequence\" approach is good but requires defining what constitutes a valid \"sequence\" for each system type. This is specification work that precedes implementation.\n- For the general Art 12(2) requirements (a-c), the check is somewhat qualitative (\"events relevant for identifying situations that may result in risk\"). Automating verification of \"relevance\" is harder than checking for specific fields. The test suite might end up being more of a structured checklist than a true automated reconstruction test for the general case.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric ID), it's very concrete and automatable.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea is technically sound, well-scoped, and aligns with good engineering practice (shift-left compliance testing). It directly serves prototyping speed by catching logging gaps early. The main risk is scope creep in the \"