        "reasoning_content": "The compliance officer's domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation. I need to evaluate this proposal strictly from that vantage point against Article 12 of the EU AI Act.\n\nLet me analyze Proposal P2837: \"Unified Telemetry Middleware\"\n\n**What it proposes:**\n- A standardized software layer (shared library) for internal framework\n- Intercepts API calls to log event data\n- Captures system inputs, outputs, and timestamps across all high-risk model versions\n- Logs into a centralized, immutable database\n- Decouples regulatory record-keeping from AI development\n- Preserves prototyping speed while ensuring traceability\n\n**Compliance analysis against Article 12:**\n\nArticle 12(1): \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n- The proposal addresses this directly: it's a technical layer that automatically captures events. This is a core compliance requirement.\n\nArticle 12(2): Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for:\n(a) identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification;\n(b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 72);\n(c) monitoring operation per Art 26(5).\n- The proposal captures inputs, outputs, and timestamps. This covers a lot but the proposal text doesn't explicitly address all three sub-points. However, having inputs/outputs/timestamps is foundational to enabling those purposes.\n\nArticle 12(3): For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), specific minimum logging:\n(a) period of each use (start/end date and time);\n(b) reference database against which input data was checked;\n(c) input data for which search led to a match;\n(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification per Art 14(5).\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps\" which covers start/end dates. It captures inputs/outputs. But it doesn't explicitly