e framing to suggest that speed is prioritized over completeness of logging. However, the actual mechanism (decoupling via middleware) is sound.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from a compliance standpoint. It directly addresses the core legal obligation in Article 12(1), provides infrastructure for meeting 12(2), and can be extended to meet 12(3). The standardized, centralized approach reduces compliance risk significantly. The immutable storage aligns with record-keeping integrity. The decoupling from development ensures that logging isn't an afterthought or something developers might skip under pressure.\n\nThis is clearly a \"must-have\" for regulatory compliance with the AI Act. Without it, we cannot demonstrate that our high-risk systems \"technically allow for automatic recording of events.\" This isn't optional - it's a legal requirement with significant penalty exposure.\n\nDecision: Approve.\nPromise score: This is a foundational compliance infrastructure piece. It directly maps to a hard legal obligation. I'd rate this very highly. The only reason I'm not at 100 is that the proposal as stated is somewhat high-level and doesn't explicitly address all the sub-requirements (especially Art 12(3) specifics, GDPR intersection with logging personal data, retention periods). But the direction is exactly right and it's critical for institutional survival. I'll give it a 92.\n",
