        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2623\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The underlying tension between Article 12(3)(d)'s requirement to identify natural persons and GDPR data-minimisation is real, but the proposal bundles four separate cryptographic workstreams (hashing/encryption, differential-privacy logging, encrypted identity vaults, cryptographic proofs of access) into a single 'R&D initiative' with no defined prototype milestone, no priority among the Article 12 sub-clauses it targets, and no integration plan against our existing log pipeline. From a prototyping-speed standpoint this scope would stretch implementation resources across too many open-ended research threads to yield a verifiable, shippable logging module in any near-term window; differential-privacy noise on logs also risks undermining the very traceability Article 12(2) mandates, making the 'resolves the tension' claim premature without a working prototype that demonstrates regulator-queryable fidelity.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 38\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2623 is about implementing privacy-preserving traceability through cryptographic techniques (secure hashing, encryption, differential privacy, encrypted identity vaults, cryptographic proofs of access) for logs that involve personal data in high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The proposal bundles several technically challenging components: secure hashing/encryption for logs, differentially private logging mechanisms, encrypted identity vaults, and cryptographic proofs of access. These are all well-studied areas in cryptography, but integrating them into a cohesive system that satisfies both EU AI Act Article 12 requirements AND GDPR is non-trivial.\n- Differential privacy applied to logging is an interesting idea but technically very challenging. Logs need to be queryable by regulators (Article 12 requires they enable traceability), yet differential privacy adds noise that could undermine the \"identifying situations\" and \"monitoring operation\" purposes in Article 12(2). There's a fundamental tension: if you add too much noise, you lose the traceability the Act demands; if you add too little, you haven't achieved meaningful privacy.\n- Encrypted identity vaults with \"cryptographic proofs of access\" (likely zero-knowledge proofs or similar) are feasible but complex to implement and integrate into existing logging pipelines. The verification side (how does a regulator verify without decrypting?) adds significant complexity.\n- Article 12(3)(d) specifically requires \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results\" — this is a hard requirement for biometric systems. Encrypting this while still allowing regulatory access is technically possible but introduces key management challenges (who holds keys? what if the lab loses them?).\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is described as an \"R&D initiative\" with \"researchers will integrate...\" — it's framed as exploratory research rather than a concrete engineering deliverable. From my vantage point, this is problematic because:\n  - It doesn't specify a clear prototype milestone or MVP\n  - The scope is very broad (hashing + encryption + differential privacy + identity vaults + cryptographic proofs) — that's at least 3-4 separate technical workstreams\n  - No mention of which specific Article 12 sub-requirements it addresses first\n  - \"Resolves the inherent tension\" is a strong claim without acknowledging that this may be an unsolved or partially solved problem in practice\n  - The integration complexity with existing logging infrastructure isn't addressed\n\n**From my domain (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- The ideas are sound in principle but the proposal as written is too broad and research-oriented to commit scarce implementation resources to. It reads more like a literature survey topic than an engineering project with clear deliverables.\n- However, the underlying problem IS real: Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons, which conflicts with GDPR data minimization. A focused prototype on one specific mechanism (e.g., encrypted logging with a regulator-accessible decryption key escrow) could be prototyped in a reasonable timeframe.\n- The breadth (differential privacy + encryption + vaults + ZK proofs) makes this hard to prototype quickly and at quality. You'd need to pick ONE approach and go deep.\n\nMy assessment: The problem is real and important, but the proposal as framed is too broad, too research-y, and doesn't give me confidence it can produce a working prototype in a reasonable timeframe with the resources we have. It would be more viable if narrowed to one specific mechanism for one specific Article 12 requirement (e.g., encrypted logging of verifier identity under 12(3)(d) with key escrow). As written, it's a multi-year research program masquerading as an implementation project.\n\nI'll reject this because from my prototyping speed/quality vantage point, the scope is too diffuse, it's framed as open-ended research rather than engineering delivery, and the integration complexity of 4+ cryptographic techniques into a logging pipeline would slow us down significantly without a clear \"done\" criterion. But I'll give it a moderate promise score because the underlying problem is legitimate and a narrowed version could be valuable.\n\nPromise: I'd say around 35-40. The problem is real, but the execution as proposed would consume resources without producing a shippable artifact in reasonable time.\n",
