tems technically allow for these logs.\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- Resource commitment: periodic audits require dedicated compliance staff time, development team availability. But this is a moderate commitment compared to building the logging infrastructure itself.\n- The proposal says \"using only existing logs\" - if the logging system isn't built yet, these drills would just confirm non-compliance repeatedly without adding value until the infrastructure exists. However, even in that case, it creates urgency and accountability for implementation.\n- No mention of frequency (\"periodic\" is vague). But this is an operational detail, not a fundamental flaw.\n- No explicit mention of Article 12(2)(a) - situations presenting risk under Art 79(1) or substantial modification. The focus seems more on Art 12(3) items (biometric/identity systems in Annex III point 1(a)). But the general \"reconstruct a decision path\" could cover this broadly.\n\nFrom my compliance perspective:\n- This is a strong, proportionate proposal that directly operationalizes Article 12 obligations\n- It demonstrates to regulators that we have an active monitoring function (aligns with Art 72 post-market monitoring)\n- The \"before external authorities discover them\" framing shows proactive governance culture\n- The gap documentation creates evidence of continuous improvement\n- Low cost relative to the risk it mitigates (a failed audit or enforcement action could be existential for our market position)\n\nThis clearly clears my bar. It's a direct, practical compliance measure that strengthens our regulatory posture without requiring disproportionate resources. I'd approve this with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is squarely in my domain. It directly addresses the traceability requirements of Article 12, creates a defensible audit trail showing proactive compliance, and protects against enforcement risk. The main limitation is it's somewhat dependent on the logging infrastructure actually 