ting and validation processes used.\" Tracking data lineage and model versions directly supports the technical documentation requirements.\n- It's automated, which aligns with Article 12(1)'s requirement that systems \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\"\n- Centralized tracking provides a single source of truth, which aids in verifying compliance.\n- Dataset hashes and configuration logging support traceability of how the system was developed.\n\nWeaknesses/Gaps:\n- The proposal is focused on TRAINING-time provenance (data lineage, hyperparameters, model versions). Article 12(1) specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system\" — this includes operational/deployment logs, not just training records. The proposal doesn't address runtime event logging.\n- It doesn't explicitly address the specific requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III biometric systems (start/end times, reference database, input data matches, natural person identification).\n- It doesn't directly address post-market monitoring (Article 72) or identifying risk situations (Article 79(1)) as required by Article 12(2)(a) and (b).\n- The emphasis on \"reproducibility of our scientific findings\" suggests a research mindset rather than a compliance/operational monitoring mindset.\n- However, it does partially support the technical documentation requirement in Recital 71 about keeping information up to date throughout lifetime.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\nThis is a genuinely useful infrastructure piece for the technical documentation and development-phase traceability aspects of Article 12/Recital 71. It addresses part of what's needed — specifically the \"how high-risk AI systems have been developed\" portion (data, training processes). However, it doesn't cover the full scope of Article 12, particularly runtime event logging over the system's lifetime and operational monitoring.\n\nThat said, the question 