ystems: period of each use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification. A generic interceptor might not naturally capture all of these — particularly the \"reference database against which input data has been checked\" or \"identification of natural persons involved in verification.\" Those are more domain-specific and may need additional work beyond a simple decorator.\n2. However, the proposal says it's for \"all high-risk prototypes\" broadly, so it might be designed as a foundation that can be extended. The core logging (inputs, outputs, timestamps) is the hard part; the Annex III specifics are more about what data flows through those logged events.\n3. Implementation cost: building a robust interceptor framework in Python libraries is moderate effort — not trivial but very doable for an R&D lab with existing Python infrastructure. It's not a massive undertaking compared to, say, building entirely new infrastructure or hiring compliance staff.\n4. \"Guaranteeing consistent record-keeping\" is a strong claim. In practice, decorator patterns can be bypassed, and sidecars have their own failure modes. But as an architectural approach, it's the right direction.\n\n**Resource allocation assessment:**\n- This is a high-leverage, moderate-cost intervention that addresses a hard regulatory requirement (Article 12) across all future high-risk systems.\n- It uses existing infrastructure (Python libraries).\n- It reduces per-project compliance costs by centralizing logging.\n- The scope is well-bounded and implementable within reasonable timelines.\n- It doesn't require new headcount beyond what's likely already in the platform/infrastructure team.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding balance:**\n- This doesn't consume significant research time because it operates as middleware \"without modifying core model logic.\"\n- It frees researchers from having to think about compliance logging, letting them focus on science.\n- The cost is one-time infrastructure investment that pays dividends across all projects.\n\nOverall: This is a well-scoped, efficient use of existing infrastructure to meet a mandatory regulatory requirement with minimal disruption to scientific work. From my vantage point (resource efficiency, using existing infra, balancing ambition with constraints), this clears the bar comfortably. It's not flashy but it's exactly the kind of pragmatic, high-leverage investment that protects the lab from compliance risk while minimizing overhead on researchers.\n\nI'd rate this fairly high — maybe 82-85. It's solidly good, directly addresses a hard requirement, leverages existing infrastructure, and is appropriately scoped. I might dock a few points because:\n- The \"guarantee\" language is optimistic; in practice there will be edge cases.\n- It doesn't explicitly address the Annex III 1(a) specific minimums (reference database tracking, natural person identification), though those could be extensions.\n- There's some risk that a one-size-fits-all decorator pattern struggles with heterogeneous system architectures within the lab.\n\nBut these are implementation details, not fundamental flaws in the approach. I'll go with 84 and approve.\n",
