        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2839\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core mechanism—version-controlled Markdown docs in Git, auto-generated changelog fragments from CI test runs linked to model checkpoints—is technically straightforward and prototypable within one or two sprints; no exotic infrastructure is needed. However, the proposal conflates development-time documentation (more Article 11 territory) with Article 12's specific mandate for *runtime* automatic event logging (inference timestamps, input-data matches, verification-personnel IDs), so as scoped it would not deliver the operational log stream that Article 12(1)–(3) actually requires of the deployed system; a separate runtime-logging engineering track would still be needed, and the 'real-time evolving knowledge base' framing adds scope that slows prototyping without proportional compliance gain.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 62\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2839 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is about creating a \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Standard\" — a living documentation protocol using Markdown, Git, version-controlled docs linked to model checkpoints and validation reports, with automated log generation during testing.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Using Markdown + Git for version-controlled documentation is technically straightforward and well-understood. This is not a novel engineering challenge — it's standard practice in software development (README files, CHANGELOGs, docs-as-code).\n- Linking technical claims to specific model checkpoints and validation reports is feasible but requires careful schema design. The \"linking\" part is where complexity creeps in — you need a consistent naming convention for checkpoints, a way to reference validation reports programmatically, and a mapping layer between prose claims and artifacts.\n- \"Automate the generation of these logs during testing\" — this is the most technically substantive claim. During CI/CD test runs, generating structured documentation fragments (e.g., \"model X at checkpoint Y passed validation Z with metrics A,B,C\") is very doable. You'd need a templating system and hooks in your test pipeline. This is prototypably viable within days to a couple weeks for a basic version.\n- \"Evolves alongside the AI system to record its capabilities, limitations, and risk-management decisions in real time\" — this is where it gets more ambitious. Real-time updating of documentation as the system evolves (retraining, fine-tuning, deployment changes) requires event-driven hooks or CI integration. Feasible but adds complexity.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core infrastructure (Git repo with Markdown docs + a script that auto-generates changelog entries from test runs) is very prototypable. You could have a working prototype in a sprint or two.\n- However, the proposal conflates several things: technical documentation (Article 11 territory), automatic logging of events (Article 12 territory), and risk-management records. As a development engineer, I'd note that the \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system\" in Article 12(1) is about runtime event logging — timestamps of inference calls, inputs, outputs, decisions made. The proposal seems to focus more on documentation of model development history rather than runtime operational logs. There's a gap here between what the regulation requires for Article 12 (runtime event logs) and what this proposal delivers (development-time documentation).\n- That said, the automated generation during testing is a solid building block, and the Git-based version control is low-friction to implement.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage:**\n1. The proposal somewhat conflates Article 12's requirement for \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system\" with documentation generation. Article 12(3)(a)-(d) specifically calls for recording periods of use, reference databases, input data matches, and verification personnel — these are runtime operational logs, not development documentation. The proposal's focus on model checkpoints and validation reports is more aligned with Article 11 (technical documentation) than Article 12 (record-keeping/logging).\n2. \"Real time\" evolution of docs is technically doable but adds engineering overhead that may not be justified for prototyping speed. A batch approach (update docs after each release/checkpoint) would be faster to implement and 90% as effective.\n3. The Markdown + Git stack is a good choice — low dependency, widely supported, easy to prototype with. No heavy infrastructure needed.\n\n**Verdict:**\nThe core technical idea is viable and prototypable quickly. The Markdown+Git approach is pragmatic and fast to implement. Automated log generation during testing is a clear, bounded engineering task. However, the proposal somewhat misaligns with Article 12's actual requirement (runtime event logging vs. development documentation), and the \"real-time\" and \"living\" framing adds scope that could slow prototyping without proportional compliance benefit.\n\nFrom my vantage: it's technically sound in its core mechanism, quick to prototype a useful version of, but I'd want to narrow the scope to make it actually serve Article 12 (runtime logs) rather than being primarily an Article 11 documentation tool dressed up as something else. The technical approach is good; the alignment with the specific regulatory requirement needs tightening.\n\nI'll lean toward approve because the core engineering is viable and fast, but I'll note the scope concern. Actually, let me reconsider — my job is to screen on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality. The proposal IS technically viable and quick to prototype a useful version. The misalignment with Article 12's specific requirements is more of a compliance/regulatory mapping issue than a pure engineering feasibility issue. From a pure \"can we build this well and fast\" standpoint, yes — Markdown+Git+test-pipeline hooks is very doable.\n\nI'll approve it with a moderate-to-high promise score, noting that the core mechanism is sound and fast to prototype, but flagging that the runtime logging requirement of Article 12 specifically needs its own engineering track beyond this documentation system.\n",
