        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2105\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal sits squarely in my domain: Article 12(1) imposes lifetime-long automatic logging, and without a deliberate storage architecture the cumulative log volume will silently consume disk I/O, network bandwidth, and compute that our R&D pipelines depend on. A tiered cold-storage design is the most resource-efficient way to satisfy multi-year retention at audit-grade durability without locking expensive fast media onto data accessed perhaps once per regulatory inspection cycle; explicitly budgeting the archive as a line item also prevents compliance overhead from being absorbed informally by science teams, which is precisely the slow erosion of scientific ambition I am accountable for protecting. The main reservation is that the 'capacity audit' component is fairly standard IT hygiene and may yield limited new insight if the infrastructure team already tracks utilisation dashboards; the real value lies in the architectural decision and the committed budget envelope rather than the assessment itself, so the proposal's scope could be tightened to avoid spending person-weeks on a review whose outputs are largely predictable.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 74\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2105) as the Lab Director of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P2105:\n\n\"Regulatory Infrastructure Capacity Audit. A strategic review of our storage and compute resources to accommodate long-term event logging mandates. The infrastructure team will assess current disk capacities and implement a tiered cold-storage architecture for historical logs over the system lifetime. We will then adjust the annual budget to secure scalable cloud or on-premise archives. It prevents regulatory requirements from compromising our existing R&D performance through resource contention.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with:\n1. Resource-allocation efficiency\n2. Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints\n3. Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\n\nLet me assess this proposal:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses the core concern: Article 12(1) mandates automatic logging \"over the lifetime of the system.\" This is a long-term storage obligation that will consume disk space, I/O bandwidth, and potentially compute for log ingestion. If we don't plan for it now, it WILL compete with R&D workloads for resources.\n- The \"tiered cold-storage architecture\" is a sensible engineering approach — hot data for active systems, warm for recent monitoring (Article 72 post-market monitoring), cold for historical compliance records. This is cost-efficient and doesn't waste expensive fast storage on data that's rarely accessed.\n- It frames the problem correctly: regulatory requirements shouldn't silently cannibalize R&D capacity. By budgeting explicitly, we protect scientific ambition from being eroded by compliance overhead.\n- \"Strategic review\" before implementation means we don't over-provision or under-provision — this is efficient resource allocation.\n- The explicit mention of adjusting the annual budget to secure scalable storage shows awareness that this isn't free and should be a deliberate line item, not an unplanned drain.\n\n**Weaknesses / Concerns:**\n- It's somewhat generic — \"assess current disk capacities\" is basic IT hygiene, almost table stakes. The novelty is low.\n- It doesn't specify what triggers the tiered migration (e.g., after 90 days of inactivity, after system deprecation). Without clear policy parameters, implementation could drift or become unfocused.\n- \"Over the system lifetime\" is potentially very long for AI systems that might be updated/replaced frequently. The scope could balloon if not bounded carefully.\n- It's framed as a one-time audit + architecture change, but logging compliance is ongoing. Does it account for the continuous operational cost of log ingestion pipelines?\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is squarely in my domain. Resource contention between compliance infrastructure and R&D workloads is exactly what I worry about. A tiered storage approach is the most resource-efficient way to handle long-lived logs — you don't pay for NVMe SSDs on data that's accessed once a year during an audit. The explicit budget line protects scientific programs from silent erosion.\n\nHowever, it's also somewhat basic and the \"audit\" component might yield limited new information if our infrastructure team already tracks disk utilization. The real value is in the architectural decision (tiered cold storage) and the budget commitment, not the audit itself. Still, doing a formal capacity assessment before committing to an architecture is prudent resource allocation — you don't want to buy 10PB of cold storage when your actual log volume trajectory suggests you need 500TB.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's directly relevant to my domain, it's cost-conscious (tiered rather than flat expensive storage), it protects R&D from resource contention, and it's a bounded, actionable workstream for the infrastructure team. The promise score: it's solid but not groundbreaking — it's necessary compliance infrastructure planning done in a resource-efficient way. I'd say around 72-78. It addresses a real risk to my domain, uses an efficient architecture pattern, and explicitly protects scientific resources. But it's also somewhat expected/obvious work that any competent lab would do eventually. The fact that it's being proposed proactively is good, but the innovation content is modest.\n\nI'll go with 74.\n",
