hether it's \"complete.\"\n2. The proposal says it \"directly satisfies\" the requirement — but does it? Article 14(5) is about human oversight/monitoring by natural persons. The logging requirement in 12(3)(d) requires that the SYSTEM technically allows for automatic recording of this identification. A web dashboard where validators manually sign off might not be \"automatic recording\" as required by 12(1). There's a nuance: 12(1) says systems shall \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs).\" If the human verification is captured through a system that automatically logs the identity, timestamp, and action, then it could qualify. But if it's purely manual sign-off without automated capture, there might be a gap.\n3. However, the proposal says \"digital timestamps of their verification process\" which implies some form of automatic recording by the system. If the portal captures these events automatically (e.g., when a validator clicks \"verified,\" the system logs who, when, what record), that could satisfy 12(1)'s \"automatic recording.\"\n4. From an institutional survival and market reputation standpoint: having a dedicated compliance tool for a specific Annex III requirement is good. It shows regulatory diligence. If a regulator audits, being able to point to a purpose-built system for verification traceability is reassuring.\n5. The proposal is scoped as a \"lightweight web dashboard\" — this is practical and implementable. It doesn't overpromise or underdeliver on scope.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer vantage point:**\n- This is directly relevant to our compliance obligations for Annex III systems.\n- It addresses a specific, identifiable requirement (12(3)(d)) that we would need to meet.\n- The \"lightweight\" nature makes it resource-feasible.\n- It supports our ability to demonstrate compliance during audits or regulator inquiries.\n- It contributes to the traceability chain required by Article 79 and 72.\n\nHowever, I should note:\n- It only addresses one sub-point of 12(3). But that's fine — it's a focused tool for a specific requirement.\n- The \"lightweight\" nature is good from a resource perspective but I need to ensure it meets the technical standard of \"automatic recording.\"\n- For market reputation, having purpose-built compliance tools signals seriousness to regulators and clients.\n\nMy assessment: This is a solid, targeted proposal that addresses a real regulatory requirement. It's feasible, narrowly scoped (which is good for resource management), and directly tied to Article 12(3)(d). From my vantage point, I'd approve it as it reduces compliance risk for Annex III systems. The promise score should reflect that it's useful but narrow — it doesn't solve the entire logging problem, just one piece. But it does what it says it will do well.\n\nI'll rate this around 68-72. It's a clear \"approve\" from my perspective because:\n- It addresses a specific compliance obligation we have\n- It's implementable within reasonable resources\n- It reduces audit risk\n- It supports the traceability narrative regulators look for\n- It's scoped appropriately (not over-engineered)\n\nBut it's not a 90+ because it only covers one sub-requirement and I'd want to ensure the \"automatic recording\" aspect is technically sound. The proposal as described sounds more like a manual sign-off tool that generates timestamps, which may or may not fully meet the \"technically allow for automatic recording\" standard of 12(1).\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 70.\n",
