ns for performance in high-throughput inference scenarios. This needs careful engineering.\n\nSpeed/quality of prototyping:\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, building a generic middleware layer that works across ALL systems is the opposite of fast prototyping. It's a large infrastructure investment upfront before you can validate whether it actually fits the diverse set of models in the lab.\n- However, once built, it would standardize compliance logging and reduce per-model implementation effort. But the initial build cost is high.\n- The proposal doesn't address: what events are \"relevant\" for different system types (Article 12(2) says \"appropriate to the intended purpose\"), how the event schema maps to Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements, or how it handles the diversity of AI systems in the lab.\n\nConcerns from my vantage:\n1. Scope is too broad without a concrete first target system — \"all high-risk AI models across the institution\" is a multi-quarter infrastructure project, not a prototable unit.\n2. The proposal conflates compliance architecture with engineering implementation. It says WHAT (compliance) more than HOW (specific technical design for our heterogeneous systems).\n3. No mention of performance overhead, storage scaling, or how it integrates with existing MLOps pipelines — these are critical for viability.\n4. The immutability requirement is good but adds complexity; need to specify the mechanism (hash chain? WORM? distributed ledger?).\n\nPositives:\n- It's a real requirement (Article 12(1) mandates technical capability for automatic logging).\n- The direction is correct — you do need automated, tamper-resistant logging.\n- As a long-term infrastructure investment, it makes sense.\n\nBut from MY specific vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping), this proposal as written is too vague and too broad to be actionable. It's more of a strategic direction than a technically viable prototype plan. I can't proto