t don't exist. However, as a proposal within a larger program, it could be complementary.\n- \"Automatically synchronize\" - this is good because Recital 71 requires documentation to be kept \"up to date\" throughout the lifetime. Manual synchronization creates gaps.\n- The scope seems reasonable and focused on a specific compliance pain point.\n- From an institutional survival standpoint: if you're found non-compliant in an audit because your documentation was stale or inconsistent with actual system logs, that's a serious regulatory finding. This tool directly mitigates that risk.\n- Market reputation: being the first R&D institution to have automated, always-current technical documentation could be a competitive differentiator and signal compliance maturity to partners and customers.\n\nHowever, I should note some limitations:\n- It doesn't address Article 12(3) specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.) - those require specific data fields beyond generic logs.\n- \"Parsing raw system logs\" assumes a structured logging format exists upstream. The proposal is somewhat dependent on the actual logging architecture being in place.\n- It's a tooling/infrastructure project, not a policy or process change per se. But as an implementation resource commitment, it's reasonable.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point: This directly supports the traceability and documentation requirements that are central to Article 12 and Recital 71. The risk of non-compliance if this isn't done well is high (fines, market access restrictions). The proposal addresses a real gap between \"we log things\" and \"our technical documentation reflects what actually happened.\" I'd approve this but note it's a supporting tool rather than the primary compliance mechanism.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely useful for compliance operations. It doesn't create compliance on its own (the systems must still have logging capabilities), but it makes demonstrating compliance dramatically easier and reduces drift risk. I'd say around 72-78. It's valuable but somewhat derivative - it depends on the underlying logging architecture being solid first. It's a force multiplier for compliance rather than the foundation.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 75.\n",
