ntage point:\n- Technical viability: Low. The core premise is flawed — you don't need differential privacy for audit logs, and MPC would make the system impractical for real-time event logging over a system's lifetime.\n- Speed/quality of prototyping: Poor. This is open-ended research with no clear deliverable path to an implementable component. No off-the-shelf building blocks exist for this specific combination.\n\nI'll reject this. The promise score should be low — maybe 10-15. It's not a terrible idea in the abstract (privacy in logs matters), but the specific technical approach is fundamentally mismatched, and from my prototyping/engineering lens, it doesn't offer a viable path to a working solution within reasonable timelines.\n\nLet me settle on a score: I'd say 10. There's a kernel of legitimate concern (log data privacy) buried in an impractical technical approach that would consume engineering resources for years without delivering a deployable component.\n",
