        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1195\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core mechanism—using LLM-assisted summarization to generate Recital-71-grade regulatory documentation from code comments and free-form R&D notes—is technically fragile: it conflates verifiable system metadata (model versions, dataset hashes, validation results) with prose-level claims about capabilities, limitations, and risk management that no amount of comment-scraping can reliably produce, and LLM hallucination in a compliance artifact is an unacceptable failure mode. The 'real-time' framing also overstates urgency for documentation that updates at merge cadence, while the actual engineering lift (structured template mapping, provenance tracking, deterministic validation layers) would make this a multi-quarter build, not the quick administrative offload it presents as.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 28\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1195 is about a \"Dynamic Technical Documentation Pipeline\" that auto-generates technical documentation required by Recital 71 from code comments and experiment logs in real-time, using LLM-assisted summarization synced with version control.\n\nLet me think about this from my technical viability and prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- The core idea is to use LLMs to auto-generate regulatory documentation (Recital 71 requirements) from code comments and experiment logs. This is a stretch technically for several reasons:\n  - Recital 71 requires \"clear and comprehensive\" documentation covering \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" These are not things that can be reliably extracted from code comments or experiment logs alone.\n  - Code comments are often incomplete, outdated, or informal. Relying on them as a primary source for regulatory-grade documentation is technically fragile.\n  - LLM-assisted summarization introduces hallucination risk. For regulatory compliance documents, this is a critical problem — you need deterministic, verifiable outputs, not probabilistic ones. If an auditor asks \"where does this claim come from?\" and the answer is \"an LLM synthesized it,\" that's problematic.\n  - The proposal conflates two different things: (1) the actual technical documentation content (which requires expert judgment about system capabilities, limitations, risk management), and (2) the mechanical act of keeping documents in sync with code changes.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, building an LLM pipeline that maps code comments to regulatory document sections is actually a complex NLP/information extraction problem. It's not a quick win. You'd need:\n  - Structured templates for each section of Recital 71 documentation\n  - Reliable mapping from free-form code comments to structured regulatory claims\n  - Validation layers to ensure LLM outputs are factually accurate\n  - Version control sync mechanisms (this part is more straightforward)\n- The \"real-time\" aspect adds significant engineering complexity without clear proportional benefit for documentation that changes at a slower rate than code.\n\n**What's actually useful here:**\n- The underlying instinct — reducing administrative overhead by automating parts of the documentation workflow — is valid from an engineering productivity standpoint.\n- Syncing version control with a documentation portal is a well-understood technical problem (CI/CD patterns, doc-as-code).\n- However, the LLM-assisted summarization of R&D notes into regulatory-grade documentation is where the technical viability drops significantly.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability and prototyping speed/quality), this proposal has a seductive surface — \"automate docs with AI\" sounds like an easy win — but the core technical challenge (reliably generating compliance-grade documentation from informal sources using probabilistic models) is neither as tractable nor as quick to prototype as presented. The risk of producing subtly wrong regulatory documents via LLM hallucination is a genuine engineering concern that the proposal doesn't address. The \"real-time\" framing also overstates urgency for something that updates on code merges, not per-second.\n\nHowever, there IS a viable subset: a documentation sync pipeline (code changes → trigger doc update review → structured templates get populated with verifiable metadata like commit hashes, model version numbers, dataset references) is technically sound and prototypable in reasonable time. But that's NOT what the proposal actually describes — it describes LLM summarization of R&D notes into comprehensive regulatory documentation.\n\nI'm leaning toward reject. The technical viability of the specific mechanism proposed (LLM auto-generating Recital 71 docs from code comments) is low, and the prototyping timeline would be longer than implied by the \"reduces administrative load\" framing. A more honest version — a structured metadata sync with human review checkpoints — would be viable, but that's not what's being proposed here.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 25-30. There's a kernel of useful automation thinking (doc-as-code, version control sync), but the specific technical approach described is not viable for its stated purpose without significant rework that essentially becomes a different project.\n",
