        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1710\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the core hard obligation in Article 12(1)—that high-risk systems must *technically allow* automatic event recording—by providing standardised, framework-level interception that removes reliance on individual engineers to remember or manually implement logging. From a compliance and audit-readiness standpoint, a single unified middleware layer is far more defensible than ad-hoc per-project scripts: it demonstrates systematic organisational control, reduces the risk of inconsistent or missing logs across teams (a common market-surveillance finding), and can be selectively scoped to high-risk deployments without imposing overhead on non-high-risk prototyping. The main gap I note is that the proposal speaks generically of 'system events and timestamps' but does not yet specify capture of the granular data fields mandated by Article 12(3) (reference database, matched inputs, human-verifier identification under Art 14(5)) or address log integrity/retention over the system's lifetime; however, as a foundational plumbing layer it is clearly the correct architectural substrate on which those specifics can be configured.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P1710 is about a \"Unified High-Risk Logging Middleware\" — a standardized software library that automates event capture and timestamps across all AI prototypes using API wrappers for primary frameworks.\n\nLet me assess this against Article 12 requirements:\n\n**Article 12(1):** High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n- This proposal directly addresses \"automatic recording\" — it's a middleware that captures events automatically via API wrappers. This is squarely on-target.\n\n**Article 12(2):** Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for:\n(a) identifying situations presenting risk (Art 79(1)) or substantial modification\n(b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 72)\n(c) monitoring operation (Art 26(5))\n- The proposal mentions \"system events and timestamps\" but doesn't explicitly address the specific event categories in 2(a), 2(b), 2(c). It's a general-purpose logging tool. However, as infrastructure, it could be extended to capture these specific events.\n\n**Article 12(3):** For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification/verification/categorization):\n- recording of period of each use (start/end timestamps)\n- reference database against which input data was checked\n- input data for which search led to a match\n- identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art 14(5))\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps\" and \"inputs and outputs automatically.\" It addresses start/end timing somewhat. But it doesn't specifically mention the reference database, matched inputs, or human verifier identification. However, as middleware that intercepts inputs/outputs, it could be extended to capture these.\n\n**My professional vantage point (Compliance Officer):**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the \"automatic recording\" requirement of Art 12(1). This is a hard legal obligation — systems must \"technically allow for\" automatic logging.\n- Standardization across prototypes reduces compliance risk from inconsistency. If different teams log differently, you have gaps that regulators will flag.\n- The plug-and-play nature means less human error in forgetting to implement logging. Compliance is often about process reliability.\n- Reduces the burden on engineers who might otherwise deprioritize logging — this is a real practical concern. Engineers building prototypes tend to skip logging.\n- \"Without modifying core model logic\" is good — it means compliance infrastructure doesn't interfere with R&D, reducing friction and the temptation for teams to bypass it.\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal says \"all AI prototypes.\" Article 12 applies specifically to HIGH-RISK AI systems, not all prototypes. This could be over-engineering or misallocation of resources if applied to non-high-risk prototypes. However, having a unified tool that can be selectively enabled for high-risk deployments is actually sensible — you don't want to retrofit logging after deciding a system is high-risk.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic: \"system events and timestamps.\" Article 12(3) requires very specific data fields (reference database, matched inputs, human verifier IDs). The middleware as described may not capture these without additional configuration/development. But as a foundation, it's a reasonable starting point.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — this implies long-term storage and integrity of logs. The proposal doesn't address retention, immutability, or archival. However, that might be out of scope for a middleware layer (storage could be separate infrastructure).\n- From a compliance audit perspective, having a standardized logging architecture is actually quite valuable. Regulators can verify that logging is systematic rather than ad-hoc.\n\n**Institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- If the lab is found non-compliant on Article 12 during an audit or market surveillance, the consequences include fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations) and potential withdrawal from EU market.\n- A unified logging infrastructure demonstrates organizational commitment to compliance — this matters for market reputation, especially when selling AI systems to enterprises who will ask about your compliance posture.\n- The fact that it's \"plug-and-play\" reduces the risk of inconsistent implementation across teams, which is a common audit finding.\n\n**Decision:** This clears my bar. It directly addresses a core obligation (Art 12(1)), standardizes compliance infrastructure, and reduces practical risk of non-compliance due to human error or inconsistency. While it needs extension for specific Art 12(3) fields, as a foundational layer it's highly relevant. I approve it with moderate-high confidence.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my core concern (automatic logging is the first and most fundamental requirement of Article 12). The slight deductions are for: (1) not explicitly addressing the specific data fields in Art 12(3), (2) the \"all AI prototypes\" scope being broader than strictly necessary, (3) no mention of log integrity/retention. But as a foundational infrastructure piece, it's strongly aligned with what I need to see for compliance readiness.\n",
