ped**: It's not a moonshot. Extending CI/CD with test suites is well-understood engineering. Low risk of failure, bounded scope.\n\n**Arguments for concern/rejection:**\n1. **Complexity of \"simulated high-risk events\"**: Triggering simulated high-risk scenarios that are realistic enough to validate all logging requirements could be non-trivial. What counts as a valid simulation? This might require domain expertise per use case (Annex III categories vary). Could become more expensive than anticipated.\n2. **Maintenance burden**: As the Act evolves, guidelines change, or new Annex III categories apply, test suites need updating. But this is relatively minor compared to manual compliance checking.\n3. **Scope creep risk**: \"All required metadata\" across all high-risk system types could be broad. But the proposal is scoped to logging capabilities specifically (Article 12), not full Article 11 technical documentation or other requirements.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is an excellent proposal. It:\n- Leverages existing CI/CD infrastructure (no new platform needed)\n- Converts recurring manual compliance labor into a one-time automated check\n- Scales with the number of systems without proportional cost increase\n- Protects R&D throughput (scientific ambition) by not slowing down deployment for manual review\n- Is well-scoped to Article 12 specifically\n- Has low implementation risk (CI/CD extensions are standard practice)\n\nThe \"simulated high-risk events\" part is the main area where costs could exceed expectations, but even so, it's still far more efficient than manual compliance verification per deployment. The investment-to-return ratio is very favorable from a resource-efficiency standpoint.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses efficient use of existing infrastructure, scales well, and protects R&D throughput. I'd put it around 82-85. The 