        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen a single proposal (P1759) from my perspective as Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P1759: Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline**\n\nThe proposal describes creating a system that automatically generates and updates technical documentation required by Recital 71 using version control data. It integrates experiment tracking tools with a template generator that maps research metadata to regulatory requirements for capabilities and limitations. The goal is to eliminate human error in record-keeping while keeping documentation up to date.\n\nLet me assess this from my vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- This addresses a real ongoing compliance burden. Recital 71 explicitly states \"The technical documentation should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" Manual updating is resource-intensive and error-prone. Automating this could save significant researcher time that would otherwise go to admin/compliance work rather than actual R&D.\n- However, building an automated pipeline itself requires significant upfront investment: developing the template generator, mapping logic, integration with existing tools, testing, maintenance. The question is whether the lab already has experiment tracking tools and version control infrastructure in place (likely yes for an R&D institution) or whether this would require new procurement.\n- If we're already using experiment trackers (like MLflow, Weights & Biases, etc.) and version control (Git), then the marginal cost of building a mapping layer to regulatory templates could be moderate. But \"automatically generate\" technical documentation that satisfies regulatory scrutiny is harder than it sounds — regulators want comprehensible, clear, comprehensive forms. Auto-generated docs might not meet that bar without significant human review anyway.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The proposal frames compliance as a cost center (which it is), but the automation angle means we're investing in infrastructure that reduces recurring costs. This is reasonable from a budget perspective — one-time build cost vs. ongoing manual documentation effort across multiple AI systems over their lifetimes.\n- However, there's a risk of over-engineering. If we have 2-3 high-risk AI systems, the documentation burden might be manageable without full automation. The proposal doesn't specify scale. Building an elaborate pipeline for a small number of systems could be wasteful.\n- On the other hand, if the lab is scaling up R&D and producing multiple high-risk systems, the compounding documentation burden becomes significant.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is actually a strong point for the proposal. It explicitly leverages EXISTING tools: version control data and experiment tracking tools. This is exactly the kind of \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" that my domain cares about. We're not buying new systems; we're adding a mapping/generation layer on top of what we already have.\n- The integration approach (connecting existing metadata to regulatory templates) is a sensible architectural choice rather than building parallel data stores.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Eliminates human error\" is too strong a claim. Automated generation still needs validation. Regulatory documentation requires judgment about what's relevant, how to characterize limitations, etc. A template generator can't fully capture nuanced scientific assessments.\n- The proposal doesn't address maintenance of the pipeline itself — who keeps the templates updated as regulations are interpreted? What happens when our research processes change?\n- There's a risk this becomes an over-investment in compliance infrastructure at the expense of actual R&D output, if the build scope creeps.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a well-targeted proposal that leverages existing infrastructure (experiment trackers, version control) to reduce ongoing compliance overhead. The resource-allocation logic is sound: one-time development investment to eliminate recurring manual documentation work. It's not a flashy scientific ambition project; it's practical infrastructure that frees researchers from admin burden. However, I need to be careful about scope — the \"automated\" claim may overpromise what a template generator can do for nuanced regulatory documentation, and there's maintenance cost not accounted for.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's efficient use of existing tools, addresses a real recurring cost, and doesn't require major new funding beyond development effort. The promise score should reflect that it's good but not perfect — the \"automated\" framing may be slightly overblown, and maintenance costs are understated, but the core idea is sound and resource-efficient.\n\nI'd say: approve, with a promise around 72-78. It's genuinely useful, leverages existing infrastructure well, but has scope-risk and the automation claim needs tempering. Let me settle on 74.\n",
