lready address most of the privacy concerns without needing DP or MPC. The \"conflict\" may be somewhat overstated for the actual regulatory requirement — you can log events with encrypted fields that are only decryptable by authorized auditors.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a research project, not an implementation project. The language says \"investigating,\" \"explore,\" \"prototype.\" But from my vantage point, committing scarce implementation resources to a research project on DP/MPC for logging is very risky. The prototyping timeline would be extremely long — MPC alone could take 12-24 months just to get a basic working prototype.\n- Differential privacy for structured event logs (timestamps, database references, person IDs) is not straightforward. You'd need to define sensitivity parameters, calibrate noise budgets, and then prove that the resulting logs still satisfy Article 12's specific recording requirements. This is a research problem with uncertain outcomes.\n- The \"prototype\" described — a system that \"proves compliance without exposing raw PII\" — is vague on what \"proves\" means technically. Zero-knowledge proofs? Verifiable computation? These are even harder to prototype quickly.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping, this proposal has significant concerns:\n1. The core technologies (DP for structured logs, MPC) are extremely complex to prototype and have uncertain timelines.\n2. There's a real risk that the \"privacy-preserving\" approach conflicts with the specific granularity required by Article 12(3).\n3. Simpler engineering solutions (encryption, access control, data minimization, tokenized references) likely address 90% of the actual privacy concern without the research uncertainty.\n4. As a research project committed from scarce implementation resources, the risk-to-reward is poor for my function.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge that there IS a