eader\n\nBut there are significant risks:\n- It's framed as R&D/research, not as a compliance implementation\n- LLM-based generation of compliance documents introduces accuracy risks that I need to manage\n- No mention of how outputs will be validated for regulatory purposes\n- The \"small team\" allocation is vague — is this pulling resources AWAY from direct compliance implementation?\n\nOverall assessment: This is a reasonably well-aligned idea with my domain, but it needs guardrails. As stated, it's a research project that happens to touch on compliance rather than a compliance-driven engineering effort. I can see the value in automating traceability and report generation — it genuinely helps meet Art. 12 obligations at scale. But as Compliance Officer, I need to ensure any such tool outputs are validated before they become part of our regulatory record.\n\nI'll lean toward approving this but with moderate confidence, because:\n- The core idea (automated traceability from logs) directly serves Article 12\n- It could enhance market reputation\n- But the research framing and LLM-based approach introduce risks I'd need to manage\n- It's not a bad use of resources IF properly scoped\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 62-70. The idea aligns with my domain but the execution as described has gaps that concern me from a compliance standpoint. It's promising enough to approve (it does address real needs) but not something I'd \"back without hesitation\" because of the research framing and accuracy concerns.\n\nLet me settle on: Approve, promise 65. The idea is genuinely aligned with Article 12 obligations and could strengthen both compliance posture and market reputation. But the research/LLM framing introduces risks that require careful scoping, and it's not a substitute for actually implementing the logging capabilities required by Art. 12(1) and (3).\n",
