llectual content is applying known drift/anomaly detection methods to compliance logs. It's useful work but not pushing the knowledge frontier significantly.\n\nI'd say this is a solid engineering proposal with modest research elements. From my vantage point focused on knowledge frontier and intellectual merit, it doesn't quite clear the bar for a high promise score, but it's not worthless either - there are some interesting sub-questions embedded in it. I'll reject it as a research priority because the core intellectual contribution is incremental application of existing methods, though I'll give it a moderate promise score acknowledging the potential sub-problems.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, with a promise around 30-40. It's not frontier research, it's applied engineering with some interesting sub-problems that could be extracted but aren't what's actually proposed here.\n",
