ments than other high-risk categories). The proposal says \"standardizes event logging across all high-risk model prototypes\" which might be premature - the requirements differ by Annex III category.\n- The middleware approach could add latency to API calls during prototyping, potentially slowing iteration cycles if not done carefully. But this is a well-known engineering challenge with known solutions (async logging, sampling).\n- Building an internal library vs. using existing tools (OpenTelemetry, MLflow's built-in tracking): the proposal doesn't justify why we can't build on top of OpenTelemetry or similar standards. However, for compliance-specific fields (like the specific data points in Article 12(3)), a custom layer makes sense.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Guarantees compliance with Article 12\" - this is overclaiming. A logging framework helps but doesn't guarantee compliance by itself. Compliance also depends on what events you choose to log (Article 12(2) says \"events relevant for\" specific purposes), the quality of the data, retention policies, etc. But as a proposal statement, it's aspirational rather than technical.\n- The scope is somewhat broad (\"all high-risk model prototypes\") which could lead to scope creep and slow delivery.\n- \"Immutable ledger\" without specification - if this means actual blockchain or distributed ledger technology, that's overkill and would slow prototyping significantly. If it means append-only with cryptographic hash chaining, it's fine.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point: This is technically viable and addresses a real need. The core concept (middleware logging layer in MLOps) is well-understood and buildable. It has the potential to improve prototyping speed by automating compliance tracking. The main concerns are scope breadth and the \"immutable ledger\" terminology potentially overcomplicating things, but these are manageable with proper engineering scoping.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar for technical viability. It's a well-motivated internal tool that would make our lives easier. The promise score: I'd give it maybe 72-78. It's solid and doable, the core idea is sound, but there are some scope/implementation risks that need to be managed. Not a 100 because \"immutable ledger\" could go down a rabbit hole, and the blanket \"all high-risk model prototypes\" scope might slow initial delivery.\n\nLet me settle on: decision = approve, promise = 74.\n",
