t suggests it's not a massive time sink if the rubric is well-designed.\n- The \"standardized rubric\" is key — if it works, most decisions can be made quickly and only edge cases need deep deliberation.\n- There's a risk this becomes bureaucratic overhead that slows R&D. But the counterargument is strong: misclassifying high-risk systems as low-risk creates massive compliance exposure (fines up to 7% of global turnover), so the cost of false negatives dwarfs the committee time.\n- It's a prerequisite for efficient resource allocation — you can't allocate logging infrastructure efficiently if you don't know what needs it.\n\n**From my specific vantage point:**\n- Resource-allocation efficiency: STRONG fit. This is literally about directing scarce implementation resources to where they're legally required and avoiding waste elsewhere.\n- Scientific ambition vs funding constraints: The committee uses lead scientists' time, but it's bounded (quarterly). It protects scientific freedom by ensuring low-risk projects aren't burdened with unnecessary compliance overhead.\n- Effective use of R&D infrastructure: By preventing over-application of logging requirements to systems that don't need them, existing compute and storage infrastructure is preserved for actual research needs.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value, relatively low-cost proposal from my perspective. It's a gating mechanism that makes all other compliance investments (logging infrastructure, technical documentation systems) more efficient by ensuring they're targeted correctly. The quarterly cadence and charter-stage timing keep the ongoing cost manageable. The main risk is committee overhead, but it's modest compared to the alternative of either over-compliance (waste) or under-compliance (fines).\n\nI'd approve this. Promise score: This is very well-aligned with my mandate. It's not flashy but it's fundamentally sound resource governance. I'd say around 82-85. It's high value but the implementation details (committee size, rubric quality) matter and there's some overhead concern. I'll go with 83.\n",
