s is a mandatory integration requirement. There's a risk of it becoming a bottleneck or a tax on deployment velocity if not done well. But the proposal frames it as reducing manual effort, which is net positive.\n- The scope says \"across all high-risk AI pipelines\" — we need to be clear about what counts as high-risk under the Act. Not every pipeline we run will be high-risk. Building for ALL pipelines might over-invest relative to actual regulatory exposure. However, a standardized layer that can be toggled on/off based on risk classification could be efficient.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — it doesn't specify what \"immutable database\" means in terms of existing infrastructure (do we already have WORM storage? Do we need to buy new?), nor does it address retention periods or data volume management for lifetime logging.\n- It addresses 12(1) well but is lighter on 12(3) specifics (reference databases, input data matches, identification of natural persons in verification). For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, more targeted logging is needed.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- The \"build once, deploy many\" approach is efficient for our constrained budget. One middleware team effort vs. N teams each building their own logging.\n- It protects research time by automating what would otherwise be manual compliance work.\n- However, the upfront build cost is non-trivial and the proposal doesn't acknowledge whether we can repurpose existing observability infrastructure.\n- The requirement that ALL engineers integrate it adds a coordination cost but is amortized over all deployments.\n\n**Verdict:** This is a solid, efficient approach to compliance that aligns well with resource-allocation principles. It's not a moonshot or an exploratory bet — it's a necessary engineering investment that protects the lab from compliance risk and frees researchers from manual logging tasks. The main gap is whether we're building from scratch vs. extending existi