his requires dedicated engineering time that could go toward other R&D work. But given compliance is mandatory, someone has to do it anyway; doing it well with automation vs. manually is a clear efficiency gain.\n- Scope risk: \"dynamic templates that satisfy the requirements of Recital 71\" — Recital 71 lists many categories (general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes, risk-management system). Not all of these will be automatically derivable from MLflow metadata and version control. Some content requires human judgment and narrative writing. The proposal might overpromise on automation coverage.\n- However, even partial automation (covering the structured/metadata-heavy portions) would save significant time.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- This is a high-leverage project: one-time build cost → recurring savings across all high-risk systems in the lab's portfolio.\n- It uses existing infrastructure (MLflow, git/VCS) rather than requiring new data collection systems or new tools to be purchased.\n- It directly serves compliance without diverting scientific talent from research.\n- The \"promise\" is high because it's practical, scoped, and addresses a real ongoing obligation.\n\n**Decision:** This clearly clears the bar. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, reduces long-term resource drain on scientists, and has a clear regulatory driver. I'd approve it.\n\n**Promise score:** From my vantage point, this is very strong. It's not 100 because there's some scope risk (not everything in Recital 71 will auto-generate cleanly from MLflow data), and the engineering effort required needs to be scoped carefully to avoid gold-plating. But it's clearly high-value. I'd say around 82-85. Let me settle on 83.\n\nLet me write my response now.\n",
