istency\" language shows awareness of the regulatory driver.\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal says \"mandatory event logging\" but doesn't specify whether it covers ALL Art. 12(2) categories or just basic metadata injection. The SDK needs to be designed around the specific event types required by Art. 12(2)(a), (b), (c).\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems, Art. 12(3) has very specific minimum requirements. A generic \"wrapper\" might not capture those specifics unless explicitly designed for them.\n- The proposal focuses on the *technical* logging capability but doesn't mention retention periods, immutability, access controls, or audit trails — all of which are implicit compliance concerns (Recital 71 mentions keeping records throughout lifetime).\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is a long-duration requirement. The SDK needs to ensure logs persist and aren't lost when projects end or systems are deprecated.\n- No mention of how this integrates with technical documentation requirements (which Recital 71 also emphasizes) — though that might be out of scope for a logging SDK.\n\nOverall: This is a solid, practical proposal that addresses the core mechanical requirement of Art. 12(1). Standardization is genuinely important for compliance because it reduces variability and risk of gaps. The auto-injection of metadata is directly relevant. However, as written, it's somewhat thin — it reads more like a convenience wrapper than a comprehensive compliance logging solution. But the core idea (unified SDK ensuring consistent, automatic logging) is exactly what a compliance officer would want to see implemented.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is a \"yes\" on direction but the implementation details matter enormously for actual compliance. The proposal as stated is a reasonable foundation. I'd approve it because getting standardization in place early is critical — you can iterate and add specificity later, but if every team builds their own logging from scratch, you'll have 1