astructure has an opportunity cost.\n- The middleware approach is pragmatic — it doesn't block scientific work, it just requires integration. This is reasonable. It's not a \"we'll spend 18 months building a perfect system\" proposal; it's two engineers building a shared API and library. That's bounded scope.\n- However, the mandate to integrate during development phase could slow down project starts. If a new research idea needs to get on the compute cluster quickly, having to first wire in logging middleware adds lead time. This is a minor concern but worth noting.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Integrating into the \"compute cluster\" is smart — it leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new hardware or cloud spend. The logging events are likely stored on the same cluster's storage.\n- A shared library/API approach is more efficient than each team spinning up their own logging stack (which might involve different databases, different schemas, redundant server resources).\n- However, \"integrated into our compute cluster\" could create a single point of failure or bottleneck if the middleware has issues. But this is more of an engineering risk than a resource-allocation concern per se.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events over the lifetime. A centralized middleware ensures this happens uniformly and can't be accidentally skipped by individual project teams. This is actually a compliance robustness benefit — if each team does their own thing, some might miss requirements or implement them inconsistently.\n- The standardized data formats for timestamps and system events directly address the \"comprehensible information\" requirement in Recital 71.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a well-scoped, resource-efficient proposal. Two engineers to build a shared layer that eliminates N separate implementations across projects is classic economies of scale. The integration into existing compute cluster infrastructure means minimal new capital expenditure. The main risk is the mandatory adoption adding friction to research workflows and the opportunity cost of two FTEs. But for a lab with multiple high-risk AI systems (which the EU AI Act context implies), this is clearly more efficient than decentralized approaches.\n\nThe scope is bounded — it's not \"build a data platform\" or \"rearchitect our entire compute infrastructure.\" It's an API and library. That's a two-person, maybe 2-3 month build with ongoing maintenance. Reasonable.\n\nI'd lean toward approving this. The resource efficiency argument is strong: if we have even 3+ high-risk projects, the shared middleware saves significant duplicated effort. The integration into existing infrastructure (compute cluster) means low capital cost. Two engineers is a modest and bounded commitment. The main downside — workflow friction for new projects — is manageable with good API design.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's solid, well-scoped, addresses the core efficiency concern directly. Not a 90+ because of the opportunity cost consideration and the mandate on all new repos (which could be softened to \"all high-risk project repos\" specifically). But it clears my bar comfortably.\n\nLet me settle on 74.\n",
