ary evidence for regulatory compliance,\" \"prove compliance during regulatory inspections\"). This is an administrative/bureaucratic framing, not a research-driven one.\n- The mandatory gate of \"researchers must update technical specifications and validation results before any model deployment stage\" could be seen as a bureaucratic impediment to research iteration. In active R&D, models are deployed in stages for testing, retraining happens frequently, and strict documentation gates before each deployment can slow down the exploratory process.\n- It doesn't address the knowledge frontier at all - it's purely backward-looking (recording what was done) rather than forward-looking (enabling new capabilities).\n- The \"living documentation\" concept is well-known in software engineering but doesn't contribute to advancing AI research itself.\n- No mention of how this contributes to scientific understanding, publishable findings, or pushing the boundaries of what AI systems can do.\n- It's essentially a compliance infrastructure proposal dressed up with slightly more sophisticated language than \"we need to keep our docs updated.\"\n\n**Intellectual merit:** The intellectual content here is modest. Version-controlled documentation and audit trails are well-established practices in software engineering and data management. There's no novel research question being addressed, no methodological innovation in AI development itself.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:** Minimal. This doesn't open new research directions, doesn't create tools that advance the field, doesn't generate publishable insights about how to build better AI systems. It creates a compliance artifact.\n\n**Knowledge frontier:** The proposal does not push any boundary of what we know or can do with AI. It's an administrative process.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: good documentation IS important for research integrity and reproducibility. And the idea of tracking algorithm evolution could have some value for