ompetent engineering but not intellectually meritorious research in the sense of advancing understanding of AI systems, their behavior, or new scientific capabilities. It's infrastructure.\n\n3. Broader scientific impact: Would this be publishable? Would it advance the field of AI? A regulatory compliance logging layer for an internal R&D lab is not going to contribute to the broader scientific community in any meaningful way. It's an internal operational tool. Even if published, \"we built a middleware that logs events per EU regulations\" would have very limited audience and impact beyond compliance engineering circles.\n\nHowever, I should be fair: could there be any research angle? Perhaps if the logging infrastructure enables better post-market monitoring analytics or provides new insights into model behavior over time... but the proposal as stated doesn't frame it that way. It's purely framed as an efficiency/compliance solution (\"removes burden,\" \"prevents duplication,\" \"optimizes storage\"). There's no mention of research questions, novel methods, or scientific discovery.\n\nMy assessment: From a researcher's vantage point focused on knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact, this proposal does not clear the bar. It is an operational engineering project that, while useful for compliance and internal efficiency, does not advance scientific knowledge, does not have significant intellectual merit in the research sense, and has negligible broader scientific impact.\n\nDecision: reject\nPromise score: This should be low from my vantage point. Maybe 10-15. It's a reasonable engineering project, but it simply isn't what I'm looking for in terms of advancing knowledge or contributing to science. I'll say around 12 — it has some minor tangential value (the logging infrastructure could theoretically enable research on model behavior tracking), but the proposal as framed is purely operational.\n",
