ing**: The proposal mentions \"standardized set of API hooks and database schemas\" — if these are designed too rigidly or for edge cases not yet encountered, engineering time could be wasted on unnecessary complexity. But a reasonable initial scope (timestamps, inputs, user IDs as mentioned) is aligned with Article 12(3)'s minimum requirements.\n3. **Maintenance burden**: A centralized service becomes a single point of failure and requires ongoing maintenance. However, this is still cheaper than maintaining N separate logging systems across projects.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- This is the textbook example of \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — you build once, deploy everywhere.\n- It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by preventing N teams from each spending weeks building their own logging.\n- The balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints is well-served because researchers integrate with one call rather than diverting significant time to compliance plumbing.\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded — it mentions capturing \"timestamps, inputs, and user IDs\" which maps directly to Article 12(3) requirements without overreaching into speculative capabilities.\n\nThis is a strong, well-scoped infrastructure investment that maximizes leverage per unit of engineering resource spent. I would approve this readily. It's not a research moonshot; it's operational efficiency that enables compliance at scale. The promise score should be high — maybe 88-92. There are minor risks around scope creep in the \"standardized schemas\" part and the need for dedicated maintenance, but fundamentally this is the right architectural call from a resource-efficiency standpoint.\n\nI'll give it an 88. It's very promising but I'm not at 100 because: (a) the upfront build cost is non-trivial and needs to be staffed before any project benefits; (b) \"database schemas\" could invite over-engineering if not scoped tightly; (c) centralized servic