erence DB, input data, verifier IDs). The proposal doesn't argue that what the regulation requires is insufficient — it proposes going further for intellectual/scientific reasons. That's a stretch of resource allocation when compliance itself demands significant effort.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: Ablation studies and sensitivity analyses require substantial compute resources, data engineering, and expert time. These are exactly the scarce resources needed to actually implement the logging systems that Article 12 mandates across all high-risk AI systems in the lab's portfolio. Diverting them to a research project that produces \"insights\" rather than compliant systems is questionable from an efficiency standpoint.\n\n**Assessment**: The proposal is scientifically interesting but misaligned with the immediate compliance imperative. The regulation's requirements are fairly specific and prescriptive — you need logging capabilities, minimum fields for certain systems, automatic recording over lifetime. A research project to determine \"which internal state changes are most predictive\" is a nice-to-have that could inform future optimization of logging priorities, but it doesn't address the core compliance gap. Moreover, ablation studies on complex AI systems are expensive and time-consuming with uncertain timelines — the lab may not have results before regulatory deadlines hit. The \"intellectual merit\" framing is appealing to researchers but from a resource-allocation standpoint, this competes directly with building the actual logging infrastructure that regulators will audit.\n\nThat said, there's a modest argument: if the lab operates many different high-risk AI systems, understanding which events matter could help prioritize limited logging storage and processing resources (you can't log everything at infinite granularity — storage costs are real). But even this is speculative and the regulation doesn't require you to be selective about