res vs. a gold-plated solution.\n- Article 12(2) says logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for the three purposes listed. It doesn't explicitly mandate a \"centralized visualization platform\" or \"real-time\" alerting — it mandates that logs exist and are available. A dashboard is one way to surface them, but could be over-engineering relative to what's strictly required.\n- However, Article 12(2)(b) does reference \"facilitating the post-market monitoring referred to in Article 72,\" which implies some active monitoring capability. So there's a regulatory basis for going beyond mere storage.\n- The phrase \"transforms passive record-keeping into an active tool\" is somewhat aspirational marketing language, but the core idea — building alerting on top of mandated logs — is reasonable and proportionate.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe key question is: are we committing scarce resources to something that (a) is genuinely required or strongly implied by the Act, (b) leverages infrastructure we're already building, and (c) has a clear cost-benefit ratio?\n\nI think this clears the bar. We MUST build logging per Article 12(1). The telemetry pipeline is non-negotiable. Adding an alerting/visualization layer on top of data we're already capturing is a marginal incremental investment that:\n- Serves Article 12(2)(b) \"facilitating post-market monitoring\"\n- Reduces risk of regulatory non-compliance (cost avoidance)\n- Leverages existing R&D infrastructure (we likely have observability tooling in production)\n- The \"centralized\" aspect is efficient — one dashboard rather than per-system ad-hoc checks\n\nMy concern would be scope creep: the proposal says \"real-time logs to identify drifts or anomalies\" which could balloon into a full data-science/analytics project. But as stated, flagging \"specific event patterns defined in Article 12(2)\" is bounded and tractable. I'll approve it but note that the promise score should reflect so