lent from a compliance standpoint because it creates an institutional control point.\n5. \"Over the lifetime of the system\" (Art 12(1)) — a shared library maintained by engineering ensures that even as systems evolve, the logging layer remains intact and updatable. Recital 71 says documentation should be kept \"up to date throughout the lifetime.\"\n\nConcerns/Risks:\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, system events, and input references\" but doesn't explicitly address all of Article 12(3) requirements (reference database, specific match data, identification of natural persons for verification under Art 14(5)). However, this is a framework — the modular API could be extended to cover those. The proposal says \"across various model architectures\" which suggests extensibility.\n- \"Guarantees baseline regulatory compliance\" — from a compliance perspective, I'm slightly wary of the word \"guarantee.\" Compliance is never guaranteed; it's managed. But as an aspirational statement for a framework that makes compliance the default rather than the exception, this is directionally correct and actually desirable.\n- The framework approach means one team (engineering) owns the logging layer. From a compliance standpoint, I need to ensure there's governance over what events are logged, retention periods, access controls, etc. But the proposal doesn't preclude adding those layers.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:** A standardized framework reduces the risk that individual projects miss logging requirements, which could lead to non-compliance findings, enforcement actions, or market access issues. This is a strong institutional protection mechanism.\n\n**Market reputation angle:** Having a unified compliance infrastructure signals to clients, regulators, and partners that the institution takes AI Act obligations seriously at a systemic level rather than as per-project afterthoughts. This is reputationally positive.\n\n**Regulatory compliance angle:** This directly operationalizes Article 12(1) and (2). The CI/CD integration creates an enforceable control. Standardized formats support the \"clear and comprehensive\" documentation requirement in Recital 71. It's a high-value, directly on-point proposal.\n\nThis is clearly a strong proposal from my compliance perspective. I would approve it with high confidence. The promise score should be very high — maybe 90-93. The slight deduction is because the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly enumerate all Article 12(3) data fields or address retention/access governance, but those are implementation details that would naturally fall out of building the framework under compliance oversight.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: 92\n",
