        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2034\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The underlying problem—knowing when a prototype crosses into high-risk territory—is real and I'd want clarity, but the proposed mechanism (a quarterly multidisciplinary review board) is misaligned with prototyping cadence: R&D tools can shift category between sprints or even within a sprint, so a 90-day governance gate either leaves me running unlogged past the trigger or forces me to pre-wire logging into every experiment as a precaution. From a technical-viability standpoint, the proposal offers no engineer-actionable signal (e.g., an operational deployment boundary, a data-classification tag in CI) that I could encode into a pipeline toggle; it is purely an administrative process whose output arrives too late and with too much meeting overhead to preserve the iteration speed I need.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 27\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2034 is about creating a \"High Risk Classification Trigger Protocol\" — an internal rubric with a quarterly multidisciplinary review board to determine when a research prototype crosses the threshold into \"high-risk system\" territory requiring Article 12 logging.\n\nLet me think about this from my technical/prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- The core problem it addresses is real: in an R&D institution, prototypes evolve, and at some point they may cross the line from \"research tool\" to \"high-risk AI system.\" Without a clear trigger, engineers either over-comply (slowing prototyping) or under-comply (creating compliance gaps).\n- However, the proposed solution — a quarterly review board evaluating milestones against EU AI Act criteria — has significant technical/prototyping concerns:\n  - Quarterly cadence is too slow for R&D. Prototypes can shift categories in days or weeks during active development sprints. A quarterly gate means you're either running without logging when you should have it, or over-logging when you don't need to.\n  - \"Multidisciplinary review board\" adds meeting overhead and decision latency that directly conflicts with speed of prototyping.\n  - The rubric itself is an administrative/legal process, not a technical solution. It doesn't give me as an engineer a clear, automatable signal for when logging needs to kick in. I'd prefer something like: \"when the system is deployed beyond the lab boundary\" or \"when it processes live data of natural persons\" — operational triggers that can be encoded into CI/CD pipelines.\n  - The proposal doesn't address HOW the logging would technically work once triggered. It's purely a governance gate.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- A quarterly review board is a bureaucratic bottleneck. If I'm iterating on a prototype and it hits a milestone that might make it high-risk, I have to wait up to 3 months for the board to convene? That's terrible for prototyping velocity.\n- Alternatively, if I err on the side of caution and assume it IS high-risk until the board says otherwise, that adds logging infrastructure to every prototype, which slows things down significantly.\n- The \"preserving scientific agility\" claim in the text is somewhat at odds with adding a formal quarterly gate process.\n\n**Technical viability concerns:**\n- The classification boundary between \"research prototype\" and \"high-risk system\" is often not cleanly determined by internal milestones — it depends on deployment context, intended purpose (as defined by the deployer), and actual use patterns. An internal board may not have full visibility into how a tool gets used downstream.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what technical artifacts or criteria the rubric would actually evaluate. \"Evaluates project milestones against EU AI Act criteria\" is vague — which criteria? How do you encode \"intended purpose\" into a checklist?\n\n**What I'd want instead (for context, not to compare):**\n- Operational triggers embedded in deployment pipelines: if the system goes beyond lab IP, processes personal data, or is made available to external users → logging activates automatically.\n- A lightweight, engineer-maintained classification tag in the project management system that can be updated per-sprint, not per-quarter.\n- Technical infrastructure for logging that's modular — easy to toggle on/off based on the classification state.\n\n**Decision:** This proposal is primarily a governance/administrative mechanism, not a technical solution. From my prototyping-speed and technical-viability vantage point, it introduces process overhead (quarterly meetings, board decisions) without providing actionable technical triggers for when logging infrastructure should activate. It's too slow, too bureaucratic for the R&D cadence, and doesn't give me engineer-level guidance on implementation. However, the underlying problem (knowing WHEN to apply Article 12) is real and important — a team that doesn't know when compliance kicks in will either over-build or under-build logging. So it's not worthless, but as proposed, it doesn't serve my function well.\n\nI'll lean toward reject with a moderate promise score — the problem is real but the solution as framed is misaligned with engineering/prototyping needs. Maybe 25-35 range. It addresses a necessary concern but in a way that would slow me down rather than help me. Let me settle on 28.\n",
