ters, dataset versions). Article 12's core obligation is about *automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system* — meaning operational logs during deployment, not just development records. This is a critical distinction. Article 12(1) says \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" The proposal seems to address Recital 71's documentation requirements but may underemphasize the Article 12 operational logging requirement.\n- However, the recital does say both are needed: \"This requires keeping records and the availability of technical documentation... Furthermore, high-risk AI systems should technically allow for the automatic recording of events, by means of logs, over the duration of the lifetime of the system.\" So Recital 71 encompasses both the documentation AND the logging. The proposal's title says it \"satisfies Recital 71\" but its mechanism (version control snapshots during experiments) is more about the technical documentation side than the operational logging side.\n- That said, as a compliance officer, I should note: this proposal addresses a *major* piece of the compliance puzzle (the technical documentation and lifecycle record), even if it doesn't fully cover Article 12's operational logging requirement. It's still highly valuable.\n- The \"clear and comprehensive form\" requirement from Recital 71 — automated snapshots might produce raw data that isn't necessarily in a \"comprehensible\" or \"clear and comprehensive\" format for auditors. There's a gap between capturing hyperparameters in JSON and presenting them as comprehensible documentation. But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal direction.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- Without automated documentation, we face enormous risk of non-compliance at audit time. Regulators can request technical documentation under various provisions. If we're relying on manual retrospective reconstruction, we will fail audits and face penalties (up to 7% global turnover for the most serious violations).\n- This is a foundational infrastructure investment that reduces ongoing compliance costs and risk.\n\n**From market reputation perspective:**\n- Having robust automated documentation demonstrates to business partners, customers, and regulators that we take AI governance seriously. It's a differentiator in tenders and partnerships.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal that directly addresses core obligations under Recital 71. While it may not fully cover the operational logging aspect of Article 12(1)-(3), it addresses the technical documentation and lifecycle tracking requirement which is equally critical. The automation approach is the right call for an R&D institution generating many experiments. I would approve this with a high promise score, noting that it should be complemented by (not confused with) the operational logging system required by Article 12(1)-(3).\n\nMy decision: APPROVE. This is directly on-mission for my function.\n",
