mpact. While it indirectly supports research by protecting budgets, my mandate is to evaluate proposals on their contribution to research quality and scientific progress. A budget allocation model for compliance engineering, even a well-designed one, falls outside what I can endorse from my vantage point.\n\nPromise score: Maybe 25-30. It's not useless (it removes a distraction), but it's far from something I'd \"back without hesitation\" for its scientific contribution. Let me go with 28.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The instruction says \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposal that does not.\" My bar is: does this advance the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, or broader scientific impact? A funding model for compliance doesn't do any of those things directly. It's orthogonal to my domain. I should reject it.\n\nPromise: 25 seems fair. It has some indirect value (protecting research time from being eaten by unplanned compliance work), but it's fundamentally an administrative proposal, not a research one.\n",
