le compared to building the actual logging infrastructure itself.\n\n**Quality of Prototyping:**\n- It's a natural complement to whatever logging system you build - it ensures quality from day one rather than retrofitting.\n- It's test-driven in nature, which aligns with good engineering practice.\n- The scope is well-bounded: you know exactly what fields are mandatory (Art. 12(3) for Annex III 1(a), and the more general requirements of Art. 12(2)).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Simulates high-risk events\" - this requires access to the system's internals or at least its API. For a general-purpose suite across multiple systems, you'd need adapters per system. This adds maintenance burden.\n- The proposal says it checks \"mandatory fields required by Article 12\" but Art. 12(3) only has explicit field requirements for Annex III 1(a). For other high-risk categories, the requirements are more principle-based (Art. 12(2)(a)-(c)), which is harder to automate into a fixed test suite. The proposal somewhat overstates what can be mechanically verified for non-Annex-III-1(a) systems.\n- However, even for the general case, you can check structural requirements: are logs being produced? Do they capture temporal information? Can they identify risk situations? These are harder to automate but partially verifiable.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a technically viable, well-scoped engineering project. It's essentially a test harness / schema validator with CI integration. The core concept is sound and the implementation path is clear. It's not a research risk - this is known engineering. It directly supports speed/quality by catching logging defects early in development rather than at compliance audit time.\n\nThe main technical challenge is scope: how general vs. specific should it be? If scoped to Annex III 1(a) systems (which have explicit field requirements), it's very straightforward. For broader applicability, you'd need more flexible assertion frameworks. 