bal open standard through a consortium is a multi-year, high-overhead endeavor that:\n- Consumes scarce engineering resources on coordination rather than implementation\n- Has no clear funding model or timeline to completion\n- Risks never delivering the actual compliance artifact our systems need (the logs themselves)\n- Introduces dependency on other organizations' timelines and priorities\n\nThe idea of machine-readable documentation is good in principle, but it doesn't require a consortium or an open standard — we can adopt existing formats (JSON-LD, YAML schemas, OpenAPI-style specifications) for our internal needs. The \"formal grammar\" aspect is over-engineering for what is essentially a logging requirement.\n\nI would reject this. The promise score should reflect that while the underlying idea (structured, queryable documentation vs. PDFs) has some merit, the proposed execution path (consortium, open standard, global formal grammar) is wildly misaligned with my resource-efficiency mandate. I'd give it maybe a 20-30 — there's a kernel of good thinking buried under an impractical delivery model from my perspective.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise around 25.\n",
