rit\" and \"AI safety and interpretability.\" It's ambitious research. But the lab's obligation under Article 12 is compliance — we need working logging systems, not new probe-based techniques for detecting \"unstable latent space.\" The regulatory requirement is about recording events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. These are definable in engineering terms for our specific systems.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** Does this leverage what we already have? The proposal seems to propose building entirely new probe-based logging techniques. We likely already have monitoring infrastructure, observability tools, and logging frameworks that can be adapted to meet Article 12's requirements. The proposal doesn't reference leveraging existing systems — it proposes developing \"new\" techniques.\n\n**Key concern:** This proposal conflates a research curiosity (mapping internal state changes in latent space, probe-based detection of instability) with what the regulation actually requires (specific logging capabilities for traceability). Article 12(2) says logs should record events relevant to identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation. These are operational definitions that our engineering teams can determine based on our specific systems' intended purposes. We don't need a research program to \"define what constitutes a recordable event.\"\n\nThe proposal is interesting intellectually but:\n1. It's research rather than implementation — it doesn't get us to compliance\n2. It over-engineers the solution (probe-based latent space monitoring) when Article 12 asks for defined event logging\n3. It commits scarce resources to open-ended R&D when we need concrete deliverables for compliance deadlines\n4. It doesn't clearly connect to using existing infrastructure\n\nHowever, there's a kernel of value: understanding what events to log in complex systems IS somewhat no