ments. It talks about \"inputs, outputs, and system states\" but not the specific items like reference databases or verifier identities. However, as a modular framework, it could be extended.\n\n**From my Compliance Officer perspective:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic recording capability\n- Standardization across projects reduces compliance risk from inconsistency\n- Removes burden from individual engineers (reduces human error in logging)\n- Single configuration toggle simplifies enabling/disabling for different deployment contexts\n- Modular design allows adaptation to different AI architectures\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is addressed by making it a persistent library\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It doesn't specify how it addresses Article 12(2)(a)-(c) specifically, nor does it address the Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements in Article 12(3).\n- \"Compliance mode via a single configuration toggle\" — this raises a concern: if compliance logging can be toggled off, is that acceptable? The regulation says systems \"shall technically allow for\" automatic recording. Having it as an opt-in feature rather than always-on could be problematic for certain deployments. However, for R&D prototypes that aren't yet deployed as high-risk systems, this might be appropriate.\n- The proposal focuses on the lab's internal development process (\"high-risk prototypes\"). Article 12 applies to \"high-risk AI systems\" — there's a question of whether prototypes in development are already subject to this or only once they enter the market. This is actually a practical consideration for an R&D institution.\n- No mention of log retention, immutability, access controls, or security — all important for ensuring logs can serve as evidence of compliance.\n- No mention of how logs will be structured to facilitate audit by authorities (Regulators under Article 72 post-market monitoring).\n\nOver