uating \"intellectual coherence\" may not be the right lens for checking whether field (b) \"the reference database against which input data has been checked\" is properly documented.\n- The proposal doesn't address cost: who are these two reviewers? Are they senior researchers whose time is most expensive? Is there a dedicated compliance/documentation team that should handle this instead?\n- For a lab operating under funding constraints, dedicating researcher-hours to documentation review (rather than direct research) has an opportunity cost against the scientific pipeline.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This IS leveraging existing infrastructure (the paper review system). That's a positive.\n- But \"adapt\" is doing a lot of heavy lifting here. The paper review system evaluates novel scientific contributions. Regulatory documentation needs evaluation against a fixed checklist of legal requirements. These are fundamentally different assessment criteria. Adapting the system may require significant retooling — training reviewers in AI Act specifics, creating checklists mapped to Article 12(3) fields, etc.\n- The proposal doesn't mention any automation or tooling to reduce the burden on human reviewers. Given that Article 12 is largely about automated logging (the technical system must \"technically allow for automatic recording\"), a documentation peer-review process is somewhat orthogonal — it reviews the documentation OF the logging system, not the logs themselves.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe idea has merit in principle — quality documentation matters, and using existing review culture is smart. But from my resource-allocation perspective:\n1. It adds a mandatory human-intensive step (2 reviewers × multiple milestones) to every high-risk project without specifying how this fits within current budget/headcount.\n2. The \"adaptation\" of paper review to regulatory documentation is non-trivial and likely requires significant setup investment (training, checklists, process design) before it becomes efficient.\n3. It may not be the most resource-efficient way to ensure documentation quality — a structured checklist + automated validation tools + a single designated compliance reviewer might achieve the same outcome with fewer researcher-hours consumed.\n4. The proposal is somewhat vague on implementation specifics (how many milestones? how long does review take? what's the turnaround? who handles conflicts?).\n\nHowever, it's not a terrible idea. Leveraging existing peer-review culture is clever. But from a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, I'd say this is moderate promise — it has some appeal but the resource cost relative to benefit is uncertain and potentially high for a constrained lab. The \"two researchers\" requirement per project is the biggest concern.\n\nI'll lean toward reject or low-approve. Actually, let me reconsider. The bar is: does it clear MY bar from MY vantage point? \n\nThe proposal's core value proposition is that existing infrastructure (paper review) can be repurposed. That's appealing for resource efficiency. But the execution as described (two full researcher audits per milestone) is expensive and the adaptation required is substantial. I think this is a \"reject\" with moderate promise — it has a kernel of good thinking but as specified, it would consume disproportionate resources relative to the regulatory need, and the framing mismatch (scientific peer review vs. regulatory compliance checking) means the existing system doesn't actually transfer cleanly.\n\nI'll go with reject, promise around 35-40.\n",
