tirely. In practice, regulatory documentation requires human judgment about what's relevant, how to frame limitations, etc. A fully automated pipeline might produce technically accurate but legally insufficient documentation, creating compliance risk.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The good news: Git repos, hyperparameter logs, validation reports - we likely already generate these. The proposal leverages data we're already producing.\n- CI/CD pipelines may already exist in the lab's MLOps setup. Adding documentation generation as another step is architecturally sound.\n- The \"live compliance dossiers\" concept aligns with the requirement that documentation be kept \"up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\"\n\n**Concerns:**\n- Building an AI-driven tool to write documentation for AI systems adds meta-complexity. Who validates the validator? If the auto-generated dossier has errors, we have a compliance problem compounded by having automated it.\n- The scope is potentially very large: \"comprehensive documentation required by Recital 71\" includes risk-management system documentation, algorithms descriptions, data provenance, etc. Not all of this lives in Git commits or hyperparameter logs.\n- There's a resource commitment here that could be substantial - building, testing, maintaining an AI tool is not trivial.\n- However, the alternative (manual documentation for every iteration of every high-risk system) scales poorly with our R&D throughput.\n\n**My judgment:**\nThis is a genuinely good idea in direction, but the proposal as stated has some overreach (\"AI-driven tool,\" \"replaces manual reporting\" entirely). The core insight - leverage existing CI/ML infrastructure to auto-generate much of the compliance documentation from metadata we're already collecting - is sound and resource-efficient. It uses what we have (Git, hyperparameter logging, validation reports) rather than requiring new data collection.\n\nF