ut are standard CI/CD work.\n- The risk is scope creep: trying to make it truly comprehensive (covering ALL Article 12 requirements) could balloon significantly. But a scoped prototype targeting the structured data extraction portion is very doable.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- This is a well-scoped, technically viable engineering project with clear deliverables.\n- It directly addresses a real pain point (manual documentation for compliance).\n- The prototyping path is clear: start with extracting structured metadata from existing CI/CD and experiment tracking tools, format into the EU template, trigger on commits.\n- The \"replaces manual reporting\" claim is slightly overblown - you'll still need human review for risk assessments, limitation statements, etc. But as an engineering project, the automated extraction layer is solid.\n- It's not a moonshot; it builds on existing patterns (MLflow + CI/CD + templated output).\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Real-time\" might be overpromising for complex model training pipelines where documentation generation needs to wait for validation to complete.\n- The scope as stated is large - building an integrated system that pulls from code comments, VCS, AND training logs into a unified template is non-trivial but still very doable.\n- \"Replaces manual reporting\" - in practice this will supplement rather than fully replace, especially for the qualitative aspects (risk management documentation, limitation descriptions).\n\nOverall: This is technically viable, well-aligned with existing engineering patterns, and would prototype quickly. It addresses a genuine compliance need with an engineering solution that fits naturally into DevOps workflows. I'd approve this from my vantage point. The promise score should reflect high technical feasibility and good prototyping speed, but tempered slightly by the breadth of scope and the \"replaces\" language which sets unrealistic expectations for full automation.\n\nI'll rate this 