 reputation-building rather than operational necessity.\n- Collaborative initiatives with other institutions and EU regulators are notoriously resource-intensive: meetings, alignment across organizations, iterative schema design, negotiation on standards. These can balloon in scope and timeline while producing little concrete output for our lab's compliance needs.\n- The proposal doesn't clearly articulate how this directly serves OUR high-risk AI systems' compliance obligations under Article 12. It's framed as an external contribution (publishing templates for \"other institutions\") rather than solving our own logging/record-keeping infrastructure.\n- From a resource-allocation standpoint, we could achieve the same internal efficiency by simply defining our own documentation format internally without publishing it or collaborating with regulators on standards. The open-source/collaborative element adds significant coordination cost for marginal additional benefit to us.\n- \"Establishes our institution as a leader in AI governance\" is a reputation goal, not an operational one. My mandate is resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, not institutional branding.\n- The collaboration with EU regulators could create dependencies — if we're co-designing the schema with them, any changes to the regulation or enforcement guidance could require rework of our investment.\n\n**My assessment:** From a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, this proposal over-invests in external standardization and reputation at the expense of focused internal compliance infrastructure. The core need (Article 12 logging capabilities) is best served by building our own logging infrastructure directly. A lightweight internal documentation format would serve that purpose without the coordination overhead of a multi-institutional standards body. However, there IS a kernel of practical value: if we define our own schema well and it happens to align with what others