ecifics once rather than per-project.\n\n**Arguments AGAINST or concerns:**\n- \"Mandatory APIs\" could be seen as constraining scientific experimentation — if a researcher wants to try a novel architecture, rigid API contracts might slow them down. However, the proposal says \"standardized event recording capabilities,\" which is about what gets logged, not how the AI system itself works. This seems reasonable.\n- Development cost: Building this framework itself requires engineering resources upfront. But the ROI is clear — it's a one-time investment that amortizes across all future and current projects.\n- \"Centralized\" could become a bottleneck if the infrastructure team can't keep up with new project needs. But this is an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal.\n- The scope is appropriately bounded: it's about logging/event recording, not about changing how AI systems are built or trained. This respects scientific ambition while handling compliance overhead centrally.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is squarely within my domain of concern. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency (one framework vs. N ad-hoc solutions), protects funding by preventing duplicated effort, and makes effective use of existing infrastructure (the secure storage system can likely be repurposed or built on top of what we already have). The fact that it's framed as a \"software library\" rather than a full platform suggests it's lightweight enough not to become a bureaucratic overhead. The mandatory API approach is the right call for compliance — you don't want teams opting out.\n\nThe main risk is over-engineering: if the framework becomes too rigid or complex, it could actually slow down R&D. But the proposal as stated is appropriately scoped. It's about standardizing what gets captured (timestamps, input data, user IDs) and where it goes (immutable storage), which are relatively straightforward engineering tasks.\n\nI would approve this. It's a high-lev