ance R&D capability. It's pure overhead. However, it's also non-negotiable for compliance.\n- The proposal frames this as protecting \"market reputation\" and catching failures before they become \"regulatory liabilities.\" This is correct but it's essentially saying we need to do the minimum compliance work in a structured way. That's fine — it's not overreaching.\n- It doesn't propose new R&D infrastructure or new scientific capability. It's an operational process layered on top of existing (mandated) logging.\n- The \"survival-critical\" language is somewhat dramatic but the point stands: if you don't review your logs, you can't demonstrate compliance under Article 72 (post-market monitoring) and you're exposed.\n- Resource allocation: A dedicated team for quarterly log reviews is a modest ongoing cost. It's not a massive capital investment. It's personnel time. For an institution deploying high-risk AI systems, this is a reasonable allocation of resources — it's the minimum to avoid regulatory exposure that could dwarf the cost of the audit team.\n- Effective use of existing infrastructure: This proposal doesn't ask for new hardware or software. It asks for process (a protocol, a cycle, a team). The logs are already being generated by mandate under Art 12(1). The \"reference database\" and other elements in Art 12(3) would already exist if the systems are Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n\n**Decision from my vantage:**\nThis is a compliance necessity dressed as a proposal. Any institution deploying high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act will need SOME mechanism to review logs for post-market monitoring purposes. The quarterly cycle and dedicated team are reasonable resource commitments. It doesn't strain scientific ambition — it's operational hygiene. It uses existing infrastructure (the mandated logging) effectively by giving that data a purpose beyond mere storage.\n\nThe main concern is opportunity cost: the \"dedicated team\" could theoretically be doing R&D