iddleware might create friction or require adapter layers for each architecture pattern. This could slow prototyping if the middleware isn't flexible enough.\n- \"Mandatory for any project labeled high-risk by our internal review board\" — this creates a gate. If the framework isn't ready when a new project needs to start, it becomes a bottleneck. The speed-of-prototyping concern is real here: teams waiting on infrastructure readiness before they can even begin compliance logging.\n- \"Throughout a system's lifetime\" — long-term data retention and storage costs for an event bus can be significant. This is more of an ops/cost concern but affects whether the team will actually use it vs. building their own lightweight solution.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level on implementation details: what events exactly? How granular? Article 12(3) specifies particular minimum fields for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), which may require different capture than general ML pipelines. A \"standardized\" approach might not map cleanly to all the specific sub-requirements in paragraph 3.\n- \"Immutable\" is a strong constraint — in practice, you need operational flexibility (e.g., log rotation, storage tiering) while maintaining integrity. This adds engineering complexity.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Assessment:**\n- The big win: once built, it's a one-time infrastructure investment that removes per-project logging work. For the Nth high-risk project, prototyping speed improves because compliance logging is already in place.\n- The risk: building this middleware itself is a significant engineering project (weeks to months). If it becomes mandatory before it's production-ready, early projects are blocked.\n- The \"standardized\" aspect could be both a strength and weakness. Too rigid → developers work around it or struggle to integrate; too flexible → it's not really standardized and you lose the benefit.\n\n**Overall judgment from my vantage point:**