tage R&D projects (prototyping, experimentation), demanding full Article 12(3) compliance (for Annex III point 1(a) systems like biometric verification) might stifle scientific ambition. A prototype that's still exploring novel architectures shouldn't need the same logging granularity as a deployed system.\n- The proposal says \"project leads must demonstrate functioning automated logging before accessing next-stage resources.\" This could create a perverse incentive where teams build minimal logging to pass the gate rather than designing it into the architecture properly. However, this is somewhat mitigated if the audit checks are meaningful.\n- From an infrastructure perspective, requiring all projects to have logging built in from the start does mean we need shared logging infrastructure (log storage, parsing tools, audit dashboards) that serves multiple projects efficiently. This is actually good for leveraging existing R&D infrastructure - rather than each project building its own ad-hoc solution.\n- The quarterly review criteria update is a modest administrative change, not a massive resource commitment.\n\n**Net assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a well-conceived proposal that aligns strongly with resource-allocation efficiency. Front-loading compliance infrastructure prevents the expensive \"retrofit\" scenario. It leverages shared infrastructure (logging systems) rather than per-project ad-hoc solutions. The quarterly cadence is reasonable and not overly burdensome. The main risk is that it could slow early-stage scientific exploration if applied too rigidly to exploratory projects, but the proposal as written says \"next-stage resources\" which implies it's a progression gate, not a blanket requirement on all work from day one.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure well, and addresses a real resource-allocation problem (compliance costs landing as surprise overruns at project end). I'll approve with a high pr