acts as a byproduct of the development process itself. This is exactly the kind of \"shift-left\" approach that preserves prototyping speed.\n- Version-controlled documents mean you have traceability (which maps well to Article 12's requirements for logs and record-keeping).\n\n**Concerns / Limitations:**\n- Recital 71 mentions \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" Auto-extracting hyperparameters and dataset versions covers part of this, but the *narrative* documentation (why certain architectural choices were made, risk assessment reasoning, limitations discussion) is harder to auto-generate. The proposal somewhat overstates what automation can cover — it handles the structured metadata well but the qualitative/comprehensive form required by Recital 71 may still need human input.\n- Article 12(3) specifically requires things like \"recording of the period of each use,\" \"reference database against which input data has been checked,\" \"input data for which search led to a match\" — these are runtime logging requirements, not documentation generation. The proposal addresses the Recital 71 (technical documentation) side but doesn't really address the Article 12(3) automatic event logging requirement. However, it does touch on the broader record-keeping theme.\n- \"Transforms static reporting into a living stream\" — this is good in principle, but compliance documents may need to be *frozen* at certain points for regulatory review. A continuously changing document might not satisfy auditors who want a point-in-time snapshot. Version control helps here though (git tags/snapshots).\n- The scope seems reasonable and the technical approach is sound. It's a well-scoped, achievable project that addresses a real pain point.\n\n**From my vantage point specifically:**\n- Technical viability: High. The components are all mature technology. No novel research needed. A competent team