ing exists. From my perspective as someone who builds prototypes and evaluates whether solutions are technically feasible, this is outside my domain. I don't design budget formulas. However, I do care about whether the process change would impede or help prototyping speed.\n\n4. Does it actually solve a problem from my vantage point? The concern \"compliance costs eating into research funds\" is more of a PMO/finance/leadership concern. From my engineering perspective, what matters is:\n   - Can I get the right engineers and tools to implement logging quickly?\n   - Are there clear technical requirements I can prototype against?\n   - Does this proposal create friction in getting resources to me when I need them?\n\nThe mandatory percentage-based allocation could actually be counterproductive for prototyping. Early-stage prototypes might not need full production-grade logging, but a rigid funding formula tied to \"high-risk project\" classification at the planning phase locks in compliance spending before technical requirements are clear. This could either overfund (waste) or underfund (if the percentage is too low for complex systems).\n\nAlso, from a prototyping speed perspective: adding another gate to the resource planning process means longer time-to-prototype-start. For R&D where you're iterating quickly, having an additional bureaucratic checkpoint can be a drag on velocity.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal is fundamentally outside my technical domain (it's a finance/process change), it doesn't propose any technically viable solution to Article 12 requirements, and it could potentially add friction to the prototyping pipeline through mandatory planning gates. However, I acknowledge that having dedicated resources for compliance infrastructure is not unreasonable in principle—but the mechanism proposed (percentage formula + grant process gate) is a management/finance decision, not an engineering one, and from my vantage point it doesn't advance technical viability or prototyping speed/quality.\n\nDecision: Reject. It's not technically actionable from my seat, it adds process overhead to prototyping timelines, and the percentage-based approach is too rigid for the variable nature of engineering work.\n\nPromise score: This is low. Maybe 10-15. It's not harmful per se, but from my vantage point it doesn't advance technical viability or prototyping speed/quality in any meaningful way, and could add friction. I'll say 12.\n",
