g.\n- It's somewhat tangential to what Article 12 specifically mandates (operational logs) vs. the broader technical documentation requirements in Recital 71 / Annex IV. But it does contribute to the overall compliance picture.\n- The \"absolute reproducibility\" framing is ambitious but not a blocker for prototyping — you can start with the core logging and expand.\n\n**Decision:** From my vantage point, this is technically viable as a core concept and the middleware pattern is well-established enough that we could prototype meaningful parts quickly. However, the scope as written (\"every... intermediate state\", \"absolute reproducibility\") is so broad it risks becoming an unbounded project that doesn't ship. The centralized ledger for all training provenance is overkill for what Article 12 actually requires and introduces unnecessary architectural complexity for a compliance logging requirement.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My job is to assess technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping. Is this technically viable? Yes, the core parts are. Can we prototype it quickly? The MVP (hash inputs, log hyperparams, append to ledger) — yes, within a sprint or two. The \"every intermediate state\" part slows things down significantly in terms of storage design, performance overhead on training jobs, etc.\n\nI think this clears my bar for approval because:\n1. The technical approach (middleware hashing + centralized logging) is sound and well-understood.\n2. A meaningful prototype can be built quickly.\n3. It contributes to the compliance picture even if it goes beyond strict Article 12 text.\n\nBut I'd give a moderate promise score because the scope ambition (\"absolute\", \"every\") makes this harder to deliver at quality, and there's some mismatch with what Article 12 specifically requires vs. broader documentation requirements.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise of around 58-62. The technical approach is solid and prototypable, but the scope breadth and the slight misalignment with Article 12's specific operational logging mandate (vs. training provenance) keep it from being higher. Also, \"absolute reproducibility\" as a design goal introduces complexity that could slow prototyping significantly.\n\nI'll go with approve at 60.\n",
