ering.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n- Building this is itself an upfront resource commitment. Who builds it? How long does it take? Does it pull engineers away from current research deliverables?\n- \"All high-risk AI prototypes\" — scope creep risk. If the library tries to handle every possible Article 12 data point for every conceivable system type, it becomes a massive project. The Annex III specific requirements (paragraph 3) are very different from general logging (paragraphs 1-2), so you might need multiple modules.\n- Maintenance burden: ML frameworks evolve. If PyTorch updates or the lab switches to a new framework, this middleware needs updating. That's ongoing resource commitment.\n- \"Plug-and-play\" is aspirational. In practice, integration with diverse research codebases (which are often messy and idiosyncratic) can be harder than it sounds. Researchers' code isn't always structured in ways that make middleware clean to insert.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a very strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses the core tension I care about — how do you meet regulatory requirements without consuming all your engineering capacity and pulling researchers off their actual science? A shared, reusable logging layer is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that amortizes compliance costs across many projects. The upfront cost is real but bounded (it's a library, not a new platform), and it integrates with what we already have rather than replacing it.\n\nThe main risk is scope: if \"all high-risk AI prototypes\" means trying to handle every possible edge case in the Annex III requirements from day one, this could balloon. But even a v1 that handles the general paragraphs 1-2 requirements plus the most common Annex III categories would save enormous researcher time.\n\nI'd approve this. It's efficient, leverages existing infrastructure, and directly protects scientific capacity. Promise score: I'd give it around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with m