re the right events in edge cases, we fail compliance.\n- Treating logging as a \"first-class functional requirement\" aligns with how the Act frames it - it's not an afterthought or nice-to-have; it's a mandatory technical capability (Article 12(1) uses \"shall\").\n- The pre-audit validation aspect is crucial for institutional survival: catching logging gaps internally before a market surveillance authority does means we can remediate rather than face enforcement action, fines, or product withdrawal.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, being able to demonstrate (with test evidence) that our traceability system works as required shows diligence and reduces regulatory risk.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The proposal says it \"guarantees\" compliance - no single testing framework can truly guarantee full legal compliance across all scenarios. But this is a marketing/overstatement issue, not a fundamental flaw in the approach.\n- It's an additional implementation resource commitment on top of building the logging system itself. However, from a compliance perspective, if you're going to build the logging (which is mandatory), validating that it works correctly is not optional - it's part of ensuring compliance. A QA/testing step for a legally mandated feature is standard practice.\n- The scope seems appropriately bounded: it focuses on the Article 12 requirements specifically.\n\n**Decision:** This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly supports our ability to demonstrate compliance with Article 12, protects against enforcement risk (institutional survival), and builds a defensible audit trail showing we've validated our logging capabilities. The adversarial testing approach mirrors what regulators will do. I would approve this.\n\n**Promise score:** This is highly relevant to my function. It's not the most novel or ambitious idea, but it's directly on-mission for compliance. I'd put it around 82-85. It's important but somewhat expected/necessary r