d.\n4. The proposal is somewhat vague on implementation specifics - what experiment tracking systems? What template format? How does it handle the qualitative content requirements?\n\n**Decision:** This is a partially viable idea that overstates its capabilities. The technical core (automating data extraction and formatting into templates) is feasible, but the framing as replacing manual documentation work is misleading from an engineering standpoint. It would be more accurately scoped as \"semi-automated compliance document assembly\" rather than full replacement. From my vantage point on prototyping speed/quality, this has moderate value - it reduces some administrative drag but doesn't directly improve the core R&D workflow. The technical risk is low for the narrow version (template filling from structured data) but high if you expect it to handle all of Recital 71's qualitative requirements.\n\nI'll reject it because:\n- It overstates what automation can achieve for compliance documentation (much is expert judgment, not data extraction)\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, the ROI is moderate and the proposal doesn't clearly articulate how it accelerates or improves AI system development itself\n- The \"push-button\" framing suggests an engineering underestimate of the integration complexity across heterogeneous systems\n- It's more of an administrative efficiency project than something that advances technical capability in AI development\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The question is whether it clears MY bar as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality. \n\nThe technical viability of the narrow version (pull structured data, fill templates) is fine - maybe 70% feasible for what's described. But the proposal as written implies broader scope that isn't technically achievable through scripts alone. The \"replaces manual report writing\" claim is not technically supportable.\n\nFor prototyping speed: this doesn't make 