:**\n1. \"Automated system that generates living technical documents\" — this sounds ambitious for the scope of a single proposal. Generating *comprehensive* technical documentation (algorithms, data provenance, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management documentation) purely by pulling metadata from logs and VCS is technically very challenging. The Recital says documentation should include \"the general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used as well as documentation on the relevant risk-management system.\" Auto-generating all of that from metadata alone may overpromise what's achievable with reasonable resources.\n2. There's a risk this becomes a scope-creep monster: \"pulling metadata directly from training logs\" sounds simple until you realize we need to parse heterogeneous log formats across multiple projects, handle versioning complexity, and produce documents in the \"clear and comprehensive form\" required by regulation. The engineering effort could balloon.\n3. However — the core idea (auto-update compliance docs when code/data changes) is fundamentally sound and high-leverage. Even a partial implementation would save significant recurring human effort.\n4. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this is a one-time build with ongoing low maintenance, versus the alternative of permanent staff time on manual doc updates. The ROI case is strong IF scoped correctly.\n\n**My decision:**\nThe core idea is excellent from a resource-efficiency perspective — it repurposes existing infrastructure (CI/CD, VCS, logs) to eliminate recurring compliance overhead. It's not asking for new funding categories or exotic capabilities; it's engineering work within our existing stack. The main risk is scope inflation, but the proposal as stated focuses on the automation trigger mechanism, which is well-defined. I'll approve it, noting that the promise score should reflect high value from my vantage point while acknowledging some execution risk in the \"living document generation\" part.\n\nI'd rate this around 78-82. It's highly aligned with resource efficiency and existing infrastructure use. The slight discount is because \"generates living technical documents\" could balloon in scope, but the core mechanism (CI/CD trigger updating compliance dossier on model/data changes) is clearly implementable within our existing toolchain and would deliver meaningful recurring savings.\n\nLet me settle on 80.\n",
