        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to act as the Compliance Officer of an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. I need to screen a single proposal (P2975) from my professional vantage point focused on: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze proposal P2975: \"Post-Market Drift and Risk Monitor\"\n\nThe proposal describes an observability dashboard that uses statistical drift detection algorithms to compare real-time inference distributions against the original validation baseline. It claims this provides early warning for risk and satisfies Article 12(2) without requiring constant manual audits.\n\nLet me assess this from a compliance officer's perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- It addresses Article 12(2)(a) - identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk or a substantial modification\n- It addresses Article 12(2)(b) - facilitating post-market monitoring under Article 72\n- Drift detection is a legitimate technical approach to continuous monitoring\n- The concept of comparing against validation baselines aligns with the spirit of traceability and compliance verification\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n1. The proposal says it \"satisfies Article 12(2) without requiring constant manual audits\" - this is problematic from a compliance standpoint. Article 12 requires LOGGING (automatic recording of events), not just monitoring/dashboards. A drift detection dashboard is not the same as a logging system that records individual events with timestamps, reference databases, input data matches, etc. The proposal conflates \"monitoring\" with \"logging.\"\n\n2. Article 12(1) specifically requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" - this is about granular event-level logging, not aggregate statistical monitoring. A drift detection dashboard tells you the distribution has shifted, but it doesn't give you the individual event records needed for traceability.\n\n3. For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), Article 12(3) requires very specific minimum logging: start/end times of each use, reference database used, input data matched, and identification of natural persons verifying results. A drift detection dashboard does NOT provide any of these granular details.\n\n4. The proposal's claim that it \"satisfies Article 12(2)\" is an overstatement. It may SUPPORT compliance with the intent behind 12(2), but it doesn't fulfill the literal requirement for automatic logging capabilities.\n\n5. From a market reputation standpoint, claiming this satisfies the Act when it only partially addresses the monitoring aspect could create regulatory risk if a regulator examines whether we have proper event-level logging in place.\n\n6. However, as a component of a broader compliance strategy, drift detection IS valuable. It complements (but does not replace) proper logging infrastructure.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point as Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival and regulatory compliance:\n\n- The proposal addresses a real need (continuous monitoring for risk identification)\n- But it overstates its compliance value by claiming it \"satisfies Article 12(2)\"\n- It's a useful component but NOT sufficient for Article 12 compliance - it doesn't provide the event-level logging required by 12(1), and for Annex III 1(a) systems, it completely misses the specific minimum requirements of 12(3)\n- The framing as \"without requiring constant manual audits\" is actually a red flag from a compliance perspective - regulators will expect both automated AND periodic human review\n- Approving this proposal would commit resources to building something that gives false confidence about Article 12 compliance\n\nHowever, I should also consider: the proposal IS directionally correct in terms of what's needed for post-market monitoring. The concept is sound as one layer of a broader system. The question is whether approving