        "reasoning_content": "The proposal P2220 suggests creating an open-source AI Act Logging Standard Library - modular software library with standardized API wrappers for Article 12 requirements, released under permissive license on GitHub, targeting PyTorch and TensorFlow plugins.\n\nLet me evaluate this from my vantage point as Lab Director focused on:\n- Resource-allocation efficiency\n- Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints\n- Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\n\nKey considerations:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- Creating AND maintaining open-source plugins for multiple frameworks (PyTorch, TensorFlow) is a massive ongoing commitment. This isn't a one-time build - it's continuous maintenance as those frameworks evolve, as the AI Act gets updated with implementing regulations, and as community issues pile up.\n- The lab has scarce implementation resources. Diverting engineers to maintain external-facing open-source libraries (handling GitHub issues, PRs, compatibility across framework versions) is a significant ongoing drain on staff who should be focused on our own compliance and research.\n- \"Create AND maintain\" is the key phrase here. Maintenance of open-source infrastructure for the broader community is essentially an indefinite commitment with no bounded endpoint.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs funding constraints:**\n- The framing is \"increases our scientific impact by providing critical infrastructure to other R&D institutions worldwide.\" This is a nice-sounding mission, but it's not what Article 12 requires of us specifically. We need to comply for OUR systems. Building a general-purpose library for the global community is a much larger scope than compliance.\n- The lab's funding constraints mean we should focus on doing our own compliance well rather than becoming an infrastructure provider for the world. That's a different organization's job (or a dedicated standards body, or a consortium).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- We already have internal logging and monitoring systems. The question is whether we can adapt those to meet Article 12, not whether we should build a new public library from scratch.\n- However, there IS value in standardizing our internal approach - if we structure our own compliance work as modular components, that's good engineering practice regardless of whether we open-source it. But the proposal goes well beyond \"structure our code well\" to \"publish and maintain for the world.\"\n\n**The specific scope creep concern:**\n- Article 12 is about ensuring OUR high-risk systems log events properly. The proposal transforms a compliance obligation into a global infrastructure project.\n- Supporting PyTorch AND TensorFlow plugins means tracking two major frameworks' release cycles, API changes, version compatibility - each with large communities generating support requests.\n- \"Permissive license on GitHub\" means we're making a public commitment to availability and quality that has reputational risk if we can't maintain it properly.\n\n**What I would approve vs reject:**\n- I think the CORE idea of building modular, well-structured logging components for our own compliance is sound and efficient. But as framed - an open-source library FOR THE GLOBAL COMMUNITY with multi-framework plugins, maintained indefinitely under permissive license - this significantly overcommits scarce resources to a mission that goes well beyond our obligations and capabilities given funding constraints.\n- The \"scientific impact\" framing is appealing but it's essentially a vanity metric relative to the actual resource cost. We're an R&D institution, not an open-source infrastructure provider.\n\nThis feels like a reject from my vantage point. It's well-intentioned but overcommits resources for a scope that exceeds our obligation and capabilities. The promise score should reflect that while the underlying engineering principle (modular logging)