 drift from actual system state - a major compliance failure mode in practice\n- \"Living technical dossier\" vs. static PDFs addresses the practical reality that high-risk AI systems evolve\n\n**Compliance concerns:**\n- The proposal is about documentation infrastructure, not the logging capabilities of the AI system itself (Article 12(1) requires the SYSTEM to technically allow automatic recording). However, it's complementary - you need both the logs AND the documentation that interprets/contextualizes them.\n- \"Metadata extraction\" for capabilities and limitations reports is ambitious - can automated metadata truly capture all relevant compliance information? Some aspects of risk management documentation (Article 12(2)(a)) may require human judgment. But this is a starting point, not a complete solution.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems (biometric identification). But it's not claiming to be comprehensive - it's addressing the documentation layer.\n\n**Institutional survival / market reputation angle:**\n- A robust, automated documentation system is a strong signal to regulators and market participants that the institution takes compliance seriously\n- In case of a regulatory audit or incident investigation (Article 79), having perfectly synchronized version-controlled documentation would be a significant advantage\n- Demonstrates maturity in AI governance - important for market reputation\n- Reduces the risk of documentation drift, which is one of the most common findings in regulatory audits\n\n**Resource commitment consideration:**\n- This is an implementation project with clear scope (CI/CD pipeline integration, metadata extraction)\n- It's not exploratory R&D - it's engineering work with a well-defined deliverable\n- The ROI on compliance risk reduction is high relative to the investment\n- It creates infrastructure that serves multiple regulatory requirements simultaneously\n\n**My assessment:** This is strongly aligned with my function's mandate. It directly addresses Recital 71's requirement for up-to-date technical documentation and supports Article 12's traceability objectives. The CI/CD approach eliminates a major compliance failure mode (documentation drift). From institutional survival perspective, having this in place before any regulatory scrutiny would be protective. From market reputation, it signals operational maturity.\n\nThe only reservation is that \"metadata extraction\" for capabilities and limitations may need human review to ensure completeness - but the proposal doesn't claim full automation eliminates all human involvement. It's a strong foundation.\n\nI'll approve this with a high promise score. It's well-scoped, directly relevant, addresses a real compliance risk, and the engineering approach is sound. I'd say around 82-85. The slight deduction is because it doesn't explicitly address how it handles the \"risk-management system\" documentation aspect or the human verification aspects of Article 14(5), and the metadata extraction approach for \"capabilities and limitations\" may need more nuance to satisfy regulators who want clear, comprehensive, human-readable documentation (Recital 71: \"drawn in a clear and comprehensive form\"). But these are refinements within an otherwise strong proposal.\n\nI'll go with 84.\n",
