ay already exist for other regulatory frameworks (ISO 13485, IEC 62304, etc.).\n- From resource efficiency: if the lab already has a QMS and periodic review cycles for other regulations, layering another \"quarterly audit cycle\" specifically for AI Act logging might be redundant or at minimum should integrate with existing processes rather than creating parallel structures.\n- The proposal is vague on implementation details: who exactly are these compliance officers? Are they dedicated to this task (opportunity cost from R&D work)? What's the tooling? How does this connect to actual engineering workflows?\n- It's a necessary component of overall compliance but as stated, it's somewhat generic. It doesn't specify how logs are accessed, what tools are used, or how findings feed back into engineering decisions.\n- From my perspective: I'd want this integrated with existing infrastructure rather than creating new siloed processes. The quarterly cadence is reasonable and not resource-intensive per se, but the proposal as written is a process document, not an engineering solution. It's low-hanging fruit compliance work that doesn't significantly advance scientific ambition or require major funding commitment.\n\n**Decision:** This is borderline. From my vantage point:\n- Pro: Low resource cost (process design, not infrastructure build), addresses a real regulatory requirement, quarterly cadence is sustainable\n- Con: Vague on implementation, may duplicate existing QMS processes, doesn't specify how it uses \"existing R&D infrastructure,\" doesn't address the technical logging capability gap (Article 12(1)) which is where most of the engineering resource would go\n- The proposal is essentially a governance checkbox. It's not wrong, but from my perspective focused on resource efficiency and effective use of existing infrastructure, I'd want to know if this can be folded into existing review cycles rather than creating a new parallel process.\n\nI think I'll approve it with