anizational process improvement, it's reasonable but not critical. It won't make or break compliance.\n- The risk is that researchers end up spending time filling out templates that don't actually satisfy either peer reviewers OR regulators, creating a third parallel documentation burden rather than consolidating two.\n\nI think this is borderline. It's a sensible organizational idea but it's somewhat superficial relative to the actual regulatory requirements, and I'm not convinced the \"unification\" claim holds up under scrutiny. From a resource-allocation standpoint, I'd want to see evidence that the peer-review documentation cycle already covers most of what Article 12 demands before committing resources to building a merged framework. The proposal as stated is somewhat hand-wavy about how the alignment actually works.\n\nI'll lean toward reject — it's a reasonable idea but doesn't clear the bar for committing implementation resources when the actual technical requirements (automatic logging, event recording) are not addressed, and the \"unification\" premise is questionable. It's more of an administrative convenience than a compliance-critical measure. But I'll give it a moderate promise score because the intuition behind it (reduce duplicate documentation work) is sound in principle.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal is specifically about documentation templates for capabilities and limitations — which IS part of what Recital 71 calls for (\"information which should include the general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system\"). And if researchers are already documenting these for publication, having a structured template that also satisfies regulatory needs does save time. The question is whether this warrants dedicated implementation resources from my perspective.\n\nI think the honest assessment is: it's a low-effort, moderate-value process improvement that could be handled by a small documentation team without requiring signi