al says \"all high-risk prototypes\" — but the scope of what counts as \"high-risk\" under Annex III vs. other categories could complicate the design. The middleware needs to handle Article 12(3) specific requirements for biometric identification systems (point 1(a) of Annex III) differently from other high-risk systems. A one-size-fits-all decorator might be too rigid or need complex configuration.\n- Integration risk: if the lab's core framework is evolving rapidly, coupling compliance logging into it creates maintenance burden. Framework updates could break logging hooks. This could become a drain on engineering resources over time.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — \"API decorators and middleware hooks\" — but doesn't address edge cases like asynchronous processing pipelines, multi-model ensembles, or systems where the \"event\" boundary is ambiguous.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\nThis is a strong candidate for approval from my perspective. The upfront investment (a dedicated engineering team to build the middleware) is bounded and finite. The ongoing benefit scales with the number of high-risk projects — every new project that would otherwise need custom logging now just flips a flag. This is a classic \"build once, use many\" efficiency play. It frees research scientists from compliance implementation details so they can focus on the science.\n\nThe main concern is whether the engineering investment is proportionate to the lab's actual pipeline of high-risk systems. If we have 3-5 high-risk projects, building custom logging for each might be comparable in cost to building the middleware. But if we anticipate a growing portfolio (which an R&D lab developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act likely does), the middleware approach clearly wins on total cost of ownership.\n\nI'd approve this. It's well-aligned with my domain concerns: it improves resource-allocation efficiency (one build, many uses), protects scientific ambition (researchers aren