ng against Annex III requirements (especially point 3, which requires recording reference databases, input data for matches, verification steps) could push toward new interpretability methods that go beyond current post-hoc explanations.\n\nThe Annex III point 1(a) systems are biometric identification and categorization systems — so the logging requirements there (recording of use periods, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification) are quite specific and operational. The research question becomes: can you architect a system where these traces emerge naturally from the computation rather than being bolted on as external logging?\n\n**Intellectual Merit:**\nThe proposal is somewhat vague — \"investigate methods\" is not a sharp research hypothesis. It doesn't specify what particular architectural mechanisms are under consideration (attention-based provenance? gradient tracking? auxiliary metadata channels?). The phrase \"without degrading performance\" is a necessary constraint but the proposal doesn't articulate what trade-offs they expect or what theoretical bounds might apply.\n\nThe connection to XAI literature is real but somewhat generic. There's existing work on attention visualization, attribution methods, and interpretability that touches on related ground. What would make this genuinely meritorious is if it produces new architectural principles for self-documenting computation — not just applying existing logging frameworks to AI systems.\n\nThe proposal also has a slight issue: it conflates \"logging\" (which in the regulatory sense is an external audit trail of events) with \"internal state transparency.\" Article 12's requirements are about recording events over time — start/end times, database references, input data, verification steps. These are largely operational/metadata concerns that don't necessarily require architectural innovation in the model itself. The biometric system (Annex III point 1(a))