logging library doesn't contribute to the scientific understanding of AI, doesn't advance state-of-the-art methods, and isn't publishable as a research contribution. It might have some minor ecosystem benefit (other labs could adopt similar patterns), but that's engineering practice sharing, not scientific impact.\n\nHowever, let me consider if there are any angles where this touches on my domain:\n- The logging of \"events relevant for identifying situations that may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk\" — understanding what events matter for risk identification could touch on monitoring science, but the proposal as stated is about building the API, not researching what to log or how to interpret logs.\n- Post-market monitoring (Article 72) is mentioned, but again, the proposal is about the technical plumbing of recording, not about developing new methods for monitoring AI system performance.\n\nMy assessment: From a pure research vantage point focused on knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and broader scientific impact, this proposal has very little to offer. It's a legitimate engineering task that serves compliance needs, but it doesn't advance scientific understanding or generate novel knowledge. A researcher would not view this as a meaningful contribution to the research program. The \"research\" teams integrating into their pipelines are being asked to adopt an engineering constraint, which is fine for compliance but doesn't constitute or enable research advancement per se.\n\nThat said, I should be fair: there IS a small argument that standardized logging could enable better cross-project analysis of AI system failures, which could have some scientific value in understanding failure modes. But the proposal as written doesn't aim at that — it aims at compliance standardization and avoiding redundant engineering work. The framing is entirely about efficiency and regulatory compliance, not research.\n\nDecision: I would reject this from my v