onstrain scientific ambition — it's orthogonal to the science; it just ensures the engineering wrapper around the science meets regulatory minimums.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- CI/CD pipelines are standard in modern software development. Adding a compliance-checking stage is an extension, not a new build.\n- It reuses existing testing frameworks (unit tests, integration test patterns) applied to a new domain (logging verification).\n- The \"audit simulations\" can be structured as standard automated test cases — the same infrastructure used for regression testing, performance testing, etc.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Building the test suite itself requires upfront effort: someone needs to understand Article 12 requirements and translate them into testable assertions. This is a one-time cost but non-trivial.\n- The scope of \"audit simulations\" could balloon if not carefully scoped. But the proposal says it checks \"specific criteria of Article 12,\" which is bounded.\n- Risk: if the test suite is too rigid, it might create false confidence or require constant maintenance as systems evolve. But this is a manageable operational concern.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a high-value, low-marginal-cost proposal from my perspective. It leverages existing infrastructure (CI/CD), reduces long-term compliance costs by catching issues early, doesn't divert resources from scientific work, and has bounded scope. The resource-efficiency argument is strong: automate what would otherwise be manual review, use existing pipeline stages, get continuous coverage rather than one-time checks.\n\nI'd rate this highly. It's not a 100 because there's some upfront build cost and maintenance overhead, and the \"audit simulations\" could scope-creep. But it's clearly worth pursuing from an efficiency standpoint. I'll put it around 82-85.\n\nLet me settle on my decision: approve, promise score around 83.\n",
