        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer, focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is P2905: \"Unified Compliance Logging Framework\" — a centralized software library providing standardized event-logging capabilities for all high-risk AI prototypes, with mandatory API for timestamps, input data references, operator IDs, integrated into CI/CD pipeline.\n\nLet me assess this against Article 12 and Recital 71:\n\n**What the regulation requires:**\n- Art 12(1): High-risk AI systems SHALL technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\n- Art 12(2): Logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant to: (a) identifying risk situations/substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring (Art 72), (c) monitoring operation (Art 26(5)).\n- Art 12(3): For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically: recording period of each use, reference database checked, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification.\n- Recital 71: Technical documentation should be kept up to date; high-risk AI systems should technically allow automatic recording of events by means of logs over the lifetime of the system.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance perspective:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Art 12(1) — makes logging an \"intrinsic feature rather than an add-on,\" which is exactly what the regulation demands (\"shall technically allow for automatic recording\"). This is a strong alignment.\n- The mandatory API approach reduces the risk of individual researcher oversight, which is a compliance vulnerability. If each researcher builds their own logging, inconsistencies arise and gaps emerge that could fail an audit.\n- CI/CD integration means logging capabilities are verified before deployment — this supports continuous compliance rather than bolt-on.\n- Captures timestamps (aligns with