ompared to ad-hoc implementations. The CI/CD integration adds a verification layer. However, it has notable gaps:\n- Scope limitation to \"prototypes\" rather than full lifetime\n- Incomplete coverage of specific Art 12(3) data fields for Annex III systems\n- No retention/immutability provisions\n- No governance framework around the logs\n\nDespite these gaps, the direction is strongly aligned with compliance needs. A unified logging framework IS what regulators would expect to see as evidence that the institution takes Art 12 seriously. The proposal creates a single point of control for log generation, which dramatically reduces audit risk.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is a high-value investment. It addresses the most fundamental technical requirement of Article 12 in a way that scales across the organization. The gaps are addressable within the implementation scope without changing the fundamental architecture. I would approve this but note it needs refinement to cover full lifetime (not just prototypes) and complete Art 12(3) fields.\n\nPromise score: This is strong from a compliance perspective. It's not perfect — the \"prototypes\" scoping issue and missing specific fields concern me — but it hits the core requirement head-on in an institutional, scalable way. I'd put this around 80-85. The gaps are fixable without redesigning the approach. Let me settle on 82.\n",
