uto-population from CI/CD may not capture all required elements (e.g., risk-management system documentation, human oversight measures under Article 14).\n- The proposal doesn't mention versioning of the compliance dossier itself, audit trails for who approved what, or how to handle situations where auto-generated content needs human review before submission to authorities.\n- \"Replaces static PDFs with living documents\" — regulators will likely expect a specific snapshot at time of conformity assessment (Annex IV), and then updates thereafter. The pipeline needs to support both a \"frozen\" version for CE marking and ongoing updates.\n- The proposal doesn't address the interaction with Article 12's logging requirements specifically — it focuses on technical documentation (Recital 71/Article 11 territory) rather than the event logging of Article 12 itself, though these are related.\n- From a market reputation standpoint, having an automated system that \"just generates\" compliance docs could be seen as perfunctory if audited. Regulators want to see genuine understanding and human oversight.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal that addresses a real and critical compliance need (keeping technical documentation current throughout the lifetime). The CI/CD integration is smart and practical. However, from my vantage point, I have concerns about:\n1. Lack of governance layer (human validation/approval workflow)\n2. Risk of auto-generated content being incomplete or misleading\n3. No mention of how this interfaces with conformity assessment documentation requirements\n4. The \"replaces static PDFs\" framing is risky — regulators may still require specific formatted documents at certain points\n\nBut the core idea is sound and directly addresses a compliance obligation. It's not perfect but it's directionally correct and addresses a genuine regulatory requirement. I think this clears my bar for approval with moderate confidence. The promise score should 