g time-series databases with retention policies.\n- The proposal doesn't justify why existing infrastructure (which we likely already have for other purposes) cannot be adapted. It assumes a greenfield build.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This is where I have the strongest concern. The proposal builds a NEW dedicated system rather than leveraging what we already have. We almost certainly already have logging pipelines, data storage, and possibly even some immutable record systems (for research provenance, audit trails for other regulations).\n- Building a bespoke \"high-performance NoSQL store\" when solutions like Apache Kafka (for high-throughput write), combined with object storage (for immutability), or even simple append-only files on distributed filesystems are available, represents poor resource allocation.\n- The \"dedicated\" nature is concerning - it implies we're not reusing existing infrastructure.\n\n**Technical merit assessment:**\n- The core insight (logging shouldn't bottleneck inference) is valid and important.\n- But the solution (build a new dedicated distributed NoSQL ledger) seems disproportionate to the problem. You can achieve async logging with message queues, write to object storage with WORM (write-once-read-many) guarantees, all without building a \"dedicated\" system.\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) ensuring logs don't block inference (solved by async/buffered writes), and (2) ensuring immutability (solved by WORM storage or hash-chaining). Neither requires a \"new dedicated distributed NoSQL store.\"\n\n**My decision:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal is over-engineered for the actual requirement. It commits significant engineering resources to building bespoke infrastructure when existing tools and simpler architectures can achieve the same compliance goals with far less investment. The real insight (async logging to avoid inference bottleneck) is valuable but doesn't require a \"dedicated distr