ion efforts during audits.\" This is compelling — the current pain point for R&D labs is that compliance documentation pulls researchers away from their actual work. Automating this directly addresses a real resource drain.\n- However, \"automated knowledge graph\" is ambitious terminology. In practice, what's needed is structured metadata capture and templated document generation. Calling it a knowledge graph might imply over-engineering. The proposal should be judged on whether the core value (automated compliance documentation from existing experiment data) can be delivered without building an enterprise-grade knowledge graph platform.\n- The key question: does our lab already have experiment trackers that capture model versions, dataset provenance, and validation results? If yes, this is primarily a glue-and-presentation layer. If no, we'd need to build the tracking infrastructure first, which significantly changes the cost profile.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal explicitly says \"automated metadata harvesting from our experiment trackers.\" This suggests they're building on what already exists rather than creating new data capture systems. That's good resource allocation — leveraging existing investment.\n- The replacement of \"static documents with an automated knowledge graph\" is the risky part. Static documents are cheap to maintain (just edit a PDF/Word doc). A knowledge graph requires ongoing engineering maintenance, versioning of the graph schema, and potentially new tooling that the team must learn.\n- However, if the lab has multiple AI systems in various stages of development, the static document approach doesn't scale. Each system needs its own documentation kept current. An automated pipeline scales better with the number of systems.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and addresses a real pain point that directly impacts research productivity (the audit-time documentation burden). Building on exi