        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2252\",\n      \"rationale\": \"As Compliance Officer, I view 'selective event triggering' as a material risk to the Article 12(1) obligation of recording logs over the full lifetime of the system; if the selection logic misses an event that later proves relevant for traceability under Art. 12(2)(a) or post-market monitoring under Art. 72, we face a compliance gap with no cure short of retroactive reconstruction that the regulation does not permit. Additionally, framing this as a 'scientific research initiative' investigating formats and triggers rather than committing to an implementable logging architecture prioritises efficiency optimisation over the robustness and auditability my function must guarantee before any high-risk system reaches market.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 32\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2252) from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P2252:** \"Low-Overhead Traceability Research Initiative\" — A scientific project focused on developing high-fidelity logging methods that don't degrade performance or inflate storage costs. Researchers will investigate compressed logging formats and selective event triggering to satisfy Article 12 without straining existing server infrastructure. It aligns regulatory necessity with scientific ambition by optimizing efficiency of R&D hardware.\n\nNow, let me assess this from my Compliance Officer perspective:\n\n**Key considerations:**\n\n1. **Regulatory compliance (my primary concern):**\n   - Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\"\n   - Article 12(2) specifies what logging must capture: situations presenting risk, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring.\n   - Article 12(3) sets minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n   \n   The proposal mentions \"selective event triggering\" — this is a RED FLAG from a compliance standpoint. Article 12 says logs must be recorded \"over the lifetime of the system.\" \"Selective event triggering\" implies that not all events will be logged, only some selected ones. This could conflict with the requirement to record \"events relevant for\" the purposes in Art. 12(2)(a)-(c). The regulation doesn't say \"selective\" — it says logging capabilities shall enable recording of events relevant for specific purposes. There's a tension here: you need to capture ALL events that are \"relevant,\" but how do you know ex-ante which events will be relevant? \"Selective triggering\" risks missing events that later prove to be relevant for traceability, risk identification, or post-market monitoring.\n\n   However, one could argue that \"selective event triggering\" is a legitimate engineering approach if the selection criteria are well-defined and map to the Article 12(2) categories. The regulation says logging shall \"enable\" recording of events relevant for those purposes — it's about capability, not necessarily capturing every single byte of activity. But \"selective\" still introduces risk of under-logging.\n\n   Compressed logging formats: This is more neutral. As long as the compressed format preserves all required information and can be decompressed for audit/inspection, compression itself isn't problematic. But there's a compliance risk if compression leads to lossy reduction of data that regulators or notified bodies need for traceability. The regulation requires \"comprehensible information\" (Recital 71) — lossy compression could undermine this.\n\n2. **Institutional survival:**\n   - This is framed as a \"scientific project\" and \"research initiative.\" As a compliance officer, I'm concerned about the distinction between research/exploration and actual implementation. The proposal talks about \"investigating\" methods — this sounds like R&D exploration rather than a concrete compliance implementation plan. \n   - However, it does aim to \"satisfy Article 12\" — so there's an implementation orientation.\n   - The framing as optimizing \"our R&D hardware\" is concerning from my vantage point. It positions the logging infrastructure as something to be optimized for performance/cost rather than prioritizing regulatory robustness. A compliance officer would want logging that is thorough, auditable, and meets the standard — not one that's been \"optimized\" in ways that might create gaps.\n\n3. **Market reputation:**\n   - Positioning this as aligning \"regulatory necessity with scientific ambition\" could be good marketing, but it also subtly reframes compliance as something to be worked around (finding lower-overhead solutions) rather than something to be met robustly.\n   - If regulators or market participants see that the institution is trying to minimize its logging footprint through compression and selective triggering, that could raise questions about whether they're taking traceability seriously enough.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal has a legitimate underlying concern (logging infrastructure costs and performance), but from my compliance vantage point:\n- \"Selective event triggering\" introduces meaningful risk of non-compliance with Art. 12(1)-(3). The regulation requires recording over the full lifetime and capturing events relevant for specific purposes. Selective triggering could miss edge cases that are exactly what traceability is meant to capture (e.g., a rare failure mode).\n- \"Compressed logging\" introduces risk if it's lossy or if decompression isn't guaranteed to be available when regulators request records.\n- The framing as a \"research initiative\" rather than an implementation commitment is concerning — we need compliance NOW for high-risk systems, not research into how to do it more cheaply later.\n- However, the underlying goal of efficient logging infrastructure is reasonable and the proposal does aim to satisfy Article 12.\n\nI think this is borderline. The core idea (efficient logging) is fine, but the specific approaches mentioned (selective triggering, compression) introduce compliance risks that I as a Compliance Officer would flag. The \"research initiative\" framing also makes it seem like we're exploring rather than committing to compliance. \n\nBut wait — let me reconsider. The proposal says \"to satisfy Article 12.\" If done correctly, compressed logging is fine (as long as data is recoverable and complete). Selective event triggering could be fine if the selection criteria are mapped to Art. 12(2)(a)-(c) purposes. The concern is whether a \"research initiative\" is the right vehicle — we need actual compliance implementation, not just research into how to do it.\n\nI'll lean toward reject because:\n1. The framing as a \"scientific project/research initiative\" rather than an implementation plan doesn't match the urgency of regulatory compliance for high-risk systems.\n2. \"Selective event triggering\" is a compliance risk — I cannot approve an approach that might result in incomplete logging.\n3. The emphasis on not degrading performance and not inflating costs puts operational efficiency ahead of regulatory robustness, which is the wrong priority from my vantage point.\n\nActually, let me reconsider again. Is this really reject-worthy? The proposal does say \"to satisfy Article 12.\" If the research ultimately produces a compliant solution (compressed but complete logs, selective triggering that captures all Art. 12(2)-relevant events), then it's serving compliance. The issue is whether I can approve a *research* approach when we need *implementation*. \n\nAs a Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival: if our high-risk systems go to market without robust logging in place because we're still \"investigating\" compressed formats and selective triggering, that's a regulatory violation. We need logging NOW. This proposal sounds like it would delay actual compliance implementation while researchers figure out optimal methods.\n\nI'll reject it. The promise score should reflect that the underlying concern is valid but the approach as framed introduces compliance risks and delays. I'd give it maybe 30-40 — the efficiency angle has some merit, but from my vantage point, it's the wrong priority framing and introduces specific compliance risks (selective triggering especially).\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 35.\n",
