ther an idea might someday work. The proposal says \"will investigate novel methods\" — this is R&D exploration, not implementation.\n\n2. **Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints**: This is explicitly pushing \"the knowledge frontier of AI observability.\" It's a research initiative with publication goals at top-tier venues. This is scientific ambition that goes well beyond what the regulation requires. The regulation (Art. 12) says systems must \"technically allow for automatic recording of events (logs)\" and logging capabilities shall enable recording of specific events. It doesn't require novel algorithmic interpretability research. Standard structured logging with appropriate metadata already satisfies Art. 12.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: The lab likely already has logging infrastructure, monitoring systems, etc. This proposal wants to build something new and novel rather than leveraging what exists. The \"bridge the gap between binary logs and semantic understanding\" framing suggests they see current logs as inadequate, but standard practice (structured JSON logs, event schemas, dashboards) already provides comprehensible information without needing novel research.\n\n4. **The \"new research initiative\" framing is a red flag from my perspective**: It's not an implementation plan. It's a research exploration. My job is to commit resources to things that will deliver compliance and operational capability. A frontier research project with publication goals is a different kind of commitment — it has uncertain timelines, uncertain outcomes, and diverts people who could be implementing actual logging capabilities.\n\n5. **Publication goals**: \"We will publish these findings in top-tier AI safety venues.\" This signals the primary driver is scientific prestige/knowledge contribution, not operational compliance. While publication can have externalities (building institutional reputation), from a pure resource-allocation and implementation standpoint, this adds cost without adding compliance value.\n\n6. **Scope creep**: \"Event aggregation and anomaly detection within high-dimensional log data to identify substantial modifications automatically\" — this is essentially building an AI/ML system to monitor the AI system's logs for drift or modification. That's a significant engineering effort. The regulation requires that logging *facilitate* post-market monitoring and *enable identifying* situations of risk/substantial modification — it doesn't require that the identification be fully automated through novel ML methods. Human-in-the-loop review with good structured logs can satisfy this requirement.\n\n7. **The proposal conflates \"comprehensible\" (Recital 71) with \"algorithmically interpretable\"**: Recital 71 says records should be drawn in a clear and comprehensive form, containing information necessary to assess compliance. This is about documentation quality and log structure — achievable with standard engineering practices — not about novel interpretability algorithms.\n\nMy assessment: From my vantage point, this proposal is too ambitious relative to what the regulation actually requires, it's framed as open-ended research rather than implementation, it has publication-driven goals that don't serve operational compliance directly, and it risks diverting scarce resources from practical logging infrastructure buildout toward frontier research with uncertain timelines and outcomes. The lab can satisfy Art. 12 with structured logging, appropriate metadata schemas, and standard monitoring tooling — none of which requires a \"new research initiative\" pushing the knowledge frontier.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge that there IS a kernel of genuine value: making logs interpretable does serve the traceability purpose. But the way it's framed (novel methods, top-tier venues, \"pushes the knowledge frontier\") makes this a scientific ambition project rather than an efficient implementation response. The regulation doesn't demand SOTA interpretability research; it demands that logs be available and useful for monitoring.\n\nDecision: Reject. This is a well-meaning but over-scoped proposal from my vantage point. It commits scarce resources to exploratory frontier research when practical, well-understood engineering solutions can satisfy the regulatory requirements at a fraction of the cost and risk. The publication goals signal misalignment with implementation priorities.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 30-35. There's genuine intellectual value in better log interpretability, and some of the ideas (event aggregation for anomaly detection) could have practical applications if scoped down to engineering rather than research. But as proposed — a new frontier research initiative with publication goals — it doesn't clear my bar from a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint. Maybe 32.\n",
