sal says \"we will investigate\" — this is exploratory research, not implementation. My role is to ensure we meet the obligation, not to explore novel logging paradigms that may or may not materialize.\n\n2. **Resource allocation concern.** Approving this commits scarce implementation resources to a research project rather than to actually building compliant logging infrastructure. The lab cannot pursue everything. If I approve this, it means resources are tied up in \"investigating latent space monitoring and concept drift detection\" instead of implementing the straightforward requirements (start/end timestamps, reference databases, input data matching, personnel identification).\n\n3. **However, there's a legitimate compliance angle.** Article 12(2)(a) requires logging events relevant to identifying situations where the system presents a risk or undergoes substantial modification. \"Semantic\" or behavioral-shift detection could be a more effective way to identify substantial modifications than raw event logs alone. If we only log raw data, detecting when model logic has substantially shifted might be extremely difficult. So there IS a compliance-relevant question here about what granularity and type of logging is sufficient.\n\n4. **Risk assessment.** The proposal as stated is a research project, not an implementation plan. It says \"we will investigate\" — this suggests uncertainty about whether the approach will work. From my vantage point, I need committed solutions, not investigations. If we can't demonstrate compliant logging to a regulator because we were still \"investigating concept drift detection,\" that's a compliance failure.\n\n5. **Market reputation angle.** There is some value in demonstrating innovative approaches to AI governance — it could enhance our market position. But this is secondary to actually meeting the obligation. A regulator won't care about our novel semantic logging approach if we can't show we're logging what Article 12 requires.