astructure. This is excellent governance.\n- The automation reduces the risk of selective or incomplete logging, which would be a serious compliance failure.\n\nThe main concern from my perspective: does this fully cover all of Article 12's requirements? The proposal mentions timestamps and input data but doesn't explicitly enumerate the Annex III 1(a) specific requirements (reference database, identification of natural persons). However, as a framework/architecture, it could be designed to include these. The proposal is at the level of a system design concept rather than a detailed specification, so some ambiguity is expected.\n\nAlso, \"over the lifetime of the system\" — does this middleware persist in production? If it's only in \"training and deployment pipelines,\" that might not cover the full operational lifetime. But \"deployment pipelines\" could be interpreted to include ongoing production monitoring.\n\nOverall: This is a strong proposal from my compliance perspective. It directly addresses a hard legal requirement (Art 12), creates an enforcement mechanism (required dependency), automates compliance (reducing human error), and is scalable across the lab's portfolio of high-risk systems. The gaps are addressable in implementation. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is very relevant to my function. It addresses a core regulatory obligation with a practical engineering solution that creates institutional safeguards. However, it doesn't cover every nuance (the specific Annex III 1(a) sub-requirements aren't explicitly listed, and \"lifetime\" coverage could be clearer). I'd put this at around 78-82. It's clearly something I would back strongly but there are implementation details that need to nail down for full compliance assurance.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
