ld be technically fragile or slow to implement. But from a compliance standpoint, the aspiration is correct even if execution is complex.\n- \"Internal API wrappers that intercept system calls\" — this is an implementation detail that may have security implications (intercepting calls can introduce attack surfaces). However, the compliance requirement for automatic logging is non-negotiable, so this risk needs to be managed, not avoided by rejecting the proposal.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention Article 12(2)'s requirements about identifying risk situations or facilitating post-market monitoring (Article 72) or monitoring operation (Article 26(5)). It focuses more on Article 12(3) specifics. However, a \"unified framework\" that captures events broadly would presumably enable those as well — it's just not spelled out in the proposal text.\n- No mention of retention periods or how logs will be made available to authorities upon request (Article 18 covers technical documentation availability). But this is arguably a scope extension beyond what Article 12 strictly requires.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- A non-compliant high-risk system can lead to fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover. Having a single, auditable logging infrastructure across all projects dramatically reduces the risk that one project slips through with inadequate logging. This is existential protection.\n- Market reputation: if we're known as an institution with robust, standardized compliance infrastructure, it strengthens our position in tender processes and partnerships.\n\n**From regulatory compliance perspective:**\n- Article 12 is a \"shall\" requirement — mandatory. The proposal directly addresses the core obligation.\n- The immutable database ensures logs can't be tampered with, supporting integrity of records during any regulatory inspection or incident investigation.\n- Standardization means consistent interpretation of what constitutes an \"event\" across p