uire building new infrastructure from scratch.\n- The \"eliminates manual record-keeping\" claim is somewhat overstated — you still need to ensure the tracker captures everything needed, handle edge cases, version the templates, etc. But it's a significant reduction in manual work.\n- However, it only addresses one part of the compliance picture (training documentation) and not the core operational logging requirement of Article 12(1)-(3).\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe proposal is technically sound for what it covers — automating training documentation generation. It's a practical, low-effort addition that improves prototyping speed. But it has a significant scope limitation: it addresses Recital 71's documentation requirement but largely misses Article 12(1)-(3)'s core mandate about runtime automatic logging of events. For Annex III 1(a) systems especially, the operational logging (start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, verifier identification) is what the article primarily demands, and this proposal doesn't address that.\n\nFrom my vantage point on technical viability and prototyping speed:\n- What it does is technically viable and achievable quickly ✓\n- It integrates well with existing tooling (experiment trackers are already part of most ML pipelines) ✓\n- It reduces manual overhead during prototyping cycles ✓\n- But it's incomplete relative to the full Article 12 requirement — it handles the \"documentation\" side but not the \"automatic recording of events\" side ✗\n- The claim that it \"eliminates manual record-keeping for training processes\" is reasonable but the scope is narrow\n\nI think this is a viable, useful engineering task that would speed up compliance work and reduce friction in prototyping. It's a good first step but not sufficient on its own. From my perspective, I'd approve it because:\n1. It's technically straightforward to implement\n2. It integrates with tools we likely already use\n3. It reduces a real bottleneck (manual docu